C["articles"]['nightmarket'] = ('da-lat-night-market-guide', 'Da Lat Night Market Guide: What to Eat and Buy | Van Phu', 'Da Lat Night Market: What to Eat, What to Buy and How to Get There', 'A practical guide to the Da Lat night market: when to go, the best street food to try, what to buy, how to shop and bargain politely, and where to park your bike.', "Every evening the streets around Da Lat Market fill with food stalls, warm clothes and the smell of charcoal grills. Here's how to enjoy the night market like a local — what to eat, what to buy and how to get there by bike.", 'vision-white', '\n<h2>Where and when</h2>\n<p>The Da Lat night market (chợ đêm Đà Lạt) grows every evening around the central Da Lat Market (Chợ Đà Lạt), just above Xuan Huong Lake. Stalls spread along the street in front of the market and up and down the wide steps, so it\'s easy to walk around in an hour or two. Vendors start setting up in the late afternoon; the market is busiest after dark, roughly from 6 to 10 pm. Weekends and Vietnamese holidays are very crowded — on weekdays it\'s calmer and easier to find a seat.</p>\n<p>Da Lat evenings are cool, often around 15 °C or less in the dry season, so bring a jacket. That\'s also part of the fun: hot snacks and warm drinks taste much better in the mountain air.</p>\n<h2>What to eat</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Bánh tráng nướng</b> — rice paper grilled over charcoal with egg, spring onion and sauces, often called "Da Lat pizza". The classic night-market snack.</li>\n<li><b>Hot soy milk (sữa đậu nành)</b> — sold from big pots, usually with small cakes and pastries on the side. Perfect on a cold evening.</li>\n<li><b>Grilled corn and sweet potatoes</b> — simple, cheap and warming; you\'ll smell the grills before you see them.</li>\n<li><b>Grilled skewers</b> — meat, seafood, tofu and vegetables cooked to order.</li>\n<li><b>Bánh căn</b> — small rice-flour cakes made in clay moulds, dipped in fish sauce with pickles.</li>\n<li><b>Avocado ice cream (kem bơ)</b> — a Da Lat favourite: creamy avocado with coconut ice cream, found around the market area.</li>\n<li><b>Chè</b> — sweet soups with beans, jelly and coconut milk, served hot or cold.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most stalls have small plastic stools. Point, smile and ask the price before you order — many stalls don\'t post prices. Vegetarians can look for grilled corn, sweet potato, tofu skewers and bánh tráng nướng without meat (ask "không thịt").</p>\n<h2>What to buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Warm clothes</b> — knitted hats, scarves, gloves and jackets, including many second-hand stalls. Useful if you underpacked for the cool mountain nights.</li>\n<li><b>Dried fruit and candied fruit (mứt)</b> — dried persimmons, strawberries, mulberries and more; you can usually taste before buying.</li>\n<li><b>Artichoke tea (trà atisô)</b> and local coffee — popular gifts from Da Lat.</li>\n<li><b>Fresh produce</b> — strawberries, avocados and other highland fruit, depending on the season.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Shopping tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Bring small bills in cash; card payments are rare at stalls.</li>\n<li>For clothes and souvenirs, polite bargaining is normal; for food, prices are usually fixed.</li>\n<li>Keep your phone and wallet in a front pocket — crowds can be dense on the steps.</li>\n<li>Take your rubbish with you or use the bins at the stalls.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Getting there by bike and parking</h2>\n<p>The market is in the very center, so it\'s an easy short ride from most hotels. In the evening the streets right around the market are packed with people, so don\'t try to ride through the crowd: park a little away from the busiest part and walk the last few minutes. Use a guarded parking lot (gửi xe), keep the parking ticket and remember where you left the bike. If you plan to drink, leave the bike at the hotel and walk or take a Grab — no alcohol when riding.</p>\n<p>After the market, a short ride or walk around Xuan Huong Lake at night is a nice way to end the evening. For daytime ideas see our page <a href="{ROUTES}">What to See in Da Lat by Motorbike: 5 Routes</a>.</p>\n<h2>Explore Da Lat on your own bike</h2>\n<p>A scooter makes evenings easy: dinner at the night market, a lake view café afterwards, and back to your hotel whenever you like. At Van Phu every bike — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter — costs 150,000 VND per day, with no cash deposit (you leave your passport or ID card), a helmet and a phone holder included. Message us on WhatsApp and we\'ll share our favourite night-market snacks too.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-procedure'] = ('how-to-rent-a-motorbike-in-da-lat', 'How to Rent a Motorbike in Da Lat: Step by Step | Van Phu', 'How to Rent a Motorbike in Da Lat: A Simple Step-by-Step Guide', 'How to rent a motorbike in Da Lat step by step: choose a bike, message us, pick up and test ride, film the bike together, ride safely and return before 21:00.', "Never rented a scooter in Vietnam before? Here's exactly how to rent a motorbike in Da Lat at Van Phu — from the first message to handing back the keys.", 'fleet-gas', '\n<h2>Step 1: Choose your bike</h2>\n<p>All our bikes cost the same — 150,000 VND per day — so choose by how you\'ll ride:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc</b> — light and easy, a good automatic for the city and short trips.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc</b> — more power for hills, mountain passes, two riders and long day trips.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter</b> — quiet and simple for riding around the city; maximum 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg. Not for long, steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or Lang Biang — take the Air Blade for those. Details on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter page</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Step 2: Send us a message</h2>\n<p>Write to us on WhatsApp (+84 944 081 101 or +84 834 622 149), Zalo (0944 081 101), Telegram (@vanphudalat) or WeChat (vanphudalat). To book quickly, send:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>your pick-up date and approximate time;</li>\n<li>how many days you need the bike;</li>\n<li>which bike you\'d like, and whether you\'ll ride alone or with a passenger.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We reply with confirmation and keep the bike for you.</p>\n<h2>Step 3: Come to the shop</h2>\n<p>Our shop is at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học in central Da Lat and is marked on Google Maps. We open every day from 4:30 am, so even a sunrise trip is possible — though it\'s often easiest to pick up the bike the afternoon before.</p>\n<h2>Step 4: Test ride and video</h2>\n<p>Before you leave, take a short test ride to get the feel of the bike — brakes, throttle, mirrors. Then we film a short video of the bike\'s condition together: body, lights, tyres and mirrors. At return we compare with this video, so it protects you as much as us. If you\'re new to scooters, ask us to show you the basics; we\'ll take the time.</p>\n<h2>Step 5: Leave your document and pay</h2>\n<p>There\'s no cash deposit. You leave your passport (or ID card for Vietnamese guests) at the shop and pay the rental upfront: 150,000 VND per day, a fixed price. You get a helmet and a phone holder on the bike; gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel to get you to the nearest petrol station.</p>\n<h2>Step 6: Ride safely</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Go slowly for the first few minutes until you\'re comfortable.</li>\n<li>Wear your helmet, no alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>Da Lat is hilly: brake gently on descents, especially in the rain.</li>\n<li>Park in guarded parking lots and lock the bike.</li>\n<li>For an electric scooter, come back to the shop to swap the battery (20,000 VND) before it runs low.</li>\n<li>Any problem on the road — message or call us.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Step 7: Return the bike</h2>\n<p>A rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00 the same day, so return the bike before 21:00 on your last day. We check the bike together against the video and hand your passport or ID card straight back. Want to keep the bike longer? Just message us before 21:00.</p>\n<h2>Ready to rent?</h2>\n<p>That\'s really all there is to it: a message, a quick test ride, and you\'re on the road. All bikes, conditions and contacts are on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. Send us your dates and we\'ll have a bike ready for you.</p>\n')
