C["articles"]['lt-ev-foreigners'] = ('electric-scooter-rental-da-lat-foreigners', 'Electric Scooter Rental in Da Lat for Foreigners | Van Phu', 'Electric Scooter Rental in Da Lat for Foreigners: Quiet, Easy and Simple', 'Electric scooter rental in Da Lat for foreigners: how it works, who it suits, the battery and swap, the weight limit for two, where to ride and how to book in English.', "Visiting Da Lat and looking for an easy way to get around? An electric scooter is quiet, simple to ride and perfect for the city. Here's how electric scooter rental in Da Lat works for foreign travellers at Van Phu.", 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '\n<h2>Why foreign travellers like electric scooters</h2>\n<p>Many visitors have never ridden a motorbike with gears or a clutch — and they don\'t need to. An electric scooter has no gears and no engine noise: you twist the throttle, it moves smoothly, you squeeze the brakes, it stops. There\'s no petrol to buy, no hot exhaust pipe to burn your leg on, and it\'s very light to push and park. For short rides between your hotel, cafés, the market and Xuan Huong Lake, it\'s one of the most relaxed ways to see Da Lat.</p>\n<h2>Who it suits — and who should choose a gas scooter</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Good fit:</b> riding around the city, café hopping, short trips to nearby sights, travellers who want something quiet and easy.</li>\n<li><b>Two riders:</b> maximum 2 people with a combined weight under 140 kg.</li>\n<li><b>Not a good fit:</b> long, steep climbs such as Prenn Pass, Mimosa Pass or the road up Lang Biang, and long day trips. For those, take a Honda Air Blade 125cc — same price.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not sure which to choose? Tell us your plans and we\'ll suggest the right bike honestly.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Battery and battery swap</h2>\n<p>One battery lasts about 25–30 km, and each scooter comes with 2 batteries — about 50–60 km in total. You start with full batteries. Need more? Bring the scooter to our shop and we swap in a full battery for 20,000 VND. Hills and a passenger use more power, so head back when about a third is left, and never run the battery completely flat.</p>\n<h2>How the rental works</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Message us in English on WhatsApp (+84 944 081 101 or +84 834 622 149), Telegram (@vanphudalat), WeChat (vanphudalat) or Zalo (0944 081 101) with your dates.</li>\n<li>Come to our shop at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học in central Da Lat — we open every day from 4:30 am.</li>\n<li>Take a short test ride and we film the scooter\'s condition together.</li>\n<li>No cash deposit: you leave your passport while you have the scooter and pay 150,000 VND per day upfront.</li>\n<li>You get a helmet and a phone holder on the scooter for Google Maps.</li>\n<li>Return before 21:00 on your last day — a rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Please read our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a> before pick-up — they\'re short and clear.</p>\n<h2>Tips for riding in Da Lat</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Take it slowly for the first few minutes until you feel comfortable.</li>\n<li>Da Lat is hilly: brake gently going downhill, especially after rain.</li>\n<li>Wear your helmet, no alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>Park in guarded parking lots (gửi xe) and keep your parking ticket.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu}\n<h2>Book your electric scooter</h2>\n<p>Electric scooter rental in Da Lat for foreigners is simple: one message, a quick test ride and you\'re on your way. See photos and details on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter page</a>, or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. Send us your dates and we\'ll keep a scooter ready for you.</p>\n')
C["dates"]["lt-ev-foreigners"] = "2026-10-16"

C["articles"]['langbiang'] = ('lang-biang-mountain-da-lat', 'Lang Biang Mountain Guide: How to Get There by Motorbike | Van Phu', 'Lang Biang Mountain by Motorbike: How to Get There, Radar Peak and Tips', 'A practical guide to Lang Biang Mountain near Da Lat: how to ride there by motorbike, jeep or hike to Radar Peak, the summit trail, best time to go, what to wear and safety tips.', "Lang Biang is the mountain you see on the northern horizon from Da Lat — and one of the easiest big views to reach by bike. Here's how to get there, what to do at the top and how to plan a relaxed half day.", 'group-rental-air-blade-van-phu-morning', '\n<h2>Lang Biang in a nutshell</h2>\n<p>Lang Biang (also written Langbiang) is a mountain in Lac Duong district, about 12 km north of central Da Lat. Its highest peak rises to around 2,100 m, while the city sits at about 1,500 m, so on a clear day you look down over pine forests, farm valleys, greenhouses, Dankia Lake and Da Lat itself. The area around the mountain is part of the Langbiang Biosphere Reserve recognised by UNESCO.</p>\n<p>The name comes from a well-known local legend about K\'Lang, a young man of the Lat people, and H\'Biang, a girl from a neighbouring group, whose families would not let them marry. The two peaks of the mountain are said to be their resting place — you\'ll see statues and signs about the story at the foot of the mountain.</p>\n<h2>Getting there by motorbike</h2>\n<p>From the center, follow the road north towards Lac Duong. The ride takes about 25–35 minutes, mostly on an easy paved road through villages, flower farms and greenhouses, with a few gentle climbs. Google Maps leads you straight to the Lang Biang tourist area at the foot of the mountain, where there is a parking lot for motorbikes (gửi xe — keep your ticket).</p>\n<p>A gas scooter is the right choice for this trip — the Honda Air Blade 125cc is the most comfortable on the climbs, especially with a passenger. Electric scooters are not suitable for the road towards Lang Biang; keep them for rides around the city.</p>\n<h2>Radar Peak: jeep or on foot</h2>\n<p>The most popular viewpoint is Radar Peak (around 1,900 m), reached by a steep paved road from the tourist area. Private motorbikes usually park at the foot, and you go up either by the tourist area\'s jeeps or on foot:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Jeep</b> — the quick and easy option, a short, steep ride to the viewpoint and back. Tickets are sold at the tourist area; check the current price and timetable there.</li>\n<li><b>On foot</b> — walking up the road is a steady climb of roughly 1.5–2 hours for most people. Bring water and start early, before the midday sun.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At the top you\'ll find viewing platforms, cafés and small stalls. On clear mornings the view over the valleys and Dankia Lake is wide and beautiful; in the rainy season clouds can roll in quickly, so be ready to wait a little.</p>\n{PHOTO:van-phu-guest-air-blade-thumbs-up}\n<h2>Hiking to the summit</h2>\n<p>Fit hikers can follow forest trails to the higher peaks. The trail is steep, muddy after rain and not always clearly marked, so it\'s best to go with a local guide, start early in the morning and allow most of the day. Don\'t attempt it alone or in heavy rain.</p>\n<h2>When to go</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Dry season</b> (roughly December to April) gives the clearest views.</li>\n<li><b>Morning</b> is usually better than afternoon, when clouds and showers are more common in the rainy months.</li>\n<li>Weekends and Vietnamese holidays are busy; weekdays are calmer.</li>\n<li>The viewpoint is not a sunrise spot — for dawn clouds see our <a href="{ART:cloudhunt}">cloud hunting guide</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to bring and safety</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A warm jacket — it\'s windy and noticeably cooler at the top.</li>\n<li>A raincoat under the seat, sunscreen, water and some cash for tickets and drinks.</li>\n<li>Comfortable shoes if you plan to walk.</li>\n<li>Ride slowly through villages, watch for farm trucks and brake gently on downhill stretches, especially when wet.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Make it a half-day loop</h2>\n<p>Leave Da Lat around 7–8 am, spend a couple of hours at Lang Biang, then stop at Dankia Lake or the flower farms on the way back and be in town for lunch. More ideas are on our page <a href="{ROUTES}">What to See in Da Lat by Motorbike</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent a bike for Lang Biang</h2>\n<p>At Van Phu every bike — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter — costs 150,000 VND per day, with no cash deposit (you leave your passport or ID card), a helmet and a phone holder for Google Maps included. For Lang Biang we recommend the Air Blade. Message us on WhatsApp and we\'ll tell you how the weather looked at the mountain that week.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-documents'] = ('documents-to-rent-a-motorbike-da-lat', 'What Do You Need to Rent a Motorbike in Da Lat? Documents | Van Phu', 'What Do You Need to Rent a Motorbike in Da Lat? Documents, Payment and What to Bring', 'What do you need to rent a motorbike in Da Lat? Only your passport (or CCCD for Vietnamese guests) — no cash deposit. Payment, what to bring and common questions answered.', "Before you pick up a scooter, it's natural to wonder what you need to rent a motorbike in Da Lat. At Van Phu the answer is short: one document, the rental fee and your phone. Here are the details.", 'van-phu-shop-busy-morning-vision-air-blade', '\n<h2>The short answer</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Foreign guests:</b> your original passport.</li>\n<li><b>Vietnamese guests:</b> your CCCD (citizen ID card).</li>\n<li>The rental fee: 150,000 VND per day for every bike, paid upfront for the days you need.</li>\n<li>A phone with WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat so we can stay in touch.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That\'s all you need to rent a motorbike in Da Lat with us. There\'s no cash deposit and no other paperwork. The document stays safely at the shop while you have the bike and comes back to you as soon as you return it — our article on <a href="{ART:lt-nodeposit}">renting with no cash deposit</a> explains why we work this way.</p>\n<h2>Why the original, not a photo?</h2>\n<p>We keep the document itself instead of cash, so a photo or photocopy can\'t replace it. It\'s still a good idea to keep a photo of your passport on your phone for your own records.</p>\n<h2>Common questions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>My hotel kept my passport.</b> Many hotels only scan it at check-in and give it back. If yours is holding it, ask for it back before you come to pick up the bike — it\'s easiest to check in first and rent afterwards.</li>\n<li><b>I need my passport during the rental</b> (for a bus ticket, a flight or a new hotel). Plan to return the bike first, or message us and we\'ll find a simple solution together.</li>\n<li><b>We are a group.</b> Tell us in your message how many bikes you need and who will ride, and we\'ll confirm what to bring before you come.</li>\n<li><b>Do I need to book?</b> Not always, but a short message with your date and bike keeps one ready for you, especially at weekends and on holidays.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-honda-vision-van-phu-shop}\n<h2>Paying for the rental</h2>\n<p>The price is fixed: 150,000 VND per day for the Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter, with no hidden fees. You pay at pick-up for the days you plan to ride. A rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00. If you have questions about how to pay, just ask in your booking message. Everything included in the price is on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n<h2>What you get at pick-up</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A short test ride, and a video of the bike\'s condition that we film together — it protects you as well as us.</li>\n<li>A helmet and a phone holder for Google Maps.</li>\n<li>Gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel; electric scooters come with full batteries.</li>\n<li>Free delivery to hotels in central Da Lat for rentals of 2 days or more.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The whole process takes only a few minutes — see <a href="{ART:lt-procedure}">how to rent a motorbike in Da Lat step by step</a>.</p>\n<h2>Before you ride</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; take it slowly for the first few minutes.</li>\n<li>Wear your helmet, no alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>Return the bike before 21:00 on your last day and your document is handed back on the spot.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Ready to rent?</h2>\n<p>Now you know what you need to rent a motorbike in Da Lat: your passport or CCCD, the daily fee and a phone. Message us on WhatsApp (+84 944 081 101 or +84 834 622 149) or Zalo (0944 081 101) with your dates, or visit our shop at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học — we open every day from 4:30 am. Bikes and prices are on the <a href="{HOME}">rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-experience'] = ('electric-scooter-da-lat-tips', 'Renting an Electric Scooter in Da Lat: Tips From Experience | Van Phu', 'Renting an Electric Scooter in Da Lat: Practical Tips From Experience', 'Tips for renting an electric scooter in Da Lat: plan your day around the battery, handle the hills, park safely, dress for the weather and avoid common first-day mistakes.', "Every day we hand out electric scooters and hear how our guests' rides went. These are the practical tips we share most often — what to know before you rent an electric scooter in Da Lat and how to get the most out of it.", 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>1. Pick the right bike for your plans</h2>\n<p>An electric scooter is perfect for the city: Xuan Huong Lake, the market, cafés, the flower park, Da Lat Railway Station and other central sights. It\'s quiet, light and has no gears. It is not made for long, steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road to Lang Biang, or for long day trips — for those days take a Honda Air Blade 125cc at the same price. Many guests mix: electric for city days, gas for the day they ride out of town.</p>\n<h2>2. Plan your day around the battery</h2>\n<p>One battery lasts about 25–30 km, and each scooter comes with 2 batteries — about 50–60 km in total. That\'s plenty for a full day in the city if you plan a little:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Do the hilliest part of your plan first, while the battery is full.</li>\n<li>Head back when about a third is left — hills and a passenger use more power.</li>\n<li>Need more? Ride to our shop and we swap in a full battery for 20,000 VND. Our shop is central, so it\'s easy to swap at lunchtime.</li>\n<li>Don\'t use other chargers and don\'t remove the battery yourself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>3. Respect the hills</h2>\n<p>Da Lat is built on hills, even in the center. Going up, keep a steady throttle; if a slope is very steep and long, it\'s fine to choose another road. Going down, brake gently with both brakes and slow down before bends. Two riders must weigh under 140 kg combined — this keeps the scooter safe and comfortable on slopes.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>4. Dress for Da Lat weather</h2>\n<p>Mornings and evenings are cool, and you feel the wind more on a scooter. Bring a jacket, keep a raincoat under the seat, and in the rainy season ride slowly: roads get slippery, and you should never ride through deep water.</p>\n<h2>5. Parking and security</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use guarded parking lots (gửi xe) at markets, cafés and sights, and keep your ticket.</li>\n<li>Always lock the scooter and take the key with you.</li>\n<li>Don\'t leave helmets or bags on the scooter in busy areas.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>6. Small habits that make a big difference</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Take a short test ride at the shop and film the scooter\'s condition with us before you leave.</li>\n<li>Use the phone holder for Google Maps instead of holding your phone.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; give way at roundabouts and ride calmly in traffic.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>Return before 21:00 — a rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00. Running late? Message us first.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>7. Read the rules once</h2>\n<p>Our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a> fit on one page: price, rental day, weight limit, battery swap and what happens if something goes wrong. Five minutes of reading saves misunderstandings later.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter with Van Phu</h2>\n<p>All our bikes cost 150,000 VND per day, with no cash deposit (you leave your passport or ID card), a helmet and a phone holder included. See photos and details on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. Send us your plans — we\'ll tell you honestly whether an electric scooter fits them.</p>\n')
