C["articles"]['coffee'] = ('da-lat-cafes-with-a-view-by-motorbike', 'Da Lat Cafés With a View: Where to Ride for Coffee | Van Phu', 'Coffee With a View: Da Lat Cafés You Can Reach by Motorbike', 'Where to drink coffee with a view in Da Lat: lakeside, valley, pine-forest and rooftop cafés by area, the best time to go, parking and riding tips, plus two free café maps.', 'Coffee in Da Lat comes with a view: misty lakes, pine hills, greenhouse valleys and city lights. Here is how to plan a café day by motorbike — which areas to head for, when to go and how to ride there comfortably.', 'white-honda-vision-sidewalk-dalat', '\n<h2>Why Da Lat is a café town</h2>\n<p>Da Lat sits on a high plateau at about 1,500 metres, so mornings are cool, the air is fresh and the coffee is strong. Much of Vietnam\'s coffee grows on the Central Highlands around the city, and drinking it slowly while looking over a valley is a local habit. Many of the best view cafés are on hillsides a few kilometres from the centre — easy on a scooter, tiring on foot.</p>\n<h2>The main café areas</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Xuan Huong Lake and the centre:</b> rooftop cafés above the city and garden cafés right by the lake. Flat and close together — the easiest start, perfect for a first morning.</li>\n<li><b>3/4 Street and the southern hills:</b> cafés built on the slopes look over pine forest and the valley, and are best at sunset. Some lanes down to the cafés are short but steep — ride slowly or park at the top.</li>\n<li><b>East towards Trai Mat:</b> garden cafés with flower beds and rooftops over greenhouse valleys. A nice morning loop that you can combine with Da Lat Railway Station.</li>\n<li><b>Tuyen Lam Lake:</b> about 5 km south of the centre as the crow flies, with tables near the water and pine hills around — calm in the morning.</li>\n<li><b>Coffee farms outside town:</b> near Ta Nung, about 11 km away by road, you can see how coffee is grown and drink it among the plants. The road is hilly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our free <a href="../../cafe-view-map/">map of 12 cafés with a view</a> shows these places with directions, and the <a href="../../night-view-cafe-map/">night-view café map</a> covers six cafés on the eastern hills where you watch the city lights after dark.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-cafe-garden-dalat}\n<h2>When to go</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Early morning:</b> the best chance of mist over the lakes and valleys, and the quietest tables.</li>\n<li><b>Late afternoon to sunset:</b> the classic time for valley-view cafés — arrive a little early to get a seat by the edge.</li>\n<li><b>Evening:</b> city-light cafés on the hills. It gets cold quickly, so bring a jacket.</li>\n<li><b>Weekends and holidays</b> are busy with visitors from Ho Chi Minh City; weekday mornings are calmest.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Opening hours and menus change often, so check Google Maps before you ride. Many cafés ask each guest to order a drink to sit.</p>\n<h2>What to order</h2>\n<p>Try cà phê sữa đá (iced coffee with condensed milk), cà phê đen (strong black coffee) or a hot cà phê sữa on a cold morning. Many places also serve egg coffee, coconut coffee, artichoke tea or hot soy milk. If you do not drink coffee, ask for trà (tea) — Da Lat\'s highland tea is excellent.</p>\n<h2>Riding and parking tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Most cafés have their own parking; staff often give you a parking ticket (gửi xe) — keep it.</li>\n<li>Plan two or three cafés in the same area instead of crossing town; it is more relaxing and you see more.</li>\n<li>Hillside lanes can be steep and wet after rain: go slowly, use both brakes and walk the bike if needed.</li>\n<li>Pull fully off the road before stopping for photos.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:couple-air-blade-dalat-center-street}\n<h2>Which bike for a café day</h2>\n<p>For cafés around the lake and in the centre, a Honda Vision or an electric scooter is light and easy. For the southern hills, Trai Mat or coffee farms outside town, the Honda Air Blade 125cc is more comfortable, especially with two people. After dark, you can continue to the <a href="{ART:nightmarket}">night market</a> for dinner.</p>\n<h2>Rent a scooter for your café day</h2>\n<p>At Van Phu every bike is 150,000 VND per day with no cash deposit (you leave your passport or ID card), a free helmet and a phone holder for Google Maps. Our shop opens at 4:30 am, so you can catch the morning mist. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> and message us on WhatsApp — we are happy to suggest a café route for the weather that day.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-early'] = ('early-motorbike-rental-da-lat', 'Early Motorbike Rental in Da Lat: Shop Opens 4:30 am | Van Phu', 'Early Motorbike Rental in Da Lat: Our Shop Opens at 4:30 am Every Day', 'Early motorbike rental in Da Lat for sunrise and cloud hunting: our shop opens at 4:30 am every day. How to book, pick up at dawn or the day before, and which bike to take.', 'Sunrise, clouds over the valleys and the Cau Dat tea hills all happen before most rental shops open. That is why our shop in Da Lat opens at 4:30 am every day — here is how an early motorbike rental works with us.', 'van-phu-shop-early-pickup-air-blade', '\n<h2>Why you need a bike before dawn</h2>\n<p>The best moments in Da Lat are early. Cloud hunting (săn mây) on the hills starts around 4:30–6:30, sunrise is usually between about 5:15 and 6:00, and the mist over the lakes is gone soon after. If your rental shop opens at 8:00, you miss all of it. Grab can be slow to find at that hour, and taxis cannot take you along small hill roads at your own pace.</p>\n<h2>Our shop opens at 4:30 am, every day</h2>\n<p>Van Phu opens at 4:30 am every day of the week, including weekends. Our shop is at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học, close to the centre, so most guests can reach it quickly from their hotel. You can pick up your bike right at opening time and be on the road a few minutes later. That makes us a simple choice when you need an early motorbike rental in Da Lat.</p>\n<h2>Two ways to do it</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Pick up at dawn:</b> message us the evening before with the time you will come and the bike you want. We have it ready at 4:30, do the paperwork in a few minutes (passport or ID card, no cash deposit), you take a short test ride and we film the bike\'s condition together. A rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00, so a bike picked up at dawn is yours for the whole day.</li>\n<li><b>Pick up the day before:</b> if you are renting for several days, the easiest is to collect the bike the day before and leave straight from your hotel in the morning. Tell us your plan and we will confirm how the days are counted.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:evening-pickup-van-phu-1-15-5-thong-thien-hoc}\n<h2>Which bike for an early ride</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc:</b> the best choice for the Cau Dat tea hills, Hon Bo, Lang Biang or any ride with two people up long hills.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc:</b> light and easy for hills close to town and for lakes like Xuan Huong or Tuyen Lam.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter:</b> fine for spots close to the centre. One battery lasts about 25–30 km and each scooter comes with 2 batteries (about 50–60 km in total), but avoid long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road to Lang Biang.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Gas scooters come with 1 litre of fuel. For a longer dawn ride, fill up the evening before — some petrol stations are not open yet at 4:30.</p>\n<h2>Where to go at sunrise</h2>\n<p>Our <a href="{ART:cloudhunt}">cloud hunting guide</a> explains the best hills, the season and what time to leave, and the <a href="{ART:caudat}">Cau Dat tea hills guide</a> covers the most popular sunrise ride outside town. The <a href="../../cloud-hunting-map/">cloud-hunting map</a> has eight spots with directions.</p>\n<h2>Riding safely in the dark</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>It is cold before dawn: wear a warm jacket, long trousers and gloves — we lend a free helmet.</li>\n<li>Ride slowly with the headlight on; fog can make visibility very short.</li>\n<li>Keep to the right and watch for trucks and farm vehicles on hill roads.</li>\n<li>Save the route offline and use the phone holder on the bike instead of your hand.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Book your early pick-up</h2>\n<p>Every bike is 150,000 VND per day, fixed price, with a free helmet and phone holder. Send us your pick-up time on WhatsApp or Zalo the evening before and we will have the bike waiting. See all bikes and contacts on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-city'] = ('electric-scooter-da-lat-city-ride', 'Exploring Da Lat City by Electric Scooter: Easy Routes | Van Phu', 'Exploring Da Lat City by Electric Scooter: Relaxed Routes and Battery Tips', 'Exploring Da Lat city by electric scooter: relaxed routes around Xuan Huong Lake and the centre, how to plan around the battery, where to swap it, and which roads to avoid.', 'An electric scooter is the quietest way to see the centre of Da Lat: the lake, the market, old villas and cafés are all close together. Here is how to plan a relaxed city ride on an electric scooter without worrying about the battery.', 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '\n<h2>Why an electric scooter suits the city</h2>\n<p>Central Da Lat is compact: most famous sights are within a few kilometres of Xuan Huong Lake. An electric scooter is light, quiet and has no gears, so stopping and starting between sights is easy, and you can enjoy the pine-scented air without engine noise. Exploring Da Lat city by electric scooter is ideal if you want to take your time, stop often for photos and coffee, and stay close to the centre.</p>\n<h2>A relaxed city route</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Xuan Huong Lake:</b> start with a slow loop around the lake (about 5 km). Mornings are calm, often with mist over the water.</li>\n<li><b>Da Lat Flower Park:</b> at the north end of the lake, a good first stop.</li>\n<li><b>Lam Vien Square and Da Lat Market:</b> in the heart of town; park at a guarded lot and walk around.</li>\n<li><b>Da Lat Railway Station:</b> a short ride east of the lake, with its famous old station building.</li>\n<li><b>Domaine de Marie church and Crazy House:</b> on the west side of the centre, close to each other.</li>\n<li><b>Evening:</b> come back to the centre for the night market and the lights around the lake.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For more ideas, see our <a href="{ROUTES}">routes page</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Plan around the battery</h2>\n<p>One battery lasts about 25–30 km, and each scooter comes with 2 batteries — about 50–60 km in total. A day in the centre is usually well within that. A few habits help:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ride the hilliest part of your day first, while the battery is full.</li>\n<li>Hills and a passenger use more power — head back with some battery left.</li>\n<li>Our shop is central, so you can stop by and swap for a full battery for 20,000 VND, for example around lunchtime.</li>\n<li>Do not use other chargers and do not remove the battery yourself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Roads to avoid on an electric scooter</h2>\n<p>Electric scooters are made for city riding. Avoid long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa and the road to Lang Biang, as well as long day trips out of town. For those days, take a Honda Air Blade 125cc at the same price — many guests ride electric in the city and switch to gas for one day out. Remember the limit of 2 riders weighing under 140 kg combined.</p>\n<h2>City riding tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; go slowly around the lake and at roundabouts.</li>\n<li>Even central streets have short steep slopes — brake gently with both brakes going down.</li>\n<li>Use guarded parking (gửi xe), keep your ticket, lock the scooter and take the key.</li>\n<li>Bring a jacket for cool mornings and evenings, and a raincoat in the rainy season.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>Return before 21:00 — a rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Before your first ride, read our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a> and the <a href="{ART:lt-ev-experience}">tips from our guests\' experience</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter in the centre of Da Lat</h2>\n<p>Every bike at Van Phu is 150,000 VND per day with no cash deposit (you leave your passport or ID card), a free helmet and a phone holder. See photos and details on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>, then message us on WhatsApp or Zalo.</p>\n')
