C["articles"]['days2'] = ('da-lat-2-days-1-night-motorbike-itinerary', 'Da Lat 2 Day Itinerary by Motorbike: 2 Days 1 Night Plan | Van Phu', 'Dalat 2 Day Itinerary by Motorbike: 2 Days, 1 Night in Da Lat', 'A simple Dalat 2 day itinerary by motorbike: the city centre and pine-forest lakes on day one, a sunrise over the Cau Dat tea hills on day two, with distances, timing and riding tips.', 'Only have a weekend in Da Lat? Two days and one night is enough to see the lake, the old town, a waterfall, a monastery in the pines and a misty sunrise — if you have your own bike. Here is a relaxed plan that keeps the riding short and the views long.', 'friends-air-blade-van-phu-gate', '\n<h2>How this 2-day plan works</h2>\n<p>This Dalat 2 day itinerary by motorbike keeps day one close to town and saves the early start for day two, when the morning light is best. Most sights on day one are within about 5–7 km of Xuan Huong Lake, so even a beginner can ride it comfortably. Day two goes a little further out, about 20–25 km each way to the tea hills. If you only want one day, follow day one and add the night market.</p>\n<h2>Day 1 morning: the lake and the old town</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Xuan Huong Lake:</b> start with a slow loop around the lake (about 5 km) and a coffee by the water.</li>\n<li><b>Da Lat Railway Station:</b> a short ride east of the lake, with its old French-era station building and vintage carriages for photos.</li>\n<li><b>Domaine de Marie and Crazy House:</b> two very different buildings on the west side of the centre, close to each other — the pink church and the strange, tree-like guesthouse-gallery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Park at guarded lots (gửi xe), keep your ticket and walk between nearby sights.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-bui-thi-xuan-street-dalat}\n<h2>Day 1 afternoon: waterfall, monastery and pine lake</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Datanla Waterfall:</b> about 5–7 km south of the centre. You can walk down or ride the alpine coaster to the falls.</li>\n<li><b>Truc Lam Monastery and Tuyen Lam Lake:</b> a few kilometres further, among pine hills. Ride there directly or take the cable car from Robin Hill and come back for your bike.</li>\n<li><b>Sunset:</b> stop at a café among the pines on the way back to town.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For more ideas on this side of town, see our guide to <a href="{ART:waterfalls}">waterfalls near Da Lat</a>.</p>\n<h2>Day 1 evening: the night market</h2>\n<p>In the evening, ride back to the centre, park near the central market and walk around the <a href="{ART:nightmarket}">Da Lat night market</a>: grilled rice paper, hot soy milk, sweet soups and warm clothes if you came unprepared for the cool air. Go to bed early — tomorrow starts before dawn.</p>\n<h2>Day 2 dawn: sunrise over the Cau Dat tea hills</h2>\n<p>The tea hills of Cau Dat are about 20–25 km from the centre, roughly 40–60 minutes by scooter. Leave around 4:30–5:00 to catch the sunrise and, on lucky mornings, a sea of clouds over the valley. Our <a href="{ART:caudat}">Cau Dat sunrise guide</a> explains the road and the best time. On the way back, stop at Linh Phuoc Pagoda in Trai Mat (about 8 km from the centre), famous for its mosaic decoration, and have a late breakfast in town.</p>\n{PHOTO:air-blade-handover-dalat-homestay}\n<h2>Day 2 late morning: pack up and go</h2>\n<p>Check out of your hotel, have a last coffee and return the bike before you leave for the bus or the airport. If you still have a few hours, add a flower farm or a quiet lakeside café — keep the last ride short so you are not in a hurry.</p>\n<h2>Which bike for 2 days</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc:</b> the best choice for this plan, especially for the hills to Cau Dat and with two riders.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc:</b> light and easy, fine for day one and for one rider.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter:</b> great for day one in the city, but not for the long, hilly ride to the tea hills.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Good to know</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; ride slowly on wet or misty roads and brake gently downhill.</li>\n<li>Mornings and evenings are cold — bring a warm jacket, and a raincoat in the rainy season.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>For a longer stay, see our <a href="{ART:days3}">3-day plan</a> and the <a href="{ROUTES}">routes page</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rent your bike for the weekend at Van Phu</h2>\n<p>Every bike is 150,000 VND per day, with a free helmet, a phone holder and 1 liter of fuel in gas scooters, and no cash deposit — you just leave your passport or ID card. A rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00, and for 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat. Our shop opens at 4:30 am, so an early start is never a problem. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> and message us on WhatsApp or Zalo.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-busstation'] = ('motorbike-rental-near-dalat-bus-station', 'Motorbike Rental near Dalat Bus Station: From the Bus to Your Bike | Van Phu', 'Motorbike Rental near Dalat Bus Station: How to Get from the Bus to Your Bike', 'Motorbike rental near Dalat bus station: how to get from the intercity bus station to the city center and our shop, early pick-up from 4:30 am and what to bring.', 'Just got off the bus in Da Lat? The intercity bus station is a short ride from the centre, and our shop opens at 4:30 am — so you can go from the bus to your own bike in well under an hour. Here is how.', 'honda-air-blade-vision-fleet-van-phu', '\n<h2>Where the Da Lat bus station is</h2>\n<p>The main Da Lat Intercity Bus Station is on To Hien Thanh Street, on the south side of the city, about 1.5–2 km from Xuan Huong Lake. Many buses from Ho Chi Minh City, Nha Trang, Mui Ne and other cities stop here, although some bus companies drop passengers at their own offices in town instead. Our map of the <a href="../../saigon-bus-map/">bus station and bus company offices</a> shows where they are.</p>\n<h2>How to get from Dalat bus station to the city center</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Grab or taxi:</b> the easiest way with luggage. Taxis wait at the station, and Grab cars and bikes work in Da Lat. The ride to the centre or to our shop at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học is about 3–4 km — usually around 10 minutes.</li>\n<li><b>Bus company shuttle:</b> many companies run a free shuttle van between their station and central hotels — ask when you book your ticket.</li>\n<li><b>On foot:</b> possible to the lake, but the streets climb uphill, and it\'s tiring with a big backpack. We don\'t recommend walking to our shop with luggage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prices of Grab and taxis change with time and demand, so check the app or ask the driver before you go.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-hotel-delivery-dalat}\n<h2>Pick up your bike right after you arrive</h2>\n<p>Many night buses reach Da Lat early in the morning, when most shops are still closed. Our shop opens at 4:30 am every day, so you don\'t have to wait for hours. Message us your bus company and approximate arrival time, and we\'ll have your bike ready. The paperwork takes a few minutes: leave your passport or ID card (no cash deposit), take a short test ride and we film the bike\'s condition together. Find all the steps in <a href="{ART:lt-procedure}">how to rent a motorbike in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n<p>Prefer to go to your hotel first? Drop your bags, rest, and come to us later, or for rentals of 2 days or more we deliver the bike free to hotels in central Da Lat. For a 1-day rental, message us and we\'ll see what we can do.</p>\n<h2>What to bring and what to send us</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Your passport (foreign guests) or CCCD/ID card (Vietnamese guests).</li>\n<li>Your rental dates, the bike you want (Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter) and your arrival time.</li>\n<li>Your hotel name if you want delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Leaving Da Lat by bus</h2>\n<p>On your last day, return the bike to our shop before 21:00 — a rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00 — and take a Grab or taxi to the station or your bus company\'s office. If you leave on a night bus, plan your return so you\'re not rushing with luggage. Some bus companies also pick passengers up from central hotels.</p>\n<h2>Good to know</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; Da Lat\'s streets are hilly, so take your first ride slowly.</li>\n<li>Mornings are cold — keep a jacket in your day bag after a night bus.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Motorbike rental near Dalat bus station — book ahead</h2>\n<p>Every bike is 150,000 VND per day with a free helmet and phone holder, and gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. Send us your arrival time on WhatsApp (+84 944 081 101) or Zalo (0944 081 101), and see all bikes and conditions on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-price'] = ('electric-scooter-rental-price-dalat', 'Electric Scooter Rental Price in Da Lat: 150,000 VND/Day | Van Phu', 'Electric Scooter Rental Price in Da Lat: What 150,000 VND a Day Includes', 'Electric scooter rental price in Da Lat at Van Phu: 150,000 VND per day with 2 batteries, helmet and phone holder, battery swap 20,000 VND, no cash deposit.', 'How much does it cost to rent an electric scooter in Da Lat? At Van Phu the answer is simple: 150,000 VND per day, the same fixed price as our gas scooters. Here is exactly what that includes and the only extra you might pay.', 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>Electric scooter rental price in Da Lat: one fixed price</h2>\n<p>Our electric scooter rental price in Da Lat is 150,000 VND per day. It\'s the same for every bike we rent — electric scooter, Honda Vision 110cc or Honda Air Blade 125cc — and it\'s fixed, with no hidden fees. You pay the full amount at pick-up. For prices of all bikes and longer rentals, see our <a href="../dalat-motorbike-rental-price/">rental price guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>What\'s included in 150,000 VND</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>2 batteries:</b> one battery lasts about 25–30 km; each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 50–60 km in total — more than enough for a day in the city.</li>\n<li><b>Helmets:</b> free helmets for the rider and the passenger.</li>\n<li><b>Phone holder</b> on the handlebar, for maps.</li>\n<li><b>No cash deposit:</b> you leave your passport or ID card and get it back when you return the scooter.</li>\n<li><b>Test ride and video:</b> before you leave, you try the scooter and we film its condition together.</li>\n<li><b>Free hotel delivery</b> in central Da Lat for rentals of 2 days or more.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:electric-scooters-van-phu-shop}\n<h2>The only extra: battery swap 20,000 VND</h2>\n<p>If you ride a lot and use up both batteries, come to our shop and swap for a fully charged battery for 20,000 VND. There is no need to charge anything yourself — please don\'t use other chargers and don\'t remove the battery. Most guests riding in the centre never need a swap, but it\'s useful on a long day of café hopping.</p>\n<h2>How the rental day is counted</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>One rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00 the same day.</li>\n<li>Returning at 21:00 the next day counts as 2 days, and so on.</li>\n<li>Returning after 21:00 is charged as one extra day — message us first if you want to extend.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So picking up early in the morning gives you the most riding for your money — our shop opens at 4:30 am.</p>\n<h2>Possible costs that are not ours</h2>\n<p>Parking at sights and markets (gửi xe) is a small fee paid to the parking attendant. Entrance tickets to sights are separate too. Any new damage compared with the pick-up video is paid at the actual repair cost — the full rules are in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n<h2>Is the electric scooter worth it?</h2>\n<p>For riding in the city — the lake, the market, cafés and sights in the centre — yes: it\'s quiet, light, has no gears and needs no fuel stops. It carries up to 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg. For long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road to Lang Biang, take a Honda Air Blade 125cc for the same price. Many guests ride electric in the city and switch to the Air Blade for a day trip.</p>\n<h2>Book an electric scooter</h2>\n<p>See photos and details on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter page</a>, compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>, and message us on WhatsApp (+84 944 081 101) or Zalo (0944 081 101) with your dates. No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</p>\n')
