C["articles"]['getaround'] = ('how-to-get-around-da-lat-without-a-car', 'How to Get Around Dalat Without a Car: Walking, Grab, Buses, Scooters | Van Phu', 'How to Get Around Dalat Without a Car: Walking, Grab, Buses or a Scooter', 'How to get around Dalat without a car: what you can walk, when Grab or a taxi makes sense, what local buses are good for, and why many travellers rent a scooter.', "Da Lat is a hilly mountain city, and its best views are spread over pine hills, lakes and farms outside the centre. You don't need a car to see it — but it helps to know which way of getting around fits which part of your trip. Here is an honest comparison.", 'honda-vision-girls-helmets-dalat', '\n<h2>Walking: great for the centre, hard beyond it</h2>\n<p>The heart of Da Lat is compact. Xuan Huong Lake, Da Lat Market, the night market, the Hoa Binh area and many cafés are within about 1–2 km of each other, so a walking day in the centre is easy and pleasant in the cool air. Walking stops being fun once you leave the centre: the streets climb and drop all the time, pavements are narrow or missing in places, and most sights — waterfalls, Tuyen Lam Lake, the tea hills, flower farms — are 5–25 km away.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Good for:</b> the lake, the market, cafés, an evening at the night market.</li>\n<li><b>Not good for:</b> anything outside the centre, rainy afternoons, older travellers on steep streets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Grab and taxis: easy point-to-point rides</h2>\n<p>Ride-hailing apps such as Grab work in Da Lat, and there are local taxi companies too. You can order a car or a motorbike ride (with a driver) and see the price in the app before you book. This is a comfortable choice for single trips — from the bus station to your hotel, to a restaurant at night, or when it is pouring with rain.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Pros:</b> no riding yourself, door to door, good in heavy rain.</li>\n<li><b>Cons:</b> costs add up when you visit several places a day; at remote sights you may wait a long time for a car back; you cannot stop on the road for a photo.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Local buses: for towns, not for sightseeing</h2>\n<p>Da Lat has a few local and regional bus routes linking the city with nearby towns and the airport area. They are cheap, but they follow fixed routes and timetables and don\'t stop at most viewpoints, waterfalls or cafés. Ask your hotel for the current routes before relying on them; for a sightseeing day they are rarely practical.</p>\n<h2>Tours: the whole day is planned for you</h2>\n<p>Group or private tours (by minibus, jeep or with a motorbike driver) are a good option if you don\'t want to ride at all. You give up flexibility — the timing and stops are fixed — but you don\'t have to think about navigation.</p>\n{PHOTO:couple-air-blade-dalat-center-street}\n<h2>Renting a scooter: the most freedom</h2>\n<p>This is why so many travellers choose a scooter in Da Lat. With your own bike you can catch the sunrise at the tea hills, stop at a café among the pines, change plans when the clouds roll in and reach places no bus goes. All our bikes are automatic — no gears or clutch — so it is easier than many people expect.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Pros:</b> your own timing, any detour you like, one fixed price for the whole day.</li>\n<li><b>Cons:</b> you need to ride carefully on hills and in rain; it is not for travellers who have never ridden a two-wheeler and don\'t want to learn.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Which option fits which day</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Arrival and departure day:</b> walk or take a Grab with your luggage.</li>\n<li><b>A day in the centre:</b> walking plus a scooter or Grab for the far end of town.</li>\n<li><b>Waterfalls, lakes, tea hills, flower farms:</b> a scooter, or a tour if you don\'t ride.</li>\n<li><b>Heavy rain:</b> Grab or a taxi, and an indoor plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For ideas where to ride, see our <a href="{ROUTES}">motorbike routes around Da Lat</a> and the <a href="{ART:days2}">2-day itinerary</a>. New to riding here? Read our <a href="{ART:riding}">riding tips</a> first.</p>\n<h2>Rent a scooter from Van Phu</h2>\n<p>Every bike — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or an electric scooter — costs 150,000 VND per day, with a helmet and a phone holder included, and 1 liter of fuel in gas scooters. There is no cash deposit: you leave your passport or ID card. Vietnam drives on the right; no alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law. Prices and booking: <a href="{HOME}">motorbike rental in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-automatic'] = ('automatic-scooter-rental-dalat', 'Automatic Scooter Rental Dalat: No Gears, Easy to Ride | Van Phu', 'Automatic Scooter Rental in Dalat: Which Bike to Choose and How to Ride It', 'Automatic scooter rental in Dalat: all our bikes are automatic — Honda Vision, Air Blade 125cc or electric. Which one to pick, first-ride tips, 150,000 VND/day.', "Worried about gears and a clutch? You don't need to be. Every bike we rent in Da Lat is automatic: you twist the throttle to go and use the brakes to stop. Here is how to rent an automatic scooter in Dalat and which one suits your trip.", 'van-phu-shop-busy-morning-vision-air-blade', '\n<h2>Automatic scooter rental in Dalat: all our bikes are automatic</h2>\n<p>At Van Phu there are no manual or semi-automatic bikes. For automatic scooter rental in Dalat you can choose from three models, all at the same fixed price of 150,000 VND per day:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc:</b> light, low and easy to handle — a favourite for city riding and for riders who feel less confident.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc:</b> more power for hills and two riders, comfortable on day trips to waterfalls or the tea hills.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter:</b> quiet, no fuel, very easy in the city; one battery lasts about 25–30 km, and each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 50–60 km in total. Not for long steep passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What "automatic" means on a scooter</h2>\n<p>There is no clutch lever and no gear pedal. The right grip is the throttle; the right lever is the front brake and the left lever is the rear brake. You start the engine with the brake held, twist gently and the scooter rolls off smoothly. That\'s all — which is why automatic scooters are the most common rental bikes in Vietnam.</p>\n<h2>Which automatic scooter should you pick?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Mostly the centre, one rider:</b> Honda Vision or an electric scooter.</li>\n<li><b>Two riders, or Prenn, Mimosa, Lang Biang, Cau Dat:</b> Honda Air Blade 125cc.</li>\n<li><b>Not sure yet:</b> start with a Vision for a day; if you plan a longer trip later, swap to an Air Blade.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We explain the differences in more detail in person, and you can try the bike in front of the shop before you decide.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-honda-vision-van-phu-shop}\n<h2>How to rent an automatic scooter in Dalat</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat with your dates and the bike you like.</li>\n<li>Come to the shop at 1/15/5 Thong Thien Hoc (open from 4:30 am).</li>\n<li>Take a short test ride and we film the bike\'s condition together.</li>\n<li>Leave your passport or ID card — there is no cash deposit — and pay for the rental.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The full step-by-step guide is here: <a href="{ART:lt-procedure}">how to rent a motorbike in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n<h2>First-ride tips for automatic scooters</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Twist the throttle slowly — automatics pull away as soon as you open it.</li>\n<li>Use both brakes together, gently, especially downhill and on wet roads.</li>\n<li>On long descents, ease off the throttle early instead of braking hard at the last moment.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; ride steadily and let faster traffic pass.</li>\n<li>Practise for a few minutes on a quiet street before heading into busy roads.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What\'s included</h2>\n<p>Every bike comes with a helmet and a phone holder; gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. A rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00. For 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat. Compare all bikes and book on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-battery'] = ('electric-scooter-battery-da-lat', 'Electric Scooter Battery in Da Lat: How Far Can You Go? | Van Phu', 'Renting an Electric Scooter in Da Lat: How Far the Battery Goes and When to Swap', 'How far can a rented electric scooter go in Da Lat? One battery lasts about 25–30 km, 2 batteries about 50–60 km; swap at the shop for 20,000 VND. Tips for hills.', 'The first question almost every guest asks about our electric scooters is: how far can I go? Here is the honest answer, what uses more battery in a hilly city like Da Lat, and how to plan a day so you never run low.', 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '\n<h2>How far a rented electric scooter goes in Da Lat</h2>\n<p>When you rent an electric scooter in Da Lat from us, it comes with 2 batteries. One battery lasts about 25–30 km; each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 50–60 km in total. You start the day with both full. For a day in the city — the lake, the market, cafés and central sights — that is usually more than enough.</p>\n<h2>What uses more battery</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hills:</b> Da Lat is hilly even in the centre. Long climbs use noticeably more power than flat roads.</li>\n<li><b>Two riders:</b> a passenger adds weight, so the battery goes down faster. Two riders must weigh under 140 kg combined.</li>\n<li><b>Full throttle:</b> fast starts and riding at top speed all the time shorten the distance.</li>\n<li><b>Cold mornings:</b> batteries are a little less lively in cold weather, so keep some reserve on early rides.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>When to swap the battery</h2>\n<p>Watch the battery indicator. When about a third is left, start heading back towards the centre. If you want to keep riding, come to our shop at 1/15/5 Thong Thien Hoc and we swap in a fully charged battery for 20,000 VND. Never ride until the battery is completely flat — pushing a scooter up a Da Lat hill is no fun.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Please don\'t use other chargers and don\'t remove the battery yourself.</li>\n<li>Our shop is central, so a swap fits easily into a lunch or coffee break.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Plan your day around the battery</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Do the hilliest or furthest stop first, while the battery is full.</li>\n<li>Group sights that are close together, park and walk between them.</li>\n<li>Keep the evening ride to the night market short and simple.</li>\n<li>For the wider city area, our article on <a href="{ART:lt-ev-city}">exploring Da Lat by electric scooter</a> has a relaxed route.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Where the electric scooter is not the right choice</h2>\n<p>Long, steep passes such as Prenn and Mimosa and the road up Lang Biang, as well as long day trips to the tea hills or the big waterfalls, are not a good fit for an electric scooter. For those days, take a Honda Air Blade 125cc — same price, more power, and you won\'t think about the battery at all.</p>\n<h2>Good to know</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Before you leave, we take a short test ride and film the scooter together, including the battery level.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; in rain slow down and never ride through deep water.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>All details are in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter</h2>\n<p>150,000 VND per day with 2 batteries, helmets and a phone holder, and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. See photos on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
