C["articles"]['tuyenlam'] = ('tuyen-lam-lake-truc-lam-monastery-da-lat', 'Tuyen Lam Lake & Truc Lam Monastery, Da Lat: Guide by Motorbike | Van Phu', 'Tuyen Lam Lake and Truc Lam Monastery: A Half-Day Ride from Da Lat', 'How to visit Tuyen Lam Lake and Truc Lam Zen Monastery from Da Lat by motorbike: the route, the cable car option, temple etiquette, best time and what to combine.', "Just south of the city, Tuyen Lam Lake spreads between pine-covered hills, and the Truc Lam Zen Monastery looks down on it from Phoenix Mountain. It's one of the easiest and most peaceful half-day trips from Da Lat — here's how to do it by motorbike.", 'white-honda-vision-sidewalk-dalat', '<h2>Tuyen Lam Lake in a few words</h2>\n<p>Tuyen Lam is the largest lake around Da Lat, a reservoir about 6–7 km south of the centre, surrounded by pine forest and small hills. The water is calm, the air is cool and, compared with the city, it is wonderfully quiet. Most visitors come for the views, a slow walk along the shore, a coffee by the water or a boat ride on the lake.</p>\n<h2>Truc Lam Zen Monastery</h2>\n<p>Thiền viện Trúc Lâm (Truc Lam Zen Monastery) sits on Phoenix Mountain (Núi Phụng Hoàng) above the lake. It opened in the 1990s and belongs to the Vietnamese Trúc Lâm Zen tradition. Visitors walk through gardens of flowers and bonsai, past the main hall and the bell tower, to a viewpoint over Tuyen Lam Lake.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is an active monastery: dress modestly (shoulders and knees covered), speak quietly and don\'t enter areas reserved for monks.</li>\n<li>Take off your shoes where signs ask you to, and don\'t fly drones or play music.</li>\n<li>From the monastery, steps lead down towards the lake shore.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:van-phu-guest-air-blade-thumbs-up}\n<h2>How to get there by motorbike</h2>\n<p>From the centre, the ride takes roughly 15–25 minutes. The usual way is to follow 3 Tháng 4 street (the road towards Prenn) south, then turn right onto the road signposted to Thiền viện Trúc Lâm and Tuyền Lâm. The road climbs and winds through pine forest and is in good condition. Put "Thiền viện Trúc Lâm" into Google Maps on your phone holder and you\'ll be fine.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A Honda Air Blade 125cc is the most comfortable choice with two people on these hills; a Honda Vision does the trip easily with one rider.</li>\n<li>An electric scooter can reach the lake and come back within its range, but don\'t continue down the Prenn Pass on it.</li>\n<li>Park in the guarded parking areas (gửi xe) near the monastery or the lake and keep your ticket.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The cable car option</h2>\n<p>If you like, you can also reach the monastery by the Robin Hill cable car, which starts from Robin Hill on the edge of the city and glides over the pine forest to Truc Lam. Many travellers ride a scooter to Robin Hill, take the cable car one way for the view, and ride the rest of the loop. Check the current operating hours and price on the spot, as they can change.</p>\n<h2>When to go</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Morning</b> is the best time: soft light, mist on the water on cool days and fewer tour groups.</li>\n<li><b>Late afternoon</b> is lovely too, but plan to ride back before dark — the forest road has few lights.</li>\n<li>In the rainy season (roughly May to October), showers usually come in the afternoon, so start early and bring a raincoat.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to combine it with</h2>\n<p>Tuyen Lam fits nicely into a half-day loop. Datanla Waterfall is close by on the road towards Prenn — see our <a href="{ART:waterfalls}">waterfalls near Da Lat guide</a>. In November, wild sunflowers bloom along the roads around the lake. For more ideas, look at our <a href="{ROUTES}">motorbike routes around Da Lat</a>.</p>\n<h2>Safety tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; ride slowly on the bends and watch for tour buses.</li>\n<li>Going downhill, brake gently with both brakes.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Ride there with Văn Phú</h2>\n<p>Tuyen Lam is a perfect first trip on a rented scooter: close, scenic and calm. Every bike costs 150,000 VND per day with a free helmet and a phone holder, and you leave only your passport or ID card — no cash deposit. Pick your bike on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-delivery'] = ('motorbike-rental-hotel-delivery-dalat', 'Motorbike Rental with Hotel Delivery in Dalat: Free for 2+ Days | Van Phu', 'Motorbike Rental with Hotel Delivery in Dalat: How It Works', 'Motorbike rental with hotel delivery in Dalat: free delivery to hotels in central Da Lat for 2+ days, what to send us, and how the handover at your hotel works.', "Don't want to walk to a rental shop on your first morning? We can bring the bike to you. Here's how our motorbike rental with hotel delivery in Dalat works, when it's free and what to send us so the handover is quick.", 'vision-air-blade-villa-delivery-dalat', '<h2>When is hotel delivery free?</h2>\n<p>For rentals of <b>2 days or more</b>, we deliver your bike <b>free of charge to hotels, homestays and villas in central Da Lat</b>. That\'s the simple rule. If you only need a bike for one day, or you\'re staying further out of the centre, just message us and ask — we\'ll tell you honestly what\'s possible that day.</p>\n<p>The price doesn\'t change because of delivery: every bike costs 150,000 VND per day, fixed. Planning a short stay? See also <a href="{ART:lt-2days}">renting a scooter in Dalat for 2 days</a>.</p>\n<h2>What to send us when you book</h2>\n<p>Message us on WhatsApp (+84 944 081 101 or +84 834 622 149), Zalo (0944 081 101), Telegram (@vanphudalat) or WeChat (vanphudalat) with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <b>name of your hotel</b> (and the address or a Google Maps pin if it\'s a small homestay);</li>\n<li>the <b>date and time</b> you\'d like the bike;</li>\n<li><b>how many days</b> you\'ll rent;</li>\n<li><b>which bike</b>: Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or an electric scooter;</li>\n<li><b>how many riders</b>, so we bring the right number of helmets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We confirm the time with you by message. Early riders take note: our shop opens at 4:30 am, so a bike for a sunrise trip is not a problem.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-hotel-delivery-dalat}\n<h2>What happens at the handover</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>We meet you at your hotel\'s entrance or reception.</li>\n<li>You show and leave your <b>passport</b> (foreign guests) or <b>ID card</b> (Vietnamese guests). There is <b>no cash deposit</b>.</li>\n<li>You pay the rental for your days.</li>\n<li>We show you how the bike works and you take a short <b>test ride</b>.</li>\n<li>We check the bike together and <b>film its condition on video</b>, so everything is clear for both sides.</li>\n<li>You get a free helmet (one per rider). Every bike has a phone holder, and gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The whole thing usually takes about ten minutes. More on the paperwork in <a href="{ART:lt-procedure}">how to rent a motorbike in Da Lat step by step</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:air-blade-handover-dalat-homestay}\n<h2>Tips for a smooth delivery</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Book a day ahead if you can, especially at weekends and on holidays.</li>\n<li>Keep your phone on around the delivery time so we can call when we arrive.</li>\n<li>Ask your hotel where guests can park; many have a small parking area or an attendant.</li>\n<li>A rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00 — plan your return time when you book, and ask us how you\'d like to return the bike.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Prefer to come to the shop?</h2>\n<p>Of course. Our shop is at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học in central Da Lat. If you arrive by bus, read <a href="{ART:lt-busstation}">motorbike rental near Dalat bus station</a>.</p>\n<h2>Ride safely</h2>\n<p>Vietnam drives on the right. Wear your helmet with the strap fastened, never ride after alcohol or beer, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</p>\n<h2>Book your bike with delivery</h2>\n<p>Send us your hotel name, dates and preferred bike, and we\'ll do the rest. See all bikes and terms on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-cafes'] = ('cafe-hopping-da-lat-electric-scooter', 'Café Hopping in Da Lat by Electric Scooter: Areas & Tips | Van Phu', 'Café Hopping in Da Lat by Electric Scooter: A Quiet Way to Explore', 'Café hopping in Da Lat by electric scooter: which areas to combine, short hops between cafés, parking, battery planning and what to avoid on the hills.', "Da Lat is a city of cafés — on hillsides, in pine gardens, above the lake. An electric scooter is a quiet, easy way to hop between them. Here's how to plan a relaxed day of café hopping in Da Lat by electric scooter.", 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '<h2>Why an electric scooter suits café hopping</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Short distances:</b> many of Da Lat\'s best cafés are only a few kilometres apart.</li>\n<li><b>Quiet and smooth:</b> no engine noise and no gears — just twist and go, and stop often.</li>\n<li><b>No fuel stops:</b> one battery lasts about 25–30 km; each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 50–60 km in total. That\'s more than enough for a day of short hops.</li>\n<li><b>Easy to park:</b> light enough to push into tight café parking spots.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Plan by area, not by list</h2>\n<p>The trick is to group cafés by area so you ride a little and sit a lot. A relaxed day might look like this:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Morning around Xuan Huong Lake:</b> start with coffee near the lake while the mist lifts, then ride the streets on the hills around it — many small cafés have views over the rooftops.</li>\n<li><b>Late morning in the pine-garden cafés</b> on the edges of the centre: garden seating, flowers and calm.</li>\n<li><b>Afternoon on the eastern hills</b> towards Trai Mat: quieter roads and cafés looking over greenhouses and valleys. Keep an eye on the battery here, as the roads go up and down.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For café ideas by area, read our <a href="{ART:coffee}">coffee with a view guide</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Battery planning in one minute</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Check the battery level when you leave, and again after each café.</li>\n<li>Hills use more battery than flat streets, so keep the longest ride for when the battery is fresh.</li>\n<li>When about a third is left, head back towards the centre. A battery swap at our shop costs 20,000 VND.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What to avoid</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road up Lang Biang are not for electric scooters. If your café list includes places out that way, rent a Honda Air Blade 125cc instead — same price.</li>\n<li>Max 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding — order coffee, tea or a hot chocolate instead — and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All conditions are in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>. If you want a city route as well, see <a href="{ART:lt-ev-city}">exploring Da Lat by electric scooter</a>.</p>\n<h2>Parking at cafés</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Many cafés have their own parking with an attendant; elsewhere use a guarded lot (gửi xe) and keep the ticket.</li>\n<li>Lock the steering and take the key with you.</li>\n<li>Don\'t leave phones or bags on the scooter while you sit inside.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Small comfort tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Da Lat mornings and evenings can be cold — bring a light jacket for the ride.</li>\n<li>Use the phone holder for Google Maps; never hold your phone while riding.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; ride slowly in the narrow lanes around the cafés.</li>\n<li>A rental day runs until 21:00, so evening coffee near the lake is the perfect last stop.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter for your café day</h2>\n<p>150,000 VND per day, free helmet, phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. See the scooters on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a>, or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
