C["articles"]['photospots'] = ('best-photo-spots-da-lat-by-motorbike', 'Best Photo Spots in Da Lat by Motorbike: Where & When to Shoot | Van Phu', 'Best Photo Spots in Da Lat by Motorbike (and the Best Time for Each)', 'The best photo spots in Da Lat by motorbike: lakes, pink church, tea hills, flower fields and pagodas, with the best light for each and safe riding tips.', 'Da Lat is one of the most photogenic cities in Vietnam: misty lakes, pine hills, French-era churches and flower fields. With a scooter you can reach the best photo spots at the right hour, before the crowds arrive. Here is a practical shortlist, grouped by area.', 'honda-vision-girls-helmets-dalat', '<h2>Why a motorbike makes better photos</h2>\n<p>Light is everything in photography, and in Da Lat the best light comes early: roughly 5:30–7:30 in the morning and 16:00–17:30 in the afternoon. With your own scooter you can be at a lake or a tea hill at sunrise, move on when the tour buses arrive and come back to the city for golden hour — no waiting for a car.</p>\n<h2>In the city centre (easy, any bike)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Xuan Huong Lake</b> — at dawn, mist often lies on the water. The loop around the lake is about 5 km, so you can stop at several angles.</li>\n<li><b>Lam Vien Square</b> — the giant artichoke and wild-sunflower glass domes look best in the evening, when they are lit.</li>\n<li><b>Da Lat Cathedral (Rooster Church)</b> — the French-era tower with the rooster on top glows in late-afternoon light.</li>\n<li><b>Domaine de Marie (Pink Church)</b> — pink walls and a quiet garden on a hillside; mornings are calm. See our <a href="{ART:domainedemarie}">Domaine de Marie guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Da Lat Railway Station</b> — colourful 1930s architecture and vintage carriages; come in the morning.</li>\n<li><b>Crazy House</b> — fairy-tale towers and bridges; arrive right at opening for empty frames.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:couple-air-blade-dalat-center-street}\n<h2>Just outside the centre (15–30 minutes)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Tuyen Lam Lake and Truc Lam Monastery</b> — pine forest, still water and a Zen garden; early morning is best. Read our <a href="{ART:tuyenlam}">Tuyen Lam guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Linh Phuoc Pagoda</b> in Trai Mat — the mosaic dragon made of bottle and pottery shards is full of detail; morning light works well.</li>\n<li><b>Van Thanh Flower Village</b> — flower fields and greenhouses just west of town.</li>\n<li><b>Valley of Love</b> and <b>Datanla Waterfall</b> — forest, water and flowers; mornings are quieter.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sunrise trips (start before dawn)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cau Dat tea hills</b> — rolling tea rows with clouds in the valley; the ride is long and hilly, so take a Honda Air Blade 125cc. Details in our <a href="{ART:caudat}">Cau Dat sunrise guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Cloud hunting</b> — on clear mornings after a rainy night, the hills around Da Lat fill with a sea of clouds. Our <a href="{ART:cloudhunt}">cloud-hunting guide</a> explains where and when.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Seasonal colours</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Wild sunflowers (dã quỳ): roughly late October to December, along many roads around the city.</li>\n<li>Pink grass hills (cỏ hồng): roughly November to December.</li>\n<li>Cherry blossom (mai anh đào): roughly late January to February.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All these places are marked on our free <a href="../../photo-map/">Da Lat photo-spot map</a>, with directions and a printable PDF.</p>\n<h2>Photo and safety tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Never stop on a bend or in the road for a photo — pull fully off the road first.</li>\n<li>Mornings are cold: bring a jacket, and a cloth to wipe the lens in the mist.</li>\n<li>Put the map on your phone holder; never hold the phone while riding.</li>\n<li>At pagodas and churches, dress modestly and ask before photographing people.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Helmets always on, no alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Your photo day with Văn Phú</h2>\n<p>Our shop opens at 4:30 am, so you can pick up a bike in time for sunrise. Every bike is 150,000 VND per day with a free helmet and a phone holder; you leave only your passport or ID card, no cash deposit. Choose a Honda Vision for the city or an Air Blade for the hills on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-center'] = ('motorbike-rental-near-central-da-lat', 'Motorbike Rental Near Central Da Lat & the Night Market | Van Phu', 'Motorbike Rental Near Central Da Lat: How to Find Us and Ride the Centre', 'Motorbike rental near central Da Lat and the night market: where the shop is, how to get there on foot or by Grab, and where to park near the market.', "Staying in the centre and want a scooter close by? Van Phú is in central Da Lat at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học. Here's how to reach the shop, what to rent for city riding, and how to ride to the night market and park without stress.", 'van-phu-shop-busy-morning-vision-air-blade', '<h2>Where the shop is</h2>\n<p>Our shop is at <b>1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học, Xuân Hương – Đà Lạt</b>, in central Da Lat. It is marked on Google Maps as Văn Phú, so the easiest way is to open the map on your phone and follow it. If you are looking for motorbike rental near central Da Lat, most hotels in the centre are a short ride away.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>On foot:</b> from many hotels around Da Lat Market and Xuan Huong Lake you can walk to us — check the route on Google Maps first, as Da Lat is hilly and some streets are steep.</li>\n<li><b>By Grab:</b> a Grab bike or car from the centre takes only a few minutes — just enter our address.</li>\n<li><b>Delivery:</b> renting for 2 days or more? We deliver the bike free to hotels in central Da Lat. For 1 day, message us and we\'ll see what\'s possible.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-honda-vision-van-phu-shop}\n<h2>What to rent for the city centre</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc</b> — light and easy, perfect for short city rides and narrow streets.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter</b> — quiet and simple for the centre; see our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter page</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc</b> — if you also plan longer trips over the passes or ride with two people.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every bike is 150,000 VND per day, with a free helmet and a phone holder. Gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. There is no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. Step-by-step details are in <a href="{ART:lt-procedure}">how to rent a motorbike in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n<h2>Riding to the night market</h2>\n<p>Many guests looking for motorbike rental near the Dalat night market want to ride there in the evening. The market area around Da Lat Market gets very busy after about 18:00, with lots of people walking on the streets.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ride slowly near the market — pedestrians, food stalls and other scooters are everywhere.</li>\n<li>Use a guarded parking lot (gửi xe) near the market and keep your ticket; ask the attendant before you leave the bike.</li>\n<li>Lock the steering and take your key; don\'t leave bags on the bike.</li>\n<li>Da Lat evenings are cold — bring a jacket for the ride back.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For what to eat and buy there, read our <a href="{ART:nightmarket}">Da Lat night market guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>Good to know</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>We open every day from 4:30 am — handy for sunrise trips.</li>\n<li>A rental day runs from pickup until 21:00.</li>\n<li>Before you go, we do a short test ride with you and record a video of the bike\'s condition.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Book your bike in the centre</h2>\n<p>Send us your dates on WhatsApp (+84 944 081 101 or +84 834 622 149) or Zalo (0944 081 101), or just come by. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-night'] = ('electric-scooter-da-lat-evening-night-ride', 'Electric Scooter in Da Lat in the Evening: Night Ride Tips | Van Phu', 'Riding an Electric Scooter in Da Lat in the Evening: Practical Tips', 'Riding a rented electric scooter in Da Lat in the evening: lights, cold air, the night market, battery planning and returning before 21:00.', 'Da Lat changes after sunset: the lake lights up, the night market fills with food stalls and the air turns cool. An electric scooter is a quiet, easy way to enjoy the evening — if you plan a few simple things.', 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '<h2>Why an electric scooter suits the evening</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Short city distances:</b> the lake, Lam Vien Square and the night market are all close to each other.</li>\n<li><b>Quiet and smooth:</b> no engine noise and no gears — pleasant in busy, slow evening traffic.</li>\n<li><b>Easy to park:</b> light enough to move into a tight spot at a guarded lot.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Plan the battery before dark</h2>\n<p>One battery lasts about 25–30 km; each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 50–60 km in total. An evening in the centre uses only a small part of that — but if you have been riding all day, check the level before sunset. A battery swap at our shop costs 20,000 VND, so it is easy to start the evening with a fresh battery.</p>\n<h2>Lights and visibility</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Check the headlight, brake light and indicators before you leave.</li>\n<li>Electric scooters are quiet — pedestrians may not hear you, so ride slowly and use the horn lightly when needed.</li>\n<li>Wear something light-coloured if you can, and keep a safe distance from cars.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:evening-pickup-van-phu-1-15-5-thong-thien-hoc}\n<h2>Dress for the cold</h2>\n<p>Da Lat nights are cool all year, and the wind on a scooter makes it feel colder. Bring a jacket, long trousers and closed shoes. In the rainy season, keep a raincoat under the seat.</p>\n<h2>A simple evening route</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Start with a slow loop around <b>Xuan Huong Lake</b> as the lights come on.</li>\n<li>Stop at <b>Lam Vien Square</b> to see the lit glass domes.</li>\n<li>Park at a guarded lot (gửi xe) near <b>Da Lat Market</b> and walk the night market — read our <a href="{ART:nightmarket}">night market guide</a> for what to eat.</li>\n<li>Finish with a warm drink at a café in the centre before you ride back.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For a daytime version of this, see <a href="{ART:lt-ev-city}">exploring Da Lat by electric scooter</a>.</p>\n<h2>What to avoid at night</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Dark forest and mountain roads outside the city — keep evening rides in the lit centre.</li>\n<li>Long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road up Lang Biang are not for electric scooters at any time; rent a Honda Air Blade 125cc for those trips.</li>\n<li>Max 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding — even after dinner — and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law. Vietnam drives on the right.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Return before 21:00</h2>\n<p>A rental day runs from pickup until 21:00, so plan to bring the scooter back on time or message us if you are renting for more days. All conditions are in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter for your evening</h2>\n<p>150,000 VND per day, free helmet, phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. See the scooters on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a>, or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
