C["articles"]['flowers'] = ('flower-villages-farms-da-lat', 'Flower Villages and Farms Around Da Lat by Motorbike | Van Phu', 'Flower Villages and Flower Farms Around Da Lat: A Motorbike Guide', 'Visit the flower villages and farms around Da Lat by motorbike: Van Thanh, Thai Phien, hydrangea fields, the city flower garden — distances, best time and etiquette.', "Da Lat is called the city of flowers for a reason: whole villages around it grow roses, chrysanthemums and hydrangeas in greenhouses and open fields. Here's how to visit them by scooter, when to go and how to behave on working farms.", 'friends-air-blade-van-phu-gate', '\n<h2>Why Da Lat grows so many flowers</h2>\n<p>The cool highland climate lets farmers grow flowers all year round. Since the French period, villages around the city have specialised in flowers, and today a large share of the cut flowers sold in Vietnam\'s big cities comes from Da Lat. From the hills you will see the white roofs of greenhouses (nhà kính) everywhere — most of them are flower and vegetable farms.</p>\n<h2>Van Thanh flower village — the closest</h2>\n<p>Van Thanh (làng hoa Vạn Thành) is only about 2–3 km west of the centre, close to Domaine de Marie church. It is one of the oldest flower villages in Da Lat, with small lanes between greenhouses of roses, chrysanthemums, gerberas and other cut flowers. Ride slowly through the lanes, look in from the road and, if a family is working outside, ask with a smile before you take photos. It combines well with a visit to the <a href="{ART:domainedemarie}">Domaine de Marie pink church</a>.</p>\n<h2>Thai Phien flower village — the biggest</h2>\n<p>Thai Phien (làng hoa Thái Phiên) lies about 5–6 km northeast of the centre. It is a large farming area on gentle slopes, known for chrysanthemums, roses and many other flowers grown both in greenhouses and in open beds. The roads here are quieter than in town and the views over the valley are lovely in the morning light.</p>\n<h2>Hydrangea fields and photo farms</h2>\n<p>Hydrangeas (cẩm tú cầu) grow very well in Da Lat. Several farms, especially in the Trai Mat direction east of the city (about 7–10 km), have opened their hydrangea fields to visitors. These photo farms usually charge a small entrance fee, which goes to the family that looks after the flowers — check it at the gate. On the way you can also stop at Linh Phuoc Pagoda.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-bui-thi-xuan-street-dalat}\n<h2>The city flower garden</h2>\n<p>If you want flowers without leaving the centre, the Da Lat Flower Garden (Vườn hoa thành phố) sits on the northern shore of Xuan Huong Lake. It is a landscaped park with flower beds, greenhouses and garden paths — easy to reach on any bike, including an electric scooter. There is an entrance ticket.</p>\n<h2>When to go</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Time of day:</b> early morning is best — soft light, fewer people and farmers at work. By late morning greenhouses get hot.</li>\n<li><b>Season:</b> flowers are grown all year, but the dry season (roughly December–April) gives sunny days and dry lanes. Late October and November also bring wild sunflowers along the roads — see our <a href="{ART:wildsunflower}">wild sunflower guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>December:</b> Da Lat\'s Flower Festival usually takes place around the end of the year; check the official dates before you plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Etiquette on working farms</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Most greenhouses are private workplaces: don\'t walk in without permission and never pick flowers.</li>\n<li>Don\'t touch plastic covers or irrigation pipes, and stay on paths.</li>\n<li>Buy a bunch of flowers from the farm if you\'d like a souvenir — it is the nicest way to say thank you.</li>\n<li>Don\'t fly drones over people\'s land without asking.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Riding tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Village lanes are narrow and sometimes muddy after rain — ride slowly and watch for small trucks loading flowers.</li>\n<li>Park fully off the road before taking photos, never on a bend.</li>\n<li>A Honda Vision 110cc is perfect for these short rides; the Air Blade 125cc is more comfortable for two people on hills.</li>\n<li>No alcohol when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>More directions around the city are on our page <a href="{ROUTES}">What to See in Da Lat by Motorbike: 5 Routes</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent a scooter for your flower ride</h2>\n<p>All our bikes — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc and electric scooters — cost 150,000 VND per day, with no cash deposit (you leave your passport or ID card), a helmet and a phone holder included. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver the bike free of charge to hotels in central Da Lat. Message Van Phu on WhatsApp and we\'ll suggest a flower loop for your morning.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-caudat-early'] = ('motorbike-rental-cau-dat-tea-hills-sunrise', 'Motorbike Rental for a Sunrise Ride to the Cau Dat Tea Hills | Van Phu', 'Renting a Motorbike for a Sunrise Ride to the Cau Dat Tea Hills', 'Renting a motorbike for a sunrise ride to the Cau Dat tea hills: which bike, when to pick it up (shop opens 4:30 am), fuel, warm clothes and how rental days are counted.', "Watching sunrise over the Cau Dat tea hills means leaving Da Lat in the dark. Here's how to rent a motorbike for that dawn ride: the right bike, the best pick-up time, fuel and what to wear.", 'van-phu-shop-early-pickup-air-blade', '\n<h2>A rental plan built around sunrise</h2>\n<p>The Cau Dat tea hills are about 20–25 km east of the centre, roughly 40–60 minutes by scooter. To arrive in the soft light before sunrise, most riders leave between about 4:30 and 4:45. That timing decides everything about your motorbike rental for the Cau Dat sunrise ride: when you collect the bike, which one you choose and how much fuel you carry. The route, the season for clouds and photo tips are in our <a href="{ART:caudat}">Cau Dat tea hills sunrise guide</a> — this article covers the rental side.</p>\n<h2>Which bike for Cau Dat</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc — our recommendation.</b> The round trip is about 50 km with long, gentle climbs. The Air Blade has more power for hills and two riders, and it is more comfortable on a cold, dark road.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc:</b> fine for one experienced rider who travels light.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter: not recommended for this trip.</b> One battery lasts about 25–30 km and each scooter comes with 2 batteries (about 50–60 km in total), but long climbs drain them fast and leave little margin. Keep electric scooters for the city.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When to pick up the bike</h2>\n<p>You have two easy options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>At 4:30 am, when we open.</b> Message us the evening before with your pick-up time and the bike you want. It will be ready; the paperwork takes a few minutes (passport or ID card, no cash deposit), you take a short test ride and we film the bike\'s condition together. A rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00, so this counts as one day.</li>\n<li><b>The day before.</b> Leaving straight from your hotel is more relaxed, but remember that each rental day ends at 21:00 — a bike collected the evening before counts as two days. If you are renting for 2 days or more anyway, we deliver free of charge to hotels in central Da Lat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>More about our opening time is in <a href="{ART:lt-early}">early-opening motorbike rental in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:van-phu-guest-air-blade-thumbs-up}\n<h2>Fuel: fill up the evening before</h2>\n<p>Gas scooters come with 1 litre of fuel — enough to reach a petrol station, but not a comfortable margin for a 50 km dawn ride in the hills. Some petrol stations are not open yet at 4:30, so fill the tank the evening before. If you pick up at dawn, tell us and we\'ll point you to the nearest station on your way out of town.</p>\n<h2>What to bring on the bike</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A warm jacket, gloves and long trousers — before dawn it is much colder than in the centre, and the wind on the road makes it feel colder still.</li>\n<li>A raincoat under the seat and closed shoes for muddy paths between the tea rows.</li>\n<li>A charged phone with the route saved offline, and a small power bank. Every bike has a phone holder.</li>\n<li>Helmets are free — ask for the right size for both riders.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Riding in the dark</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Check the headlight, brake light and indicators during the test ride.</li>\n<li>Ride slowly in fog, keep to the right and watch for early vegetable trucks.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Book your sunrise bike</h2>\n<p>Every bike costs 150,000 VND per day, with a helmet and a phone holder included. Send us your date and pick-up time on WhatsApp, Zalo or Telegram, or see all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. Want clouds too? Read our <a href="{ART:cloudhunt}">cloud hunting guide</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-rain'] = ('electric-scooter-rental-da-lat-rain', 'Electric Scooter Rental in Da Lat in the Rain: Safety Tips | Van Phu', 'Electric Scooter Rental in Da Lat in the Rain: What You Need to Know', 'Renting an electric scooter in Da Lat in the rain: slippery hills, braking, no deep water, raincoats, battery and parking — practical tips for the rainy season.', 'Afternoon showers are part of life in Da Lat, especially in the rainy season. An electric scooter is still a pleasant way to get around in wet weather — if you know how to ride it on slippery hills and when to wait.', 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>Rain in Da Lat: what to expect</h2>\n<p>The rainy season runs roughly from May to November. Mornings are often dry and showers usually come in the afternoon, sometimes heavy for an hour and then gone. So the simplest plan when you rent an electric scooter in Da Lat in the rain season is to ride in the morning and keep the afternoon flexible. Our <a href="{ART:rainyday}">rainy day guide</a> has ideas for what to do while it pours.</p>\n<h2>Can you ride an electric scooter in the rain?</h2>\n<p>Yes — riding in normal rain is fine. What matters is how you ride and where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Never ride through deep water.</b> After a heavy downpour some low streets flood for a short time. Turn around or wait — this is one of our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Slow down on hills.</b> Da Lat\'s streets go up and down all the time, and wet asphalt, painted lines and steel covers are slippery.</li>\n<li><b>Brake early and gently,</b> using both brakes together, and avoid sudden turns.</li>\n<li><b>Keep more distance</b> from the vehicle in front — stopping takes longer on a wet road.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>What to wear</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A good raincoat or poncho; a two-piece rain suit is best for riding. Cheap ponchos are sold in shops all over the centre.</li>\n<li>Closed shoes or sandals with grip — flip-flops slip on wet ground.</li>\n<li>A light jacket under the raincoat: rain makes Da Lat feel colder.</li>\n<li>Keep your phone and wallet in a plastic bag.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Visibility</h2>\n<p>In heavy rain drivers see less. Ride with your headlight on, use your indicators early and stay on the right. Electric scooters are quiet, so take extra care near pedestrians with umbrellas who may not hear you. Clean your mirrors before you set off again.</p>\n<h2>Battery and range in wet weather</h2>\n<p>Your range stays the same as on a dry day: one battery lasts about 25–30 km, and each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 50–60 km in total. Hills and two riders use more power, rain or not. Don\'t open the battery compartment in the rain, and if you need a fresh battery, bring the scooter to our shop — a swap costs 20,000 VND.</p>\n<h2>Where to ride when it\'s wet</h2>\n<p>Stay in the city: Xuan Huong Lake, the market area and nearby cafés are all close together, so you can ride a few minutes, then sit out a shower over coffee. Long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road up Lang Biang are not for electric scooters at any time — and even less in the rain. For trips like that, rent a Honda Air Blade 125cc. Easy city loops are in <a href="{ART:lt-ev-city}">exploring Da Lat by electric scooter</a>.</p>\n<h2>Parking in the rain</h2>\n<p>Use a guarded parking lot (gửi xe), under a roof if possible, and lock the scooter. Wipe the seat before you sit down — or keep a small cloth under the seat.</p>\n<h2>Safety basics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Max 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg; both wear helmets.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>If a storm is very heavy, simply stop somewhere safe and wait — showers in Da Lat rarely last long.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter, rain or shine</h2>\n<p>150,000 VND per day, with a free helmet and phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. See the scooters on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. Message us on WhatsApp if you want to know what the weather is doing today.</p>\n')
