C["articles"]['thingstodo'] = ('things-to-do-central-da-lat', 'Things to Do in Central Da Lat: Xuan Huong Lake, Station, Crazy House, Pink Church | Van Phu', 'Things to Do in Central Da Lat: Xuan Huong Lake, the Railway Station, Crazy House and Domaine de Marie', 'The best things to do in central Da Lat: Xuan Huong Lake, Lam Vien Square, Da Lat Railway Station, Crazy House, Domaine de Marie and the market — a relaxed one-day plan by scooter.', "Many of Da Lat's best-known sights sit within a few kilometres of each other around Xuan Huong Lake. Here's what each one is like, how to link them in an easy day and a few tips for parking and timing.", 'white-honda-vision-sidewalk-dalat', '\n<h2>Why the centre is worth a full day</h2>\n<p>Da Lat is built on hills around Xuan Huong Lake, and most of the classic sights are only 1–3 km apart. On foot the slopes get tiring quickly; with a scooter you can link everything below in one relaxed day, stop for coffee whenever you like and still be back at the market for dinner.</p>\n<h2>Xuan Huong Lake and Lam Vien Square</h2>\n<p>The crescent-shaped lake is the heart of the city. A walk or slow ride around it is about 5 km, with pine trees, flower beds and views of the hills. Early morning is the nicest time: locals jog and do exercises by the water and the light is soft. On the south side, Lam Vien Square is known for its two big glass buildings shaped like an artichoke and a wild sunflower bud — a popular photo spot, and in the evening a meeting place for young people.</p>\n<h2>Da Lat Railway Station</h2>\n<p>About 1.5 km east of the lake, the station was built by the French in the 1930s and is famous for its Art Deco design with three pointed roofs that echo the Lang Biang peaks. Old carriages and a steam locomotive stand on the platform for photos. A short tourist train runs from here to Trai Mat village and the mosaic-covered Linh Phuoc Pagoda; check the timetable and tickets at the station on the day.</p>\n<h2>Crazy House</h2>\n<p>Officially Hang Nga Guesthouse, Crazy House was designed by Vietnamese architect Đặng Việt Nga and looks like a giant tree house: twisting staircases, cave-like rooms and bridges between towers. It\'s about 1.5 km southwest of the lake. There is an entrance ticket; the walkways are narrow and high in places, so wear good shoes and keep a hand on small children.</p>\n<h2>Domaine de Marie (the pink church)</h2>\n<p>On a small hill west of the market, this pink church and convent from the early 1940s is free to enter and one of the most photographed buildings in town. Cover shoulders and knees, keep quiet and avoid Mass times. Opening hours, history and the best angles are in our <a href="{ART:domainedemarie}">Domaine de Marie guide</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-cafe-garden-dalat}\n<h2>More stops on the way</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Da Lat Cathedral</b> (the "Rooster Church") on Tran Phu street, named after the weathervane on its tower.</li>\n<li><b>Bao Dai\'s Summer Palace</b> (Dinh III), the 1930s residence of Vietnam\'s last emperor, set in a pine garden south of the centre.</li>\n<li><b>Da Lat Flower Garden</b> on the north side of the lake — easy to combine with a coffee by the water.</li>\n<li><b>Da Lat Market</b> — fruit, jams, artichoke tea and dried snacks by day; street food in the evening. Our <a href="{ART:nightmarket}">night market guide</a> tells you what to try.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A relaxed one-day plan</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Morning:</b> lake loop and Lam Vien Square, then the railway station (and the train to Trai Mat if it fits).</li>\n<li><b>Late morning:</b> Domaine de Marie, then lunch near the market.</li>\n<li><b>Afternoon:</b> Crazy House and Bao Dai\'s Summer Palace; a café with a view when the afternoon shower comes.</li>\n<li><b>Evening:</b> the night market and a walk by the lake.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For the best photo light at each place, see <a href="{ART:photospots}">Da Lat photo spots by motorbike</a> and our <a href="{PM}">photo spot map</a>. For trips outside the city, try the <a href="{ROUTES}">motorbike routes around Da Lat</a>.</p>\n<h2>Practical tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Park only in guarded lots (gửi xe) — most sights have one at the gate; keep the ticket.</li>\n<li>Streets around the market are one-way and busy in the evening: ride slowly and keep to the right.</li>\n<li>Bring a light jacket and a raincoat — afternoons can turn cool and wet quickly.</li>\n<li>No alcohol when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Getting around the centre with Van Phu</h2>\n<p>Our shop is in the centre, a short ride from all these sights. An electric scooter is quiet and ideal for these short hops; a Honda Vision or Air Blade is fine too. Every bike is 150,000 VND per day, with a free helmet and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. Message us on WhatsApp and we\'ll help you plan the order of your stops.</p>\n')
