C["articles"]['lt-ev-eco'] = ('eco-friendly-electric-scooter-rental-da-lat', 'Eco-Friendly Scooter Rental in Da Lat: Quiet Electric Rides | Van Phu', 'Eco-Friendly Scooter Rental in Da Lat: Travel Green by Electric Scooter', 'Eco-friendly scooter rental in Da Lat: why a quiet electric scooter suits the city, what it changes for your trip, battery facts and simple tips for travelling green.', "Da Lat is loved for its pine hills, cool air and flowers. If you'd like to see it with a lighter footprint, an electric scooter is an easy way to do it: quiet, no exhaust on the street and no petrol stops. Here's what to expect.", 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>Why green travel fits Da Lat</h2>\n<p>Da Lat sits high on the plateau, surrounded by pine forests, flower farms and lakes. Much of its charm is quiet: misty mornings by Xuan Huong Lake, birds in the pines, cafés in gardens. More and more travellers want to enjoy that without adding noise and fumes to the narrow streets of the centre. An eco-friendly scooter rental in Da Lat is one small, practical step.</p>\n<h2>What an electric scooter changes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>No exhaust on the street.</b> You don\'t breathe petrol fumes at red lights, and neither do the people walking next to you.</li>\n<li><b>Very quiet.</b> You hear the city instead of an engine — pleasant around the lake, in residential lanes and early in the morning.</li>\n<li><b>No petrol stations.</b> You start with full batteries; there is nothing to fill up during the day.</li>\n<li><b>Light and easy</b> for short city rides between sights, markets and cafés.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Battery: how far you can go</h2>\n<p>One battery lasts about 25–30 km; each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 50–60 km in total. That\'s plenty for a full day around the city. If you need more, bring the scooter to our shop and we swap in a full battery for 20,000 VND. Hills and two riders use more power, so head back when about a third is left.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Where an electric scooter is the right choice</h2>\n<p>The centre is perfect: the lake, Lam Vien Square, the market, the railway station, Domaine de Marie, flower gardens and dozens of cafés, all a few kilometres apart. See <a href="{ART:lt-ev-city}">exploring the city by electric scooter</a> and <a href="{ART:lt-ev-cafes}">café hopping by electric scooter</a> for ideas. Electric scooters are not made for long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road up Lang Biang — for those trips choose a Honda Air Blade 125cc. Maximum 2 riders, with a combined weight under 140 kg.</p>\n<h2>Simple tips for travelling green in Da Lat</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Bring a refillable water bottle and say no to extra plastic bags at the market.</li>\n<li>Take your rubbish back with you from viewpoints, lakes and pine forests.</li>\n<li>Stay on paths at flower farms and tea hills, and don\'t pick flowers.</li>\n<li>Combine riding with walking: park in a guarded lot (gửi xe) and explore the market area and lakeside on foot.</li>\n<li>Eat local: Da Lat\'s vegetables, fruit and coffee are grown right around the city.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Riding safely</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Electric scooters are quiet — pedestrians may not hear you, so ride slowly near crowds and use the horn lightly when needed.</li>\n<li>In the rain, slow down: Da Lat\'s hills get slippery.</li>\n<li>Helmets on for both riders, no alcohol when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Please read our short <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a> before you ride.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter from Van Phu</h2>\n<p>Our electric scooters cost 150,000 VND per day, like every bike, with helmets and a phone holder included and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. See them on the <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. Message us on WhatsApp and we\'ll have a scooter with full batteries ready for you.</p>\n')
