C["articles"]['strawberries'] = ('strawberry-farms-da-lat', 'Strawberry Farms in Da Lat & Local Produce: A Guide by Motorbike | Van Phu', 'Strawberry Farms in Da Lat and Local Produce: What to Taste and Where to Ride', 'Strawberry farms in Da Lat: how pick-your-own visits work, the best season, where the farms are, plus local produce to try — artichoke, avocado, persimmons, coffee.', "Da Lat is Vietnam's strawberry town. The cool highland air lets farmers grow them almost all year, and visiting a farm is one of the easiest half-day rides from the centre. Here's how it works and what else the plateau grows.", 'friends-air-blade-van-phu-gate', '\n<h2>Why Da Lat grows strawberries</h2>\n<p>At around 1,500 m above sea level, Da Lat has spring-like weather for most of the year. That is rare in Vietnam, and it\'s why strawberries (dâu tây), vegetables, flowers and temperate fruit grow here so well. Most strawberries today are grown in greenhouses or under plastic roofs, often in raised beds or hanging rows, which keeps the fruit clean and protects it from heavy rain.</p>\n<h2>How a strawberry farm visit works</h2>\n<p>Many family farms around the city welcome visitors. The usual format is simple:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>You walk through the greenhouse, take photos and pick strawberries yourself.</li>\n<li>You pay for what you pick, usually by weight — ask the price before you start, as it varies by farm and season.</li>\n<li>Some farms also sell jam, dried strawberries, strawberry wine or smoothies, and a few have a small café corner.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pick gently by the stem, don\'t step into the beds and always ask before tasting. It\'s a working farm, not a theme park — a smile and a "cảm ơn" (thank you) go a long way.</p>\n<h2>When to go</h2>\n<p>You can find strawberries in Da Lat in most months, but the fruit is usually sweetest and most plentiful in the dry season, roughly December to April. In the rainy months farms still have fruit in their greenhouses, just a little less of it. Mornings are the nicest time: cooler air, soft light for photos and the freshest picking before the afternoon showers.</p>\n<h2>Where the farms are</h2>\n<p>Strawberry farms are scattered around the outskirts rather than in one place. Two easy directions by motorbike:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>North, towards Lang Biang and Lac Duong</b> — roughly 10–12 km from the centre, with many greenhouses and farm signs along the way. Combine it with a view of Lang Biang Mountain.</li>\n<li><b>The farming valleys east and northeast of the centre</b> — the areas towards Thai Phien and Trai Mat, about 5–8 km away, where flower and vegetable farms sit side by side. See our guide to <a href="{ART:flowers}">flower villages and farms</a> for this side of town.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Look for hand-painted signs saying "vườn dâu" (strawberry garden) or "dâu tây tự hái" (pick your own strawberries). Farms open and close and change from season to season, so ask us at the shop which ones are good right now.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-cafe-garden-dalat}\n<h2>Other local produce worth tasting</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Artichoke (atiso)</b> — dried artichoke tea is Da Lat\'s classic gift and a nice warm drink on a chilly evening.</li>\n<li><b>Avocado (bơ)</b> — the highlands grow excellent avocados; try them in a smoothie or as avocado ice cream.</li>\n<li><b>Persimmons (hồng)</b> — towards the end of the year you\'ll see wind-dried persimmons (hồng treo gió), soft and sweet.</li>\n<li><b>Mulberries and passion fruit</b> — often sold as syrups, jams and juices.</li>\n<li><b>Coffee</b> — the plateau around Da Lat grows arabica; many cafés serve local beans.</li>\n<li><b>Fresh vegetables</b> — Da Lat supplies much of southern Vietnam with greens, carrots and cabbages; you\'ll taste the difference in a hotpot.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The easiest place to see all of this in one go is Da Lat Market and the <a href="{ART:nightmarket}">night market</a> next to it, where stalls sell fresh fruit, jams, teas and dried snacks. For more dishes, see <a href="{ART:food}">what to eat in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n<h2>Tips for the ride</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Farm lanes can be narrow, steep or muddy after rain — ride slowly and park where you won\'t block the farmer\'s truck.</li>\n<li>Bring small cash; farms rarely take cards.</li>\n<li>Strawberries bruise easily: carry them in a box under the seat, not in a plastic bag on the handlebar.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding (save the strawberry wine for the evening), and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Ride out with Van Phu</h2>\n<p>A farm visit is a perfect half-day trip. Our Honda Vision 110cc is great for nearby farms, the Honda Air Blade 125cc is more comfortable on the hills towards Lang Biang, and every bike is 150,000 VND per day with a helmet and phone holder included and no cash deposit. Message us on WhatsApp or Zalo and we\'ll suggest a farm loop for the season — and keep a box under the seat for your strawberries.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-multiday'] = ('rent-motorbike-da-lat-for-a-week', 'Rent a Motorbike in Da Lat for a Week or Longer | Van Phu', 'Rent a Motorbike in Da Lat for a Week or Longer: How It Works', 'Planning to rent a motorbike in Da Lat for a week or longer? How days are counted, the fixed 150,000 VND/day price, free hotel delivery, extending and tips.', 'Staying in Da Lat for a week, a month or as a digital nomad? A bike of your own for the whole stay is the simplest way to live here. This is how a long rental works with Van Phu — no surprises.', 'vision-air-blade-villa-delivery-dalat', '\n<h2>Why rent for a week or more</h2>\n<p>Da Lat rewards slow travel. With a week you can do the city at your own pace, then add the Cau Dat tea hills at sunrise, Tuyen Lam Lake, waterfalls, flower villages and quiet cafés on rainy afternoons. When you rent a motorbike in Da Lat for a week or longer, you pick it up once and simply ride every day — no booking each morning, no waiting for a car.</p>\n<h2>How the days are counted</h2>\n<p>A rental day runs from pickup until 21:00, and every following day ends at 21:00 too. So a 7-day rental picked up on Monday morning is returned by 21:00 the next Sunday.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pick up Monday 9:00 → return by Sunday 21:00 = 7 days.</li>\n<li>Pick up on the 1st → return by 21:00 on the 30th = 30 days.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our shop opens at 4:30 am, so you can collect the bike early and use all of day one.</p>\n<h2>Price: simple and fixed</h2>\n<p>Every bike costs 150,000 VND per day, the same price for one day or thirty, so a week is 1,050,000 VND. There are no hidden fees and no extra charge for long rentals. Every bike comes with a helmet and a phone holder; gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel and you refuel as you go. There is no cash deposit — you leave your passport (or ID card for Vietnamese guests) for the duration of the rental.</p>\n<h2>Free delivery to your hotel</h2>\n<p>For rentals of 2 days or more, we deliver the bike free of charge to hotels in central Da Lat. Send us the hotel name and the time, and we bring the bike to you. At handover we do a short test ride together and film the bike\'s condition, so both sides know how it looked on day one.</p>\n{PHOTO:van-phu-guest-air-blade-thumbs-up}\n<h2>Which bike for a long stay</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc</b> — the best all-rounder for a week: more power for the passes, Lang Biang and Cau Dat, and comfortable for two.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc</b> — light, easy and economical if you mostly ride around the city and to nearby sights.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter</b> — quiet for city life; one battery lasts about 25–30 km and each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 50–60 km in total, with battery swaps at our shop for 20,000 VND. Not for long steep passes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Extending or returning early</h2>\n<p>Plans change on long trips. If you want to keep the bike longer, message us before 21:00 on your last day and we\'ll extend the rental at the same daily price. If you leave Da Lat sooner, tell us when you\'d like to return the bike.</p>\n<h2>Tips for a week on two wheels</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Do a quick check every few days: tyres, brakes and lights. If anything feels wrong, stop and message us.</li>\n<li>Keep a raincoat under the seat and a warm layer for early mornings — Da Lat is cool all year.</li>\n<li>Use guarded parking (gửi xe) at markets and busy sights and always take the key.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>Planning long rides far from Da Lat? Tell us before you book so we can suggest the right bike.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Only staying a weekend? See <a href="{ART:lt-2days}">renting a scooter in Dalat for 2 days</a>, and the <a href="{ART:lt-procedure}">step-by-step rental guide</a> for how pickup works.</p>\n<h2>Book your week</h2>\n<p>Message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat with your dates, the bike you\'d like and your hotel. All bikes and conditions are on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-parking'] = ('electric-scooter-parking-da-lat', 'Electric Scooter Parking in Da Lat: Where and How to Park | Van Phu', 'Electric Scooter Parking in Da Lat: Guarded Lots, Tickets and Simple Rules', 'Electric scooter parking in Da Lat: how guarded lots (gửi xe) and tickets work, where to park at the market, cafés and sights, and how to keep the scooter safe.', "Renting an electric scooter in Da Lat is easy — knowing where to leave it is the part travellers ask about most. Here's how parking works in the city, in plain words.", 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '\n<h2>How parking works in Da Lat: "gửi xe"</h2>\n<p>In Vietnam you rarely park a scooter just anywhere on the street. Near markets, sights, shops and many cafés there is a guarded parking area — look for a sign saying <b>"Giữ xe"</b> or <b>"Gửi xe"</b>. An attendant shows you where to put the scooter, gives you a paper or plastic ticket and keeps an eye on the bikes. You pay a small fee in cash, usually when you leave. Keep the ticket safe: you need it to take the scooter out.</p>\n<p>Electric scooter parking in Da Lat works exactly the same way as for gas scooters — the attendants treat them like any other bike.</p>\n<h2>Where to park around the city</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Da Lat Market and the night market</b> — there are several guarded lots around the market area. In the evening it gets busy, so arrive a little early and walk the last part.</li>\n<li><b>Xuan Huong Lake and Lam Vien Square</b> — use the guarded areas rather than leaving the scooter on the pavement.</li>\n<li><b>Cafés</b> — many cafés have their own parking with a staff member who helps you; some put a ticket or a number on the seat. If a café has no parking, ask where the nearest "gửi xe" is.</li>\n<li><b>Sights and ticketed attractions</b> — the railway station, Domaine de Marie, flower gardens and similar places usually have parking at the entrance.</li>\n<li><b>Your hotel</b> — ask at reception where to leave the scooter overnight; many hotels and homestays have a parking space or bring bikes inside at night.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Simple rules to keep the scooter safe</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Switch the scooter off and always take the key with you.</li>\n<li>Use the steering lock when you park on the street for a minute.</li>\n<li>Don\'t leave phones, bags or valuables in the storage box or on the phone holder.</li>\n<li>Don\'t block sidewalks, gates or doors, and never park where a sign says "Cấm đỗ xe" (no parking).</li>\n<li>Avoid parking in deep puddles or right under a gutter in the rain.</li>\n<li>Take a quick photo of where you parked if the area is new to you — lanes in Da Lat can look alike.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Plan your stops around the battery</h2>\n<p>Parking is also a good moment to check the battery. One battery lasts about 25–30 km; each scooter comes with 2 batteries, about 50–60 km in total. If you need more for the day, come back to our shop and we swap in a full battery for 20,000 VND. Electric scooters are made for city riding and nearby sights — for long steep passes like Prenn, Mimosa or Lang Biang choose a Honda Air Blade 125cc. Maximum 2 riders, with a combined weight under 140 kg. All other conditions are in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n<p>Looking for ideas where to ride? Try <a href="{ART:lt-ev-city}">exploring the city by electric scooter</a> or <a href="{ART:lt-ev-cafes}">café hopping by electric scooter</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter with Van Phu</h2>\n<p>Our electric scooters cost 150,000 VND per day, like every bike, with helmets and a phone holder included and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. See them on the <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. Message us on WhatsApp or Zalo and we\'ll have a scooter with full batteries ready for you. No alcohol when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</p>\n')
