C["articles"]['where-to-stay'] = ('where-to-stay-in-da-lat', 'Where to Stay in Da Lat: Best Areas Compared for Travellers | Van Phu', 'Where to Stay in Da Lat: Areas Compared (Centre, Quiet Hills, Tuyen Lam, Outskirts)', 'Where to stay in Da Lat: the city centre, quiet hillside streets, Tuyen Lam Lake and farm stays compared — who each area suits and how you get around from it.', 'Da Lat is small, but where you sleep still shapes your trip: how far you walk to dinner, how cold the mornings feel and how easy it is to reach the sights. Here is a simple comparison of the main areas, so you can choose the one that fits your style — without us naming any particular hotel.', 'white-honda-vision-sidewalk-dalat', '\n<h2>How to think about Da Lat\'s areas</h2>\n<p>Most of what visitors want to see sits within a few kilometres of Xuan Huong Lake: the market, the night market, Lam Vien Square, the cathedral, Domaine de Marie and the railway station. Beyond that, pine hills, lakes, flower farms and tea hills spread out in every direction. So the real choice is simple: stay close to the lake and walk a lot, or stay further out for quiet and views and ride in when you need to.</p>\n<h2>1. The city centre (around the market and Xuan Huong Lake)</h2>\n<p><b>Best for:</b> first-time visitors, short stays, people without their own transport.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Restaurants, cafés, the night market and most central sights are within walking distance.</li>\n<li>The widest choice of hotels, guesthouses and hostels in every price range.</li>\n<li>Downsides: busier, more traffic noise in the evening, and some rooms face an inner wall rather than a view.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you only have one or two days, this is usually the easiest base. See our <a href="{ART:nightmarket}">night market guide</a> for evenings in this area.</p>\n<h2>2. Quiet streets on the hills around the centre</h2>\n<p><b>Best for:</b> couples, slow travellers, anyone who wants a view from the window.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Small villas, homestays and boutique places on the hillsides 1–3 km from the lake, often with gardens and pine trees.</li>\n<li>Calmer nights and cooler mornings; many places have terraces looking over the valley.</li>\n<li>Downsides: steep lanes, and walking back up after dinner is tiring — a scooter makes these areas much easier.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-hotel-delivery-dalat}\n<h2>3. Tuyen Lam Lake (about 10 km south)</h2>\n<p><b>Best for:</b> resort stays, families who want space, nature lovers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pine forest, the lake and Truc Lam Monastery close by — very peaceful. Read our <a href="{ART:tuyenlam}">Tuyen Lam Lake guide</a>.</li>\n<li>Downsides: you will ride or take a car into town for most meals and sights, and evenings are quiet.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>4. Outskirts and farm stays (Trai Mat, Lac Duong, the tea hills)</h2>\n<p><b>Best for:</b> travellers who want countryside, sunrise rides and a slower rhythm.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Homestays among flower and vegetable farms east of the city, near Lang Biang to the north, or towards the Cau Dat tea hills.</li>\n<li>Great for early starts to cloud-hunting spots and sunrise viewpoints.</li>\n<li>Downsides: longer distances, fewer restaurants nearby, and some roads are narrow or steep — choose a gas scooter rather than an electric one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Practical tips before you book</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Nights are cool.</b> Da Lat sits around 1,500 m above sea level; check that the room has warm blankets and hot water.</li>\n<li><b>Ask about parking.</b> If you rent a scooter, ask whether the hotel has a space for it overnight.</li>\n<li><b>Look at the map, not just "near the centre".</b> A place 1 km away can be a steep climb.</li>\n<li><b>Arriving by night bus?</b> Many buses arrive very early in the morning; ask if early check-in or luggage storage is possible. Our <a href="{ART:getting}">getting to Da Lat guide</a> has more.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Getting around from wherever you stay</h2>\n<p>Wherever you sleep, a scooter turns Da Lat\'s hills into an easy ride. Van Phu rents the Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc and electric scooters for 150,000 VND a day, with free helmets and a phone holder and no cash deposit. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat — see <a href="{ART:lt-delivery}">motorbike rental with hotel delivery</a>. Staying further out? Message us and we\'ll find the easiest way to get the bike to you. Book on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. No alcohol when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</p>')
C["articles"]['lt-two'] = ('motorbike-rental-da-lat-for-two', 'Motorbike Rental in Da Lat for Two People & Couples | Van Phu', 'Motorbike Rental in Da Lat for Two People: Which Bike, Comfort and Safety', 'Motorbike rental in Da Lat for two people or couples: which scooter to choose for riding two-up, one bike or two, comfort and safety tips on the hills.', 'Travelling as a couple or with a friend? Sharing one scooter is the most common way to explore Da Lat — and the most fun. Here is how to choose the right motorbike rental in Da Lat for two people, and how to make riding together comfortable and safe.', 'group-rental-air-blade-van-phu-morning', '\n<h2>One bike or two?</h2>\n<p>Most couples share one scooter: it\'s cheaper, you can talk on the way, and the passenger is free to take photos and follow the map. Two bikes make sense if both of you are confident riders, if one of you is much heavier or taller, or if you plan long rides and want to swap the tiring part. Our price is per bike, not per person: 150,000 VND a day for any bike, so one shared scooter costs 150,000 VND and two scooters 300,000 VND.</p>\n<h2>Which scooter is best for two people in Da Lat?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc</b> — our top choice for two riders. The stronger engine pulls well on Da Lat\'s hills and on longer trips out of town, such as the tea hills, waterfalls or the passes. More in our <a href="{ART:lt-airblade}">Air Blade rental guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc</b> — light, easy and comfortable for two in the city and on nearby sights. On steep climbs with two people it is slower, so take it calmly. See <a href="{ART:lt-vision}">Honda Vision rental</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter</b> — quiet and lovely for slow city rides, cafés and the lake. Maximum 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg, city and nearby sights only, no long steep passes. Details in <a href="{ART:lt-ev-couples}">electric scooter for two</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:couple-air-blade-dalat-center-street}\n<h2>Comfort for the passenger</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Fold out the passenger footrests and keep both feet on them the whole time.</li>\n<li>Hold the rider\'s waist or the rear grab handle — don\'t lean back without holding on.</li>\n<li>Wear closed shoes and long trousers; mornings and evenings in Da Lat are cool, so bring a jacket for both of you.</li>\n<li>Pack light: a small backpack for the passenger and the storage box under the seat for raincoats and water.</li>\n<li>Stop every hour or so on long rides — sitting behind is more tiring than it looks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Safety when riding two-up on Da Lat\'s hills</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>With two people the bike brakes longer. Ride slower, keep more distance and use both brakes gently, especially downhill.</li>\n<li>The passenger should lean with the rider in corners, not against them, and avoid sudden movements.</li>\n<li>Agree on simple signals — a tap on the shoulder for "stop" or "slow down".</li>\n<li>Both of you wear a helmet, fastened. We give free helmets with every bike — two for two riders.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Booking a motorbike for two in Da Lat</h2>\n<p>Tell us when you message that you\'ll ride two-up, roughly where you plan to go and, if you like, your approximate weights — we\'ll suggest the most suitable bike and adjust the mirrors and tyres before you leave. Before the rental we do a short test ride together and film the bike\'s condition. There is no cash deposit: you leave a passport or ID card. Every bike has a phone holder, and gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. For 2+ days we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat.</p>\n<p>Ready to go? Choose your bike on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> and message us on WhatsApp, Zalo or Telegram.</p>')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-halfday'] = ('half-day-da-lat-electric-scooter-loop', 'Half-Day Da Lat City Loop by Electric Scooter | Van Phu', 'Half-Day Da Lat City Loop by Electric Scooter: Central Sights in One Easy Ride', 'A half-day Da Lat city loop by electric scooter: the lake, flower garden, railway station, Lam Vien Square, cathedral and market — short distances, easy parking.', 'Only have a morning or an afternoon? Central Da Lat is compact, quiet roads link most of the classic sights, and an electric scooter is a calm, easy way to see them. Here is a simple half-day Da Lat city loop by electric scooter that stays in the centre — no long climbs, no rushing.', 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>Why an electric scooter suits a half-day loop</h2>\n<p>The central sights of Da Lat sit close together around Xuan Huong Lake, usually just a few kilometres apart. That is perfect for an electric scooter: it\'s quiet, easy to handle at low speed and simple to park. The whole loop below is short — about 15–20 km including small detours — so the two batteries that come with every scooter are more than enough. Plan around 3–4 hours with stops.</p>\n<h2>The half-day loop, step by step</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>1. Xuan Huong Lake</b> — start with a slow ride along the lake. In the morning the water is often misty; in the afternoon the light is soft. Park in a guarded lot and walk a short stretch of the shore.</li>\n<li><b>2. Da Lat Flower Garden</b> — on the north-east shore of the lake, a short ride away. A relaxed stop for flowers and photos; buy a ticket at the gate.</li>\n<li><b>3. Da Lat Railway Station</b> — east of the lake, with its colourful 1930s building and old carriages. Good for photos.</li>\n<li><b>4. Lam Vien Square</b> — back on the south side of the lake, with the big glass artichoke and wild-sunflower domes and a wide view over the water.</li>\n<li><b>5. Da Lat Cathedral</b> — the "Rooster Church" with its tall tower, a few minutes from the square.</li>\n<li><b>6. Domaine de Marie</b> — the pink church on a small hill with a quiet garden. See our <a href="{ART:domainedemarie}">Domaine de Marie guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>7. Da Lat Market</b> — finish in the centre for a drink, a snack or some local produce. If you chose an afternoon loop, stay on for the night market.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Morning or afternoon?</h2>\n<p>Morning is the calmest: cool air, fewer people at the sights and soft light over the lake. Our shop opens at 4:30, so you can start early. An afternoon loop ends nicely at the market area as the lights come on — just remember that the rental day runs until 21:00. Many sights have entrance tickets and their own hours, so check at the gate.</p>\n<h2>Tips for a smooth ride</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Parking:</b> use guarded "gửi xe" lots near the sights and keep the ticket — see <a href="{ART:lt-ev-parking}">electric scooter parking in Da Lat</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Hills:</b> even the centre has short slopes. Ride steadily uphill and brake gently on the way down.</li>\n<li><b>Stay central:</b> this loop is built for the city. For long steep passes like Prenn, Mimosa or Lang Biang, choose the Honda Air Blade 125cc instead.</li>\n<li><b>Two riders:</b> maximum 2 people with a combined weight under 140 kg.</li>\n<li><b>Battery:</b> if you add extra detours and need more, come back to the shop and we swap in a full battery for 20,000 VND. How far the batteries go is explained in <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">our battery guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Traffic:</b> Vietnam drives on the right; electric scooters are very quiet, so watch for pedestrians who may not hear you. No alcohol when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Want more ideas? Read <a href="{ART:lt-ev-city}">exploring the city by electric scooter</a> and our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter for your half day</h2>\n<p>Van Phu\'s electric scooters cost 150,000 VND per day, like every bike, with free helmets and a phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. We do a short test ride and film the scooter\'s condition together before you go. See the scooters on the <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>, then message us on WhatsApp or Zalo.</p>')
