C["articles"]['kids'] = ('da-lat-with-kids', 'Da Lat with Kids: Family Travel Tips and Easy Outings | Van Phu', 'Da Lat with Kids: Easy Outings, Cool Weather and Family Travel Tips', 'Da Lat with kids: child-friendly places in and around the city, how to plan short days, what to pack for the cool weather, food children like and how to get around safely as a family.', "Cool air, flowers everywhere, short distances and plenty to touch, taste and ride — Da Lat is one of the easiest places in Vietnam to travel with children. Here's how to plan relaxed family days without long, tiring rides.", 'vision-air-blade-villa-delivery-dalat', '\n<h2>Why Da Lat works well for families</h2>\n<p>Da Lat sits at about 1,500 m, so it is cool all year — no heavy heat for small children, and the city centre is compact. Most family favourites are within a few kilometres of Xuan Huong Lake, which means short hops and lots of breaks instead of long transfers. The flip side: mornings and evenings are chilly, and the streets are hilly, so plan gentle days.</p>\n<h2>Child-friendly places in the city</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Xuan Huong Lake</b> — a flat walk or ride around the water, swan pedal boats and plenty of space to run in the morning.</li>\n<li><b>The city flower garden</b> by the lake — colourful, shady and easy for little legs.</li>\n<li><b>Lam Vien Square</b> — open space to play, with the big glass flower and artichoke buildings children like to spot.</li>\n<li><b>Da Lat Railway Station</b> — old trains to look at, and the short heritage train to Trai Mat when it runs.</li>\n<li><b>Crazy House</b> — a fairy-tale building with tunnels and stairs; older kids love it, but hold small hands on the high walkways.</li>\n<li><b>The night market</b> — grilled rice paper (bánh tráng nướng), warm soy milk and corn are easy wins with children. More in our <a href="{ART:nightmarket}">night market guide</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-girls-helmets-dalat}\n<h2>Half-day outings just outside the centre</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Datanla Falls</b>, about 5–6 km south — the alpine coaster down to the falls is a highlight for many families (check the age and height rules on site).</li>\n<li><b>Robin Hill cable car</b> to Truc Lam Monastery and Tuyen Lam Lake — a calm ride above the pine forest. See our <a href="{ART:tuyenlam}">Tuyen Lam guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Strawberry farms</b> — picking your own fruit is fun for kids; the best season is roughly the dry months. Details in our <a href="{ART:strawberries}">strawberry farm guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Flower villages</b> such as Van Thanh — greenhouses and gardens to walk through.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keep one main outing per half day, then go back for a rest or a café with a garden. Children tire quickly in the cool air, and an early dinner keeps everyone happy.</p>\n<h2>What to pack</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Warm layers, a light jacket and long trousers — even in the dry season, early mornings are cold.</li>\n<li>A raincoat for each person; afternoon showers are common from about May to November.</li>\n<li>Comfortable shoes with grip for hilly streets and waterfall paths.</li>\n<li>Sun cream and hats — the sun is strong at altitude even when the air feels cool.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Getting around as a family</h2>\n<p>Distances in Da Lat are short, so many families combine walking, Grab cars and scooters. A scooter works well for parents with older children who can sit securely and hold on: one adult and one child per bike, both wearing helmets, riding slowly and only in daylight. For toddlers and very young children, a taxi or Grab car is the safer way to reach sights farther away. Avoid long, steep passes with children on board, and remember: no alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law. Vietnam drives on the right.</p>\n<h2>Renting bikes for a family trip</h2>\n<p>At Văn Phú every bike costs 150,000 VND per day, with no cash deposit — you leave a passport or ID card. Free helmets come with each bike (try them on at the shop so the strap fits), plus a phone holder, and the Honda Vision and Air Blade come with 1 liter of fuel. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat, so you don\'t need to bring the children to the shop. A family of four usually takes two bikes. See the bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> and message us with your dates and the number of riders.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-backpacker'] = ('motorbike-rental-da-lat-backpackers', 'Motorbike Rental in Da Lat for Backpackers & Budget Travellers | Van Phu', 'Motorbike Rental in Da Lat for Backpackers: A Budget-Friendly Way to See the Highlands', 'Motorbike rental in Da Lat for backpackers and budget travellers: one fixed daily price, no cash deposit, sharing a bike, weekly rental, fuel tips and how to keep the trip affordable.', "On a backpacker budget, your own bike is often the cheapest way to see Da Lat: waterfalls, viewpoints and cafés are spread out, and a full day of taxis adds up fast. Here's how motorbike rental in Da Lat works for backpackers — with no surprises.", 'white-honda-vision-sidewalk-dalat', '\n<h2>Why a scooter suits a budget trip</h2>\n<p>Da Lat\'s centre is walkable, but most of the good stuff — waterfalls, tea hills, flower villages, pine-forest lakes — is a few kilometres out of town. A day of separate rides there and back quickly costs more than a scooter. With your own bike you also choose when to leave, so you can catch the sunrise or skip the crowds without paying for a tour.</p>\n<h2>What you pay, clearly</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>150,000 VND per day</b> for every bike — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter. One fixed price, no "from" prices.</li>\n<li><b>No cash deposit.</b> You leave your passport or ID card, so you don\'t tie up travel money.</li>\n<li><b>Included:</b> helmet, phone holder, and 1 liter of fuel in gas scooters.</li>\n<li><b>Rental day</b> = from pick-up until 21:00. A week (7 days) is 1,050,000 VND — see our tips on <a href="{ART:lt-multiday}">renting for a week or longer</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No hidden extras: we do a test ride and film the bike\'s condition together before you leave, so there are no arguments about scratches when you return it.</p>\n{PHOTO:evening-pickup-van-phu-1-15-5-thong-thien-hoc}\n<h2>Ways backpackers save money</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Share one bike between two.</b> The price is per bike, not per person — two friends on one Air Blade split the day. Read about <a href="{ART:lt-two}">riding as two</a> first.</li>\n<li><b>Plan loops, not zig-zags.</b> Group sights by direction — south for Datanla and Tuyen Lam, east for Trai Mat and Cau Dat — so you use less fuel and time.</li>\n<li><b>Fill up at petrol stations</b>, not roadside bottles: the price is on the pump.</li>\n<li><b>Park in proper parking spots</b> (gửi xe) and keep the ticket — cheap peace of mind.</li>\n<li><b>Eat where locals eat.</b> Markets and small street stalls are good value; our <a href="{ART:budget}">Da Lat trip budget guide</a> has more ideas.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Which bike for a budget traveller?</h2>\n<p>The <b>Honda Vision 110cc</b> is light and easy in town and sips fuel. The <b>Air Blade 125cc</b> is better for two people and for hills and passes. The <b>electric scooter</b> has no fuel cost at all and is great for city rides and cafés; it comes with 2 batteries, and a battery swap at the shop costs 20,000 VND — but it is not for long, steep passes.</p>\n<h2>Arriving on the night bus or staying in a hostel</h2>\n<p>Many backpackers arrive early in the morning by bus. Our shop opens at 4:30 am, so you can pick up a bike instead of waiting for check-in. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels and hostels in central Da Lat; for 1 day, just message us and we\'ll see what\'s possible.</p>\n<h2>Ride smart</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; ride slowly on hills and in the rain.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding — a hostel bar night means walking home. Strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>Keep your helmet strapped on, even for short rides.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Book in one message</h2>\n<p>Send us your dates, which bike you\'d like and where you\'re staying on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat, and we\'ll have it ready. All bikes and contacts are on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> — that\'s the simplest motorbike rental in Da Lat for backpackers who want to spend money on the trip, not on transport.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-faq'] = ('electric-scooter-rental-da-lat-faq', 'Electric Scooter Rental in Da Lat: 10 Common Questions Answered | Van Phu', 'Electric Scooter Rental in Da Lat: 10 Common Questions Answered', "Electric scooter rental in Da Lat FAQ: price, deposit, batteries and swaps, two riders, where you can and can't go, rain, parking, helmets, hotel delivery and opening hours.", 'Thinking about an electric scooter for your Da Lat trip? These are the ten questions guests ask us most often before electric scooter rental in Da Lat — answered briefly, with links if you want the details.', 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '\n<h2>1. How much does an electric scooter cost per day?</h2>\n<p>150,000 VND per day — the same fixed price as our Honda Vision and Air Blade. A rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00.</p>\n<h2>2. Do I need to pay a deposit?</h2>\n<p>No cash deposit. You leave your passport (foreign guests) or ID card (Vietnamese guests) at the shop and get it back when you return the scooter.</p>\n<h2>3. How far can I ride on the batteries?</h2>\n<p>Every scooter comes with 2 batteries, enough for a relaxed day of city riding. Hills, a passenger and cold mornings use more power, so plan to come back when about a third is left. Need more? We swap in a full battery at the shop for 20,000 VND. The details are in our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">electric scooter battery guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>4. Can two people ride one electric scooter?</h2>\n<p>Yes — maximum 2 people with a combined weight under 140 kg. Heavier pairs are more comfortable on the Honda Air Blade 125cc.</p>\n<h2>5. Can I ride to Cau Dat, Lang Biang or over the passes?</h2>\n<p>No. Please avoid long, steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa and the road up Lang Biang. For those trips take the Air Blade, at the same price. The electric scooter is made for the city, the lake, cafés and short trips nearby.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>6. Is it hard to ride if I\'m not used to scooters?</h2>\n<p>It\'s the easiest bike we have: no gears, no clutch, a soft start. Take the free test ride in our quiet lane first. Tips for first-timers are in <a href="{ART:lt-ev-beginners}">electric scooter for beginners</a>.</p>\n<h2>7. Can I ride it in the rain?</h2>\n<p>Short city rides in light rain are fine with a raincoat; in heavy rain, wait under a roof and never ride through deep water. See <a href="{ART:lt-ev-rain}">electric scooter in the rain</a>.</p>\n<h2>8. Where do I park?</h2>\n<p>Use proper parking spots (gửi xe) at markets, sights and cafés and keep the ticket. More in our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-parking}">electric scooter parking guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>9. What is included?</h2>\n<p>A free helmet for each rider, a phone holder and 2 batteries. Before you leave we check the scooter together and film its condition, so everything is clear on return.</p>\n<h2>10. Do you deliver, and when are you open?</h2>\n<p>The shop at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học opens from 4:30 to 21:00. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat; for 1 day, message us and ask.</p>\n<h2>Before you ride</h2>\n<p>An electric scooter is almost silent, so pedestrians may not hear you — ride slowly near markets and use the horn gently. No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law; Vietnam drives on the right. Read all the <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>, see the scooters on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a>, or compare every bike on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. Still have a question about electric scooter rental in Da Lat? Message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat.</p>\n')
