C["articles"]['pinkgrass'] = ('pink-grass-season-da-lat', 'Pink Grass Season in Da Lat (Cỏ Hồng): Where to See It by Motorbike | Van Phu', 'Pink Grass Season in Da Lat: Where to See the Cỏ Hồng Hills by Motorbike', 'Pink grass season in Da Lat: when the cỏ hồng hills turn pink, which directions to ride to see them, the best light for photos and how to visit respectfully and safely.', "For a few weeks at the end of each year, some hillsides around Da Lat turn soft pink. Here's when the pink grass season in Da Lat usually happens, where to look for it by motorbike and how to enjoy it without damaging it.", 'group-rental-air-blade-van-phu-morning', '\n<h2>What "cỏ hồng" is and when it turns pink</h2>\n<p>Cỏ hồng — "pink grass" — is a wild grass that grows on open, sunny hillsides around Da Lat. Most of the year it looks like ordinary green or straw-coloured grass. As the rainy season ends, its feathery flower heads appear and whole slopes take on a pink, sometimes reddish or silvery-violet tone, especially when the low sun shines through them.</p>\n<p>The pink grass season in Da Lat usually runs from about November to December, overlapping with the yellow wild sunflowers (dã quỳ). The exact timing changes every year with the weather, and a hill that was pink last week can fade after strong wind or rain. Before you ride out, message us — we\'re happy to tell you what locals are seeing right now.</p>\n<h2>Where to look by motorbike</h2>\n<p>Pink grass isn\'t one single attraction; it grows on many bare hills on the edges of the city. A few directions where locals often find it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>North towards Lac Duong and Lang Biang</b> (about 10–15 km) — open hills and valleys on the way to the mountain; easy to combine with a coffee stop or the Lang Biang area.</li>\n<li><b>West towards Ta Nung</b> (about 15–20 km) — a quieter, winding road with wide views over hills and valleys. Ride it slowly.</li>\n<li><b>Around Tuyen Lam Lake</b> (south, about 6–10 km) — some open slopes between the pine forests, close to Truc Lam Monastery.</li>\n<li><b>East towards Trai Mat and Cau Dat</b> — farmland and tea hills where patches of pink and yellow appear on the roadsides.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our <a href="{ROUTES}">Da Lat motorbike routes</a> show how these directions fit into a day, and the <a href="{ART:wildsunflower}">wild sunflower guide</a> covers the yellow flowers that bloom at the same time.</p>\n{PHOTO:van-phu-guest-air-blade-thumbs-up}\n<h2>Best time of day for photos</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Early morning</b> — soft light, often mist in the valleys, and fewer people. Our shop opens at 4:30 am, so a sunrise ride is easy; see our <a href="{ART:cloudhunt}">cloud hunting guide</a> for riding before dawn.</li>\n<li><b>Late afternoon</b> — the sun behind the grass makes the pink glow. Leave enough time to ride back before dark.</li>\n<li><b>Midday</b> — the colour looks flatter in strong sun; a good time for coffee instead.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Visit respectfully</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Many pink hills are on private or farm land. If there is a fence, a sign or someone asking for a small entrance fee, respect it — and never cut fences or ride over crops.</li>\n<li>Stay on existing paths. The grass is delicate; walking off-path or lying in it for photos leaves bare patches that take a long time to recover.</li>\n<li>Don\'t pick the grass or take it home — it dries out within hours anyway.</li>\n<li>Take all rubbish with you.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Riding tips and which bike to take</h2>\n<p>Most pink-grass areas are outside the city on hilly roads, some with steep or rough sections. The <b>Honda Air Blade 125cc</b> is the most comfortable choice, especially with a passenger; the Honda Vision 110cc is fine for nearby spots. The electric scooter is best kept for the city — avoid long, steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa and the road up Lang Biang. For more on hill roads, read our guide to <a href="{ART:passes}">Da Lat\'s scenic roads</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pull over completely off the road before taking photos — never stop on a bend.</li>\n<li>Mornings are cold in November and December; bring a warm jacket and gloves.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Ride slowly on gravel and dirt tracks.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rent a bike for the pink grass season</h2>\n<p>Every bike at Văn Phú costs 150,000 VND per day, with a free helmet, a phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. Pick up at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học from 4:30 am, or tell us your plans and we\'ll suggest the best direction for the day. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')

C["articles"]['lt-booking'] = ('book-motorbike-da-lat-online', 'How to Book a Motorbike in Da Lat Online (WhatsApp, Zalo, WeChat, Telegram) | Van Phu', 'How to Book a Motorbike in Da Lat Online: What to Send and What Happens Next', 'Book a motorbike in Da Lat online in one message on WhatsApp, Zalo, WeChat or Telegram: what details to send, how confirmation works, changing your booking and what to bring at pick-up.', "You don't need an app or a website form to book a motorbike in Da Lat online — one chat message is enough. Here's exactly what to send us, how quickly we confirm and what happens when you arrive.", 'honda-air-blade-vision-fleet-van-phu', '\n<h2>Where to message us</h2>\n<p>Choose the app you already use — every channel reaches the shop directly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>WhatsApp:</b> +84 944 081 101 or +84 834 622 149</li>\n<li><b>Zalo:</b> 0944 081 101</li>\n<li><b>Telegram:</b> @vanphudalat</li>\n<li><b>WeChat:</b> vanphudalat</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can write in English or your own language; short messages and translation apps are completely fine.</p>\n<h2>What to send when you book a motorbike in Da Lat online</h2>\n<p>To confirm in one go, put these details in your first message:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Pick-up date and approximate time</b> — the shop opens at 4:30 am and the rental day runs until 21:00.</li>\n<li><b>Number of days</b> — this tells us whether free hotel delivery applies.</li>\n<li><b>Which bike</b> — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter. Not sure? Tell us your plans and how many riders, and we\'ll suggest one.</li>\n<li><b>Number of bikes and riders</b> — and whether you\'ll carry a passenger.</li>\n<li><b>Pick-up at the shop or delivery</b> — if you\'d like delivery, send your hotel name or a map pin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A complete message looks like this: "Hi, 2 Air Blades from 12 Nov, 9 am, 3 days, delivery to [hotel name], 4 riders."</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-hotel-delivery-dalat}\n<h2>How confirmation works</h2>\n<p>We reply in the same chat with the bike, dates and total price, and keep the bike for you. There\'s nothing to pay online and no booking fee: you pay the rental when you collect the bike — 150,000 VND per day for every bike, a fixed price. If you book a few days ahead, we\'ll send a short reminder the day before.</p>\n<h2>Free delivery or pick-up at the shop</h2>\n<p>For rentals of 2 days or more, we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat. For a 1-day rental, just message us and we\'ll see what\'s possible. Otherwise, come to the shop at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học — it\'s on Google Maps. Details are in our article on <a href="{ART:lt-delivery}">hotel delivery</a>.</p>\n<h2>What to bring at pick-up</h2>\n<p>Only your passport (foreign guests) or ID card (Vietnamese guests) — it stays with us during the rental instead of a cash deposit. See <a href="{ART:lt-documents}">what you need to rent a motorbike in Da Lat</a>. Then we do a short test ride and film the bike\'s condition together, and you\'re ready: helmet and phone holder included, and gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. The full process is in <a href="{ART:lt-procedure}">how to rent a motorbike in Da Lat step by step</a>.</p>\n<h2>Changing or cancelling</h2>\n<p>Plans change — a late bus, rain, an extra day. Just write in the same chat as early as you can and we\'ll move the time or date. Want to keep the bike longer? Message us before 21:00 and we\'ll extend the rental.</p>\n<h2>Booking tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>At weekends, on holidays and during the flower festival, book a day or two ahead.</li>\n<li>Arriving on a night bus? Tell us your arrival time — early pick-up from 4:30 am is possible.</li>\n<li>Save our number in your phone, so you can reach us on the road if you need help.</li>\n<li>Remember: no alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ready? Send us one message, or compare all bikes first on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')

C["articles"]['lt-ev-lake'] = ('electric-scooter-xuan-huong-lake-da-lat', 'Electric Scooter Around Xuan Huong Lake & the Flower Garden, Da Lat | Van Phu', 'Riding an Electric Scooter Around Xuan Huong Lake and the Da Lat Flower Garden', 'An easy loop by electric scooter around Xuan Huong Lake: the Da Lat Flower Garden, Lam Vien Square, where to park, the best time to ride and tips for a quiet, relaxed lakeside ride.', "Xuan Huong Lake is the heart of Da Lat, and the road around it is one of the easiest, prettiest rides in town. An electric scooter around Xuan Huong Lake is a relaxed way to see the water, the flower garden and the city's main square in a couple of hours.", 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>Why this loop suits an electric scooter</h2>\n<p>The road around Xuan Huong Lake is short, mostly gentle and full of places to stop — exactly what an electric scooter does best. It\'s quiet, there are no gears to think about, and you can pull over whenever a view catches your eye. The lake is only a short ride from our shop, so you can start the loop within minutes of picking up the scooter.</p>\n<h2>A simple lakeside loop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Start on the south-eastern shore at Lam Vien Square</b> — the big open square with the glass flower-bud and artichoke-shaped buildings. A good first photo stop with the lake behind you.</li>\n<li><b>Ride along the lake</b> past the pine trees and lakeside cafés. Take it slowly: this is a ride for looking around, not for speed.</li>\n<li><b>Stop at the Da Lat Flower Garden</b> at the northern end of the lake — paths full of seasonal flowers, greenhouses and shady corners. There is an entrance ticket and parking at the gate.</li>\n<li><b>Continue along the north shore</b> by the green hills of the golf course and come back around towards the centre and the market area.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The whole circuit is only a few kilometres, so even with stops it fits easily into a morning or an afternoon. Want to go further? Our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-halfday}">half-day city loop by electric scooter</a> adds the railway station, the cathedral and Domaine de Marie.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Where to park</h2>\n<p>Use the paid parking spots (gửi xe) at the Flower Garden, around Lam Vien Square and next to busy cafés, and keep your ticket. Avoid leaving the scooter on the footpath or on a bend of the lake road. More tips in our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-parking}">electric scooter parking guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>Best time to ride</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Early morning</b> — calm water, mist over the lake, locals jogging and doing exercise. Our shop opens at 4:30 am.</li>\n<li><b>Late afternoon</b> — warm light on the water and the hills; a nice time for a lakeside coffee.</li>\n<li><b>Evening</b> — the lights around the lake are pretty, but traffic near the market and the square gets busy, especially at weekends. Ride slowly and watch for pedestrians.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Battery: no need to worry on this loop</h2>\n<p>Each scooter comes with 2 batteries, which is plenty for the lake loop and a few extra stops in town. If you plan a longer day, you can swap in a full battery at the shop for 20,000 VND. Read our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">electric scooter battery guide</a> for how to plan your day.</p>\n<h2>Safety around the lake</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>An electric scooter is almost silent — pedestrians and cyclists may not hear you, so slow down and use the horn gently.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Watch for cars pulling out of parking spots and tour buses near the square.</li>\n<li>Maximum 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg; helmets on for both.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For everything else, read our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter for the lake</h2>\n<p>Our electric scooters cost 150,000 VND per day, with free helmets, a phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. See the scooters on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a>, or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. Message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat, and you can be riding around Xuan Huong Lake within the hour.</p>\n')
