C["articles"]['november'] = ('da-lat-in-november', "Da Lat in November: Weather, What's in Season & What to Pack | Van Phu", "Da Lat in November: Weather, What's in Season and What to Pack", 'Da Lat in November: cool days, the end of the rainy season, wild sunflowers and pink grass on the hills, misty mornings — plus what to pack and how to plan rides by motorbike.', "November is one of the loveliest months to visit Da Lat. The rains are fading, the hills turn yellow and pink, and the mornings are crisp and misty. Here's what Da Lat in November is really like and how to plan your days.", 'friends-air-blade-van-phu-gate', '\n<h2>Weather in Da Lat in November</h2>\n<p>November is the turning point between the rainy and the dry season. Early in the month you can still get afternoon showers, sometimes heavy but usually short; towards the end of the month dry, sunny days become more common. Days are pleasant — often around 20°C — while early mornings and evenings are cool and can feel cold, especially on open hills and when riding. For the full year, see our guide to <a href="{ART:weather}">Da Lat weather by month</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Mornings:</b> cool, often misty in the valleys — great for photos.</li>\n<li><b>Middle of the day:</b> mild and often sunny; the best time for longer rides.</li>\n<li><b>Afternoons:</b> a shower is still possible, mainly in the first half of the month.</li>\n<li><b>Evenings:</b> cold enough for a jacket at the night market.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What\'s in season in November</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Wild sunflowers (dã quỳ):</b> the yellow flowers bloom along roads and hillsides from about late October to December, often at their best around mid-November. Read our <a href="{ART:wildsunflower}">wild sunflower guide</a> for where to ride.</li>\n<li><b>Pink grass (cỏ hồng):</b> some open hills around the city turn pink from about November to December — see our <a href="{ART:pinkgrass}">pink grass season guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Misty sunrises:</b> clear, cool mornings after the rains are good for cloud hunting; our <a href="{ART:cloudhunt}">cloud hunting guide</a> explains where and when.</li>\n<li><b>Persimmons and local produce:</b> late autumn is the season for dried persimmons (hồng treo gió) and fresh fruit and vegetables at the market. Strawberry farms get busier as the dry season starts; see <a href="{ART:strawberries}">strawberry farms in Da Lat</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Flower Festival build-up:</b> the 2026 Flower Festival is planned for the second half of December. Check the official announcements before you fix your dates.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-cafe-garden-dalat}\n<h2>What to pack for Da Lat in November</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A warm jacket or fleece and a light sweater — layers work best.</li>\n<li>A rain jacket or poncho for the odd shower (we also have ponchos at the shop).</li>\n<li>Gloves and a scarf or buff if you plan sunrise rides.</li>\n<li>Closed shoes with good grip; paths to viewpoints can be muddy early in the month.</li>\n<li>Sunscreen and sunglasses — the midday sun at this altitude is strong even when the air is cool.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Riding a motorbike in November</h2>\n<p>November is a great month for riding: less rain, green hills and flowers by the road. A few tips:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Plan longer rides for the morning and early afternoon, and keep an eye on the sky after 2–3 pm early in the month.</li>\n<li>Roads can stay wet and slippery in shaded bends after rain — slow down and brake gently.</li>\n<li>For flower hills and passes outside the city, the Honda Air Blade 125cc is the most comfortable; the electric scooter is best for the city and nearby spots.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Is November a good time to visit Da Lat?</h2>\n<p>Yes — for many locals it\'s one of the best months: fewer crowds than the December holidays, flowers everywhere and cool, fresh air. Weekends and public holidays are busier, so book your room and bike a little ahead if you come then.</p>\n<h2>Rent a bike for your November trip</h2>\n<p>At Văn Phú every bike — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter — costs 150,000 VND per day, with a free helmet, a phone holder and no cash deposit (you leave your passport or ID card). The shop at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học opens at 4:30 am, early enough for a sunrise ride. Tell us your plans and we\'ll suggest where the flowers are best this week. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')

C["articles"]['lt-reliable'] = ('reliable-motorbike-rental-da-lat', 'How to Choose a Reliable Motorbike Rental in Da Lat: 8 Things to Look For | Van Phu', 'How to Choose a Reliable Motorbike Rental in Da Lat', 'How to choose a reliable motorbike rental in Da Lat: a clear fixed price, no hidden fees, written rules, a condition check on video, well-maintained bikes and a shop you can reach on the road.', "Most people rent a motorbike in Da Lat only once or twice, so it's hard to know what to look for. These are the signs of a reliable motorbike rental in Da Lat — simple things you can check in a few minutes before you hand over your passport.", 'van-phu-shop-busy-morning-vision-air-blade', '\n<h2>1. A clear, fixed price</h2>\n<p>A reliable shop tells you the full daily price before you arrive, in writing, and charges exactly that. Ask how the rental day is counted (when it starts and when the bike must be back) and what happens if you return late. If the price changes when you show up, that\'s a warning sign. For reference, see <a href="{ART:price}">how much it costs to rent a motorbike in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n<h2>2. No hidden fees</h2>\n<p>Ask directly: is anything extra? Helmet, phone holder, fuel, delivery, a "cleaning fee"? A good answer lists what\'s included and what isn\'t — before you sign or pay.</p>\n<h2>3. Simple, honest paperwork</h2>\n<p>You should know exactly what you leave with the shop and when you get it back. A cash deposit is not always necessary; some shops, like ours, keep only a passport or ID card. Read more in <a href="{ART:lt-nodeposit}">renting without a cash deposit</a>.</p>\n<h2>4. Written rules you can read</h2>\n<p>Reliable shops share their rules in writing: return time, what to do if something breaks, how many riders, where not to ride. Clear rules protect both you and the shop, and they avoid arguments later.</p>\n<h2>5. A condition check — ideally on video</h2>\n<p>Before you ride away, walk around the bike together and film it: scratches, mirrors, lights, tyres, brakes. A shop that suggests this itself is showing that it has nothing to hide. Our <a href="{ART:lt-checklist}">checklist before renting a motorbike</a> shows what to look at.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-honda-vision-van-phu-shop}\n<h2>6. Well-maintained bikes and a test ride</h2>\n<p>Look at the tyres, try both brakes and the horn, check the lights. Ask for a short test ride — a reliable shop will offer one and swap the bike if something feels wrong. Helmets should be clean and fasten properly.</p>\n<h2>7. Easy to reach while you ride</h2>\n<p>Things happen on the road: a flat tyre, a dead phone, a wrong turn. Make sure the shop answers on an app you use (WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat) and save the number before you leave.</p>\n<h2>8. Honest advice, not just a rental</h2>\n<p>A good shop tells you which bike suits your plans — even if it\'s not the one you asked for — and warns you about steep passes, weather and safety. Real reviews from other travellers are useful too; read several, not just the best ones.</p>\n<h2>Quick checklist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Full price confirmed in writing before you arrive</li>\n<li>What\'s included listed clearly</li>\n<li>You know what you leave with the shop</li>\n<li>Rules shared in writing</li>\n<li>Condition filmed together, test ride done</li>\n<li>Shop\'s number saved in your phone</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How we do it at Văn Phú</h2>\n<p>Every bike costs 150,000 VND per day — a fixed price with no extra fees. You leave only your passport or ID card, no cash deposit. A helmet and phone holder are free, gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel, and we do a test ride and film the bike together before you go. The rental day runs until 21:00, and for 2+ days we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat. Reach us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat. Please ride sober and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')

C["articles"]['lt-ev-market'] = ('electric-scooter-da-lat-market', 'Going to Da Lat Market by Electric Scooter: Parking, Shopping & Tips | Van Phu', 'Going to Da Lat Market by Electric Scooter: Parking, Shopping and Tips', 'Going to Da Lat Market by electric scooter: the best time for morning shopping, where to park in paid parking, how to carry your bags and how to ride safely in the busy market area.', "Da Lat Market sits in the middle of town, only a short ride from our shop — the perfect trip for an electric scooter. Here's how to go to Da Lat Market by electric scooter: when to go, where to park and how to bring your shopping home.", 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '\n<h2>Why take an electric scooter to the market</h2>\n<p>The market area is close, busy and full of short stops — exactly the kind of riding an electric scooter is made for. It\'s quiet, easy to handle in slow traffic, and you don\'t need to think about gears or fuel. Each scooter comes with 2 batteries, which is far more than a market trip needs.</p>\n<h2>Best time to go</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Early morning (about 6–9 am):</b> the market is at its liveliest with locals buying vegetables, flowers and fruit, and traffic around it is calmer than later. Our shop opens at 4:30 am, so you can stop for breakfast on the way.</li>\n<li><b>Late morning and afternoon:</b> good for dried fruit, tea, coffee and souvenirs; the area gets more crowded.</li>\n<li><b>Evening:</b> the streets and steps around the market become the night market. Traffic is heavy and some roads get very crowded, so park a little further away and walk in. See our <a href="{ART:nightmarket}">night market guide</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Where to park</h2>\n<p>Use the paid parking spots (gửi xe) around the market and keep your ticket — an attendant watches the scooters. Don\'t leave the scooter on the footpath, in front of shop doors or where it blocks traffic. Lock the steering, take your valuables with you and leave nothing in the basket or on the hook. More in our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-parking}">electric scooter parking guide</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>What to buy</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Fresh fruit and vegetables from the Da Lat highlands</li>\n<li>Dried persimmons, dried fruit and candied jams (mứt)</li>\n<li>Artichoke tea, local tea and coffee</li>\n<li>Fresh flowers — wrapped well, they travel fine for a short ride</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For ideas on what to taste, see our guide to <a href="{ART:food}">what to eat in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n<h2>Carrying your shopping</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Small bags fit under the seat; use the hook at the front for a light bag only.</li>\n<li>A backpack is the safest way to carry heavier things.</li>\n<li>Keep both hands free for the handlebars — never ride holding bags.</li>\n<li>Don\'t stack big boxes on the footboard; if you buy a lot, ask the seller to pack it compactly or send it to your hotel.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Riding safely in the market area</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>An electric scooter is almost silent: pedestrians stepping out between stalls won\'t hear you. Ride slowly and use the horn gently.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Watch for motorbikes turning without signalling and cars stopping to unload.</li>\n<li>Some streets near the market are steep. Brake early and smoothly on the way down.</li>\n<li>Maximum 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg; helmets on for both.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All the details are in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>A market trip uses only a small part of the battery. Each scooter comes with 2 batteries, and you can swap in a full one at the shop for 20,000 VND if you keep riding afterwards. See our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">battery guide</a> to plan a longer day.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter for your market trip</h2>\n<p>Our electric scooters cost 150,000 VND per day, with free helmets, a phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. See the scooters on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>, then message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat.</p>\n')

