C["articles"]['sunrise-sunset'] = ('best-sunrise-sunset-spots-da-lat', 'Best Sunrise and Sunset Spots in Da Lat by Motorbike | Van Phu', 'Best Sunrise and Sunset Spots Around Da Lat (By Motorbike)', 'Where to watch the sunrise and sunset in Da Lat: lakes, hills, tea plantations and view cafés, when to leave, what to bring and how to ride there safely by motorbike.', 'Da Lat sits at about 1,500 m, and its best light comes at the edges of the day — mist on the lakes at dawn, golden pine hills in the evening. Here are the easiest sunrise and sunset spots around the city, and how to plan the ride.', 'van-phu-shop-early-pickup-air-blade', '\n<h2>When the sun rises and sets in Da Lat</h2>\n<p>Da Lat is close to the equator, so the times don\'t change much through the year: sunrise is roughly between 5:30 and 6:00, sunset roughly between 17:30 and 18:15 depending on the season. Check a weather app the day before. For sunrise, plan to arrive 20–30 minutes early — the sky colours before the sun appears are often the best part. Read more about the seasons in our <a href="{ART:weather}">Da Lat weather guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>Sunrise spots</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Xuan Huong Lake</b> (city centre) — the easiest option: mist over the water, joggers and early tai chi, and bakeries opening nearby. Walk part of the lake path and you\'ll find your own angle.</li>\n<li><b>Tuyen Lam Lake</b> (south, about 6–7 km) — mist rising between pine hills on still mornings. A calm ride on good roads; see our <a href="{ART:tuyenlam}">Tuyen Lam Lake guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Thien Phuc Duc and Da Phu hills</b> (north-west, under 10 km) — open hilltops where clouds can fill the valleys after a rainy night. The last part is steep: park at the bottom and walk up.</li>\n<li><b>Cau Dat tea hills</b> (east, about 20–25 km) — rows of tea and a sea of clouds on good mornings; the most famous sunrise trip from Da Lat. Our <a href="{ART:caudat}">Cau Dat sunrise guide</a> has the details.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All these spots are on our <a href="../../cloud-hunting-map/">cloud-hunting and sunrise map</a>, and our <a href="{ART:cloudhunt}">cloud hunting guide</a> explains when the clouds are most likely. Clouds are never guaranteed — they depend on the weather that morning.</p>\n{PHOTO:van-phu-guest-air-blade-thumbs-up}\n<h2>Sunset spots</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Tuyen Lam Lake</b> — evening light on the water and the pine forest. Start back before it gets fully dark: the forest roads have few street lights.</li>\n<li><b>Xuan Huong Lake</b> — a relaxed sunset walk in the centre, then dinner or the night market close by.</li>\n<li><b>Cafés on the eastern hills</b> — several hillside cafés look west over the city, so you get the sunset and then the city lights. See our <a href="../../night-view-cafe-map/">night-view café map</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Pine hills on the edge of town</b> — almost any open slope facing west works on a clear evening. Stop only where you can pull fully off the road.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Riding before dawn or after dark</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Mornings are cold, often around 12–16 °C: bring a warm jacket, gloves and a long-sleeve layer.</li>\n<li>Ride slowly with your lights on; fog can lower visibility on hill roads.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Watch for trucks on the main roads early in the morning.</li>\n<li>Use the phone holder for navigation and download an offline map before you leave.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Which bike to take</h2>\n<p>For spots in and near the centre — Xuan Huong Lake, Tuyen Lam Lake, the eastern cafés — any scooter is fine, including an electric one. For Cau Dat and other longer hill rides, the Honda Air Blade 125cc is the comfortable choice. Our <a href="{ROUTES}">route ideas</a> help you combine a sunrise with a full day out.</p>\n<h2>Rent a scooter for sunrise in Da Lat</h2>\n<p>Our shop opens at 4:30 am, so you can pick up a bike in time for the sunrise — or collect it the day before, as explained in our <a href="{ART:lt-early}">early-opening rental guide</a>. Every bike costs 150,000 VND per day with a free helmet, a phone holder and no cash deposit. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> and message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-first-time'] = ('is-it-easy-to-ride-a-scooter-in-dalat', 'Is It Easy to Ride a Scooter in Dalat? A First-Timer Guide | Van Phu', 'Is It Easy to Ride a Scooter in Dalat? Honest Tips for First-Timers', 'Is it easy to ride a scooter in Dalat? What makes it easier or harder for beginners: automatic scooters, quiet streets to practise, hills, rain and traffic on the right.', "Never ridden a scooter before, or only a few times? Many of our guests ask the same question: is it easy to ride a scooter in Dalat? Here's an honest answer — what helps, what's tricky, and how to start safely.", 'honda-vision-girls-helmets-dalat', '\n<h2>The short answer</h2>\n<p>For most people with some riding experience, yes. Da Lat traffic is calmer than in Ho Chi Minh City or Hanoi, the scooters are automatic, and distances in the city are short. What makes it harder are the hills, wet roads in the rainy season and, for some visitors, traffic on the right. If you have never ridden a two-wheeler at all, take it slowly — or start with an electric scooter on quiet streets.</p>\n<h2>What makes it easier</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Automatic scooters:</b> no gears and no clutch — throttle and two brakes. The Honda Vision 110cc is light and easy to balance; see our <a href="{ART:lt-vision}">Honda Vision rental guide</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Slower traffic:</b> most streets in the centre move at a relaxed speed.</li>\n<li><b>Short trips:</b> the main sights are a few kilometres apart, so you\'re rarely on the road for long.</li>\n<li><b>A test ride at the shop:</b> before you leave, we show you the controls and you try the bike in front of the shop.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What can be tricky</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hills:</b> Da Lat is built on slopes. Uphill, keep a steady throttle; downhill, brake early and gently with both brakes.</li>\n<li><b>Rain and wet roads:</b> painted lines and steep streets get slippery. Slow down and leave more distance.</li>\n<li><b>Traffic on the right:</b> Vietnam drives on the right. If you are used to the left, take extra care at roundabouts and when turning.</li>\n<li><b>Busy evening spots:</b> around the market and the lake at night traffic gets dense; park a little further away and walk in.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-bui-thi-xuan-street-dalat}\n<h2>How to practise on your first day</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Start in the morning, when the streets are quieter and the light is good.</li>\n<li>Do a few loops on calm streets before heading out of town.</li>\n<li>Practise starting, stopping and turning slowly; look where you want to go.</li>\n<li>Keep both hands on the handlebars and use the phone holder for directions, not your hand.</li>\n<li>Plan your first longer ride on an easy road, such as to Tuyen Lam Lake, and leave mountain passes for later.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>More general advice is in our <a href="{ART:riding}">tips for riding a motorbike in Vietnam</a>.</p>\n<h2>Which bike for a first-timer</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Electric scooter:</b> quiet and gentle, good for the city and nearby spots — see our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-beginners}">electric scooter for beginners</a> guide.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc:</b> light, easy and fine for most day trips.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc:</b> more power for hills and two riders, once you feel confident.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When not to ride</h2>\n<p>If you feel unsure after the test ride, there\'s no shame in taking a Grab or taxi for the day and trying again later. Never ride after alcohol or beer, always wear your helmet, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</p>\n<h2>Rent your first scooter in Dalat</h2>\n<p>Every bike costs 150,000 VND per day, with a free helmet, a phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. We\'ll take a video of the bike\'s condition with you and let you try it before you go. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> and message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-hills'] = ('electric-scooter-da-lat-hills', "Riding an Electric Scooter on Da Lat's Hills: Climbing & Braking Tips | Van Phu", "Riding an Electric Scooter on Da Lat's Hilly Streets: Practical Tips", 'Riding a rented electric scooter on Da Lat hills: how to climb steep streets, brake safely downhill, ride with two people and plan your battery — and when to choose the Air Blade instead.', "Da Lat is a city of slopes — almost every ride includes a climb and a descent. An electric scooter handles the city's hilly streets well if you ride it the right way. Here's how to climb, brake and plan your battery on an electric scooter in Da Lat.", 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>Can an electric scooter climb Da Lat\'s hills?</h2>\n<p>Yes — in the city and on the roads to nearby sights it climbs normal slopes without trouble. It is slower than a petrol scooter on long, steep climbs, especially with two people, so it suits city riding and short trips best. For long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road up to Lang Biang, we recommend the Honda Air Blade 125cc instead.</p>\n<h2>Climbing tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use a smooth, steady throttle. Build up a little speed before the slope rather than starting hard in the middle of it.</li>\n<li>Try not to stop halfway up a steep street. If you must, pull over to the side, hold the brake, then start again gently.</li>\n<li>With two riders the scooter climbs more slowly. On a very short, very steep stretch the passenger can get off and walk the last bit.</li>\n<li>Keep to the right and let faster bikes pass.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Braking downhill</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Slow down before the descent, not halfway down.</li>\n<li>Use both brakes together, gently and early; avoid grabbing the front brake hard.</li>\n<li>Keep your speed low enough to stop within the distance you can see, especially on bends.</li>\n<li>Wet roads and painted lines are slippery after rain — ride even slower. See our tips for an <a href="{ART:lt-ev-rain}">electric scooter in the rain</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Two riders on hills</h2>\n<p>An electric scooter carries a maximum of 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg. Near that limit, climbs are slower and braking distances longer, so leave more space and ride calmly. The passenger should sit still and lean with the rider, not against the turn.</p>\n<h2>Hills and the battery</h2>\n<p>Climbing uses more battery than riding on the flat, and two riders use more than one. Each scooter comes with 2 batteries, and you can swap in a full one at our shop for 20,000 VND. Plan hilly rides so you pass the shop when you need a fresh battery; our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">battery guide</a> explains how to plan your day.</p>\n<h2>Where hills are fine — and where to switch bikes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Good on an electric scooter:</b> the city centre, Xuan Huong Lake, the station area, Domaine de Marie, cafés in town and other short hilly streets.</li>\n<li><b>Better on the Air Blade:</b> Prenn and Mimosa passes, Lang Biang, Cau Dat and other long hill rides out of town.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not sure which bike suits your plan? Our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-city}">city electric scooter guide</a> has ideas for short routes.</p>\n<h2>Safety basics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>An electric scooter is almost silent — pedestrians may not hear you coming downhill. Use the horn gently.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right; helmets on for both riders.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Read all the details in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter in Da Lat</h2>\n<p>Our electric scooters cost 150,000 VND per day, with free helmets, a phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. See them on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>, then message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat.</p>\n')

C["articles"]['station'] = ('da-lat-railway-station-trai-mat-linh-phuoc', 'Da Lat Railway Station, the Train to Trai Mat & Linh Phuoc Pagoda | Van Phu', 'Da Lat Railway Station and the Train to Trai Mat & Linh Phuoc Pagoda', 'Visiting Da Lat Railway Station: the old Art Deco building, the short tourist train to Trai Mat, Linh Phuoc (Ve Chai) Pagoda nearby, and how to plan it by motorbike.', "Da Lat's old railway station is one of the most photographed buildings in the city — and from its platform a small tourist train still runs to Trai Mat village, a short walk from the colourful Linh Phuoc Pagoda. Here's how to plan the half-day, by train or by motorbike.", 'white-honda-vision-sidewalk-dalat', '\n<h2>Da Lat Railway Station: what you\'ll see</h2>\n<p>The station was built in the 1930s, during the French period, when a mountain railway connected Da Lat with the coast. Its Art Deco building with three tall, pointed roof gables is often said to echo the peaks of Lang Biang and the high roofs of Central Highlands communal houses. The old line down the mountains has long been closed, but the station has been restored and is now a popular sight.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>The main hall</b> with its coloured glass windows and old ticket counters.</li>\n<li><b>Old locomotives and carriages</b> on the tracks — many visitors come just for photos here.</li>\n<li><b>The platform</b> and small cafés around it, nice for a slow morning.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The station sits on the eastern side of the centre, about 1–2 km from Xuan Huong Lake — an easy ride. There is an entrance ticket for the station area; check the current price at the gate.</p>\n<h2>The tourist train to Trai Mat</h2>\n<p>A short tourist line of about 7 km runs from Da Lat to Trai Mat, a vegetable-growing area east of the city. The ride takes roughly 30 minutes each way, passing gardens, greenhouses and pine slopes. The train runs only a few times a day and the timetable changes, so ask at the station or the day before. You usually have some time in Trai Mat before the train goes back.</p>\n<h2>Linh Phuoc Pagoda (Ve Chai)</h2>\n<p>From Trai Mat station it is a short walk to Linh Phuoc Pagoda, known locally as Chùa Ve Chai — "the broken-glass pagoda" — because its walls, pillars and statues are decorated with mosaics of broken pottery, porcelain and glass. Highlights include a long dragon covered in pieces of beer bottles, a tall bell tower with a huge bell, and detailed altars inside. It is an active temple: dress modestly (shoulders and knees covered), speak quietly and take off your shoes where asked.</p>\n<p>Want more temples? Our <a href="../../pagoda-map/">pagoda map</a> shows other pagodas worth a ride around Da Lat.</p>\n{PHOTO:couple-air-blade-dalat-center-street}\n<h2>Train or motorbike?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Train both ways:</b> the classic experience. Park your bike at the station (paid parking — keep your ticket) and take the train there and back.</li>\n<li><b>Motorbike both ways:</b> the most flexible. Trai Mat is about 8 km east of the centre on city roads, so you choose your own time and can stop at farms and cafés on the way.</li>\n<li><b>Mix:</b> visit the station by bike, then ride to Trai Mat yourself if the train times don\'t fit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The route is short and mostly in town, so any of our scooters works — including an electric one. Vietnam drives on the right; ride slowly near the market streets in Trai Mat, where traffic and pedestrians mix.</p>\n<h2>Tips for a good visit</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Go in the morning: softer light for photos and fewer groups.</li>\n<li>Bring a light jacket — the train has open windows and mornings are cool.</li>\n<li>Combine it with nearby sights: the station is easy to pair with Xuan Huong Lake or the city centre, see our <a href="{ART:thingstodo}">things to do in central Da Lat</a>.</li>\n<li>For a full day, add the station and Linh Phuoc to our <a href="{ART:oneday}">Da Lat in one day</a> loop, or look at our <a href="{ROUTES}">route ideas</a>.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rent a scooter for the trip</h2>\n<p>Van Phu Da Lat rents the Honda Vision, the Honda Air Blade and electric scooters for 150,000 VND per day, with a free helmet, a phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. Message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat and ride to the station and Trai Mat at your own pace.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-groups'] = ('motorbike-rental-da-lat-for-groups', 'Motorbike Rental in Da Lat for Groups: Booking 4+ Bikes | Van Phu', 'Motorbike Rental in Da Lat for Groups of Friends: How It Works', 'Motorbike rental in Da Lat for groups: how to book 4 or more bikes, mixing Vision, Air Blade and electric scooters, documents, pick-up and tips for riding together safely.', "Travelling with a group of friends and want everyone on a bike? Motorbike rental in Da Lat for groups is simple if you plan a little ahead. Here's how to book several bikes, choose the right mix and ride together safely.", 'group-rental-air-blade-van-phu-morning', '\n<h2>Book ahead for 4 or more bikes</h2>\n<p>For a group, message us a day or two before — especially for weekends and holidays, when many bikes are already booked. Tell us:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>how many bikes and which models (Honda Vision, Honda Air Blade, electric scooter);</li>\n<li>the pick-up date and time, and how many days;</li>\n<li>whether you\'ll come to the shop or need delivery to your hotel.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We confirm by message so everyone knows the plan. Our <a href="{ART:lt-booking}">online booking guide</a> explains what to send.</p>\n<h2>Price and documents</h2>\n<p>Every bike costs 150,000 VND per day — the same fixed price for every model, for one bike or ten. There is no cash deposit: each bike is rented against a passport (foreign guests) or an ID card (Vietnamese guests). Every bike comes with a free helmet and a phone holder, and gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel. A rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00.</p>\n<h2>Choosing the right mix of bikes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Honda Air Blade 125cc:</b> the best choice for riders carrying a passenger and for hill roads and passes.</li>\n<li><b>Honda Vision 110cc:</b> light and easy — good for less experienced riders on city streets and day trips.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooter:</b> quiet and fun in the city, max 2 riders under 140 kg combined; not for long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or Lang Biang.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Plan the route for the slowest bike in the group. If you\'re heading out of town on hill roads, put everyone on gas scooters. Travelling as couples? See our tips on <a href="{ART:lt-two}">renting a motorbike for two people</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:friends-air-blade-van-phu-gate}\n<h2>Pick-up for a group</h2>\n<p>Our shop opens at 4:30 am, which helps when a group wants an early start. For every bike we do a short test ride and take a video of its condition together with you, so it\'s worth arriving a little early rather than all at the last minute. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat — for shorter rentals, just message us.</p>\n<h2>Riding together safely</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Lead and sweep:</b> the most confident rider goes first, another experienced rider goes last.</li>\n<li><b>Ride in a line,</b> not side by side, and keep a safe gap.</li>\n<li><b>Agree on regroup points</b> — a café, a fuel station, a viewpoint — instead of trying to keep everyone in sight.</li>\n<li><b>Share a map pin</b> of each stop in your group chat; the phone holder keeps navigation hands-free.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Don\'t rush through intersections to keep up with the group.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Good group routes</h2>\n<p>Tuyen Lam Lake, the waterfalls south of town and the city centre are easy for mixed groups. For hill roads with views, see our guide to <a href="{ART:passes}">scenic roads around Da Lat</a> — on gas scooters only.</p>\n<h2>Book bikes for your group</h2>\n<p>Send us your group size and dates on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat, and see all our bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. We\'ll prepare the bikes so your group can ride off together.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-weekend'] = ('electric-scooter-weekend-da-lat', 'Electric Scooter Rental in Da Lat for a Weekend: 2-Day Plan | Van Phu', 'Electric Scooter Rental in Da Lat for a Weekend: A Simple 2-Day Plan', 'Electric scooter rental in Da Lat for a weekend: a relaxed 2-day plan in and around the centre, how to plan battery swaps at the shop, parking and booking tips for busy Saturdays.', "Two days in Da Lat and you'd like to ride something quiet and easy? Electric scooter rental in Da Lat for a weekend works well if you keep to the city and nearby sights. Here's a simple 2-day plan and how to organise the batteries.", 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '\n<h2>Why an electric scooter for a weekend</h2>\n<p>Most of Da Lat\'s classic sights are in or close to the centre: the lake, the flower garden, the station, cafés, the market. An electric scooter is quiet, easy to ride and perfect for this kind of weekend. For a weekend with long hill rides — Cau Dat, Lang Biang, the Prenn or Mimosa passes — choose the Honda Air Blade instead. General tips for a two-day rental are in our <a href="{ART:lt-2days}">2-day scooter rental guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>Book ahead for Saturday</h2>\n<p>Weekends are the busiest time in Da Lat, and electric scooters are popular. Message us a day or two before with your dates and pick-up time. Two days cost 2 × 150,000 VND = 300,000 VND, with free helmets, a phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat.</p>\n<h2>Day 1: lake, flowers and the night market</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Morning around Xuan Huong Lake and the city flower garden.</li>\n<li>Lam Vien Square and a coffee with a view in the centre.</li>\n<li>Afternoon at Domaine de Marie church and the streets around it.</li>\n<li>Evening at the night market — park in a guarded parking area nearby and walk in.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Day 2: the station and Trai Mat</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Morning at the old railway station, then on to Trai Mat and Linh Phuoc Pagoda — see our <a href="{ART:station}">railway station and Linh Phuoc guide</a>.</li>\n<li>Back in town for lunch, then a quiet café or a last walk before returning the scooter by 21:00.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prefer something else? Tuyen Lam Lake is also a calm ride from the centre on an electric scooter.</p>\n{PHOTO:evening-pickup-van-phu-1-15-5-thong-thien-hoc}\n<h2>Planning the batteries</h2>\n<p>Each scooter comes with 2 batteries. When one runs low, come by the shop and swap it for a full one for 20,000 VND. A good habit for a weekend: plan your day so you pass the shop around midday or before an evening ride. Hills and two riders use more battery, so keep an eye on the display. Our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">battery guide</a> explains how far to plan.</p>\n<h2>Parking and safety</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use paid parking (gửi xe) at sights and markets and keep the ticket; see our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-parking}">electric scooter parking tips</a>.</li>\n<li>Max 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg; both wear helmets.</li>\n<li>The motor is almost silent — use the horn gently when pedestrians don\'t hear you.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All the details are in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter for your weekend</h2>\n<p>See our electric scooters on the <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>, then message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat to book your weekend.</p>\n')
