C["articles"]['phrases'] = ('useful-vietnamese-phrases-da-lat', 'Useful Vietnamese Phrases for Da Lat: Renting, Eating, Shopping | Van Phu', 'Useful Vietnamese Phrases for Da Lat: Renting a Bike, Eating and Shopping', 'Simple Vietnamese phrases that help in Da Lat: greetings, renting and parking a scooter, buying fuel, ordering food and coffee, and shopping at the market — with easy tips.', 'You don\'t need to speak Vietnamese to enjoy Da Lat, but a few words go a long way — a smile and a "cảm ơn" open doors at markets, cafés and parking lots. Here are the phrases our guests find most useful, grouped by situation.', 'van-phu-shop-busy-morning-vision-air-blade', '\n<h2>A few tips before you start</h2>\n<p>Vietnamese is a tonal language, so the same syllable can mean different things depending on the tone. Don\'t worry about getting it perfect: people in Da Lat are friendly and patient, and they understand a lot from context, gestures and a smile. A good trick is to save the phrases below on your phone and simply show the screen when you\'re stuck. Translation apps also work well for longer questions.</p>\n<h2>Greetings and basics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Xin chào</b> — hello (works any time of day).</li>\n<li><b>Cảm ơn</b> — thank you. <b>Cảm ơn nhiều</b> — thank you very much.</li>\n<li><b>Xin lỗi</b> — sorry / excuse me.</li>\n<li><b>Vâng / Dạ</b> — yes (polite). <b>Không</b> — no.</li>\n<li><b>Bao nhiêu tiền?</b> — how much is it?</li>\n<li><b>Nhà vệ sinh ở đâu?</b> — where is the toilet?</li>\n<li><b>Tôi không hiểu</b> — I don\'t understand.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Renting and riding a scooter</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Tôi muốn thuê xe máy</b> — I want to rent a motorbike. <b>xe máy điện</b> — electric scooter.</li>\n<li><b>Gửi xe ở đâu?</b> — where can I park? Paid parking is called <b>gửi xe</b>; you get a ticket — <b>vé xe</b> — keep it until you leave.</li>\n<li><b>Cây xăng</b> — petrol station. <b>Đổ đầy bình</b> — fill it up, or say an amount, e.g. <b>năm mươi nghìn</b> (50,000 VND).</li>\n<li><b>Đi thẳng</b> — go straight, <b>rẽ trái</b> — turn left, <b>rẽ phải</b> — turn right.</li>\n<li><b>Xe bị hỏng</b> — the bike is broken down. <b>Xe bị thủng lốp</b> — flat tyre.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At our shop you don\'t need any of this — we speak English and answer on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram and WeChat. But these words help at parking lots and petrol stations around town. New to riding here? Read our <a href="{ART:riding}">tips for riding a motorbike in Vietnam</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-cafe-garden-dalat}\n<h2>Eating and drinking</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cho tôi một…</b> — give me one… (e.g. <b>cho tôi một bánh mì</b>).</li>\n<li><b>Cà phê sữa đá</b> — iced coffee with condensed milk; <b>cà phê sữa nóng</b> — the hot version, popular on cool Da Lat mornings. <b>Trà</b> — tea.</li>\n<li><b>Không cay</b> — not spicy. <b>Ít đường</b> — less sugar.</li>\n<li><b>Tôi ăn chay</b> — I\'m vegetarian. <b>Không thịt</b> — no meat.</li>\n<li><b>Ngon quá!</b> — delicious! Cooks love hearing it.</li>\n<li><b>Tính tiền</b> — the bill, please.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not sure what to order? Our guide to <a href="{ART:food}">what to eat in Da Lat</a> lists the local dishes worth trying.</p>\n<h2>Shopping at the market</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cái này bao nhiêu?</b> — how much is this?</li>\n<li><b>Đắt quá</b> — too expensive. <b>Bớt được không?</b> — can you lower the price a little?</li>\n<li><b>Một ký</b> — one kilo (useful for strawberries, avocados or dried fruit).</li>\n<li><b>Lấy cái này</b> — I\'ll take this one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bargaining is normal for clothes and souvenirs at Da Lat Market and the <a href="{ART:nightmarket}">night market</a>, but stay friendly — small food stalls usually have fixed prices.</p>\n<h2>Numbers and prices</h2>\n<p>Prices are often said in thousands: <b>nghìn</b> (or <b>ngàn</b>) means thousand, and you will often see "k", e.g. 30k = 30,000 VND. One to five: <b>một, hai, ba, bốn, năm</b>. If in doubt, ask the seller to type the price on a phone or calculator — everyone does it.</p>\n<h2>Ride around Da Lat with Van Phu</h2>\n<p>Practise your new words on the road: Van Phu Da Lat rents the Honda Vision, the Honda Air Blade and electric scooters for 150,000 VND per day, with a free helmet, a phone holder and no cash deposit — you leave your passport or ID card. Message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat — in English, no Vietnamese needed.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-sameday'] = ('same-day-motorbike-rental-da-lat', 'Same-Day Motorbike Rental in Da Lat: Walk In, No Booking | Van Phu', 'Same-Day Motorbike Rental in Da Lat: Can You Just Walk In?', 'Same-day motorbike rental in Da Lat without booking ahead: what to bring, how long pick-up takes, when it gets busy and how a quick message helps.', "Arrived in Da Lat and want a scooter today? Same-day motorbike rental in Da Lat is usually easy — you can walk in, choose a bike and ride off. Here's what to expect, what to bring and when a quick message first makes sense.", 'van-phu-shop-early-pickup-air-blade', '\n<h2>Can you rent a motorbike in Da Lat without booking?</h2>\n<p>Yes. Many guests decide on the spot — after breakfast, after the night bus or after seeing how nice the weather is. At Van Phu you can walk in to our shop at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học, choose a Honda Vision, a Honda Air Blade or an electric scooter, and leave on the same day. The shop opens at 4:30 in the morning, so even early arrivals can pick up a bike right away.</p>\n<h2>What to bring</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Your passport (foreign guests) or ID card (Vietnamese guests) — we keep it during the rental instead of a cash deposit.</li>\n<li>Money for the rental: 150,000 VND per day for every bike, fixed price.</li>\n<li>Your phone with maps — every bike has a phone holder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That\'s all. The full list is in our guide to <a href="{ART:lt-documents}">documents for renting a motorbike</a>.</p>\n<h2>How long does a walk-in pick-up take?</h2>\n<p>Usually just a few minutes. We check your passport or ID card, you choose a bike, we fit a free helmet and you take a short test ride. Together we record a quick video of the bike\'s condition, so returning it later is simple. Gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel — enough to reach a petrol station. The step-by-step process is explained in <a href="{ART:lt-procedure}">how to rent a motorbike in Da Lat</a>.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-honda-vision-van-phu-shop}\n<h2>When is it busy?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Weekends</b>, especially Saturday mornings, when many visitors arrive from Ho Chi Minh City.</li>\n<li><b>Public holidays and festivals</b> — Da Lat fills up and bikes go quickly.</li>\n<li><b>Early mornings</b> in sunrise season, when many guests want a bike for cloud hunting or the tea hills.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On normal weekdays, walking in is rarely a problem. On busy days, or if you want a specific model (the Air Blade for passes, or an electric scooter), a message a few hours before helps us keep one ready. Booking takes one minute — see <a href="{ART:lt-booking}">how to book online</a>.</p>\n<h2>Same-day rental tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>The rental day runs until 21:00.</b> Picking up in the afternoon? It is still counted as one day, so plan a short ride or keep the bike for the next day too.</li>\n<li><b>Delivery:</b> for rentals of 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat. For a single day, message us and we\'ll see what\'s possible — or simply come to the shop.</li>\n<li><b>Groups:</b> several bikes at once are best arranged in advance; see our tips for <a href="{ART:lt-groups}">group rentals</a>.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rent a bike today</h2>\n<p>Walk in, or send a quick message on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat to check availability first. See all bikes and prices on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> — same-day motorbike rental in Da Lat, with no cash deposit and a free helmet.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-homestay'] = ('electric-scooter-homestay-outside-centre-da-lat', 'Electric Scooter in Da Lat When Your Homestay Is Outside the Centre | Van Phu', 'Electric Scooter Rental in Da Lat When Your Homestay Is Outside the Centre', 'Staying in a homestay outside central Da Lat? When an electric scooter works, how to plan battery swaps around your daily rides, hills on the way home and when the Air Blade is the better choice.', "Many of Da Lat's nicest homestays sit on hillsides and in valleys outside the centre. Can you still use an electric scooter? Often yes — if you plan your days around the battery. Here's how to decide, and when we'd suggest the Honda Air Blade instead.", 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>First: how far is your homestay?</h2>\n<p>"Outside the centre" can mean a quiet street ten minutes from Xuan Huong Lake, or a farm stay deep in the valleys towards Tuyen Lam, Trai Mat or Lac Duong. Before you choose a bike, look at the map: how far is the homestay from the centre, and is the road to it steep? Send us the name or a map pin and we\'ll tell you honestly which bike suits it. Our guide to <a href="{ART:where-to-stay}">where to stay in Da Lat</a> describes the different areas.</p>\n<h2>When an electric scooter works well</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Your homestay is on the edge of the city and the road there is mostly gentle.</li>\n<li>You spend your days in and around the centre: the lake, cafés, the market, the station.</li>\n<li>You ride alone or as two people with a combined weight under 140 kg.</li>\n<li>You don\'t mind planning one battery swap at the shop on your daily route.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An electric scooter is quiet, easy to ride and pleasant on calm evening rides back to a homestay among the pines.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Planning the batteries around your homestay</h2>\n<p>Each scooter comes with 2 batteries, and you can swap a low one for a full one at our shop for 20,000 VND. The key with a homestay outside the centre is simple: make sure you never leave the city with too little battery for the ride home. A few habits that help:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Count your homestay–centre trip twice a day (there and back) when you plan the day.</li>\n<li>Pass the shop at midday or before your evening ride home to swap the battery.</li>\n<li>Uphill roads and two riders use more battery — watch the display and swap earlier rather than later.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">battery guide</a> explains how far to plan, and <a href="{ART:lt-ev-hills}">riding an electric scooter on Da Lat\'s hills</a> covers climbing and braking on steep streets.</p>\n<h2>When to choose the Honda Air Blade instead</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The homestay is far out — for example in the Lac Duong valleys, past Tuyen Lam Lake or along long hill roads.</li>\n<li>The road home has long, steep climbs, or you plan rides over the Prenn or Mimosa passes or to Lang Biang.</li>\n<li>You want to ride to Cau Dat or other distant sights from the homestay without passing the shop.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For these cases the <a href="{ART:lt-airblade}">Honda Air Blade 125cc</a> is the better and calmer choice — same price, 150,000 VND per day, and it comes with 1 liter of fuel.</p>\n<h2>Delivery, parking and safety</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver free to hotels in central Da Lat. If your homestay is outside the centre, message us with the location and we\'ll tell you what\'s possible.</li>\n<li>Park the scooter inside or in a safe spot at the homestay overnight — ask your host.</li>\n<li>Country roads can be dark at night: ride slowly, and both riders wear helmets.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law. All details are in our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Ask us before you book</h2>\n<p>Tell us where you\'re staying and we\'ll help you choose. See our electric scooters on the <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or compare all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>, then message us on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat.</p>\n')
