C["articles"]['parking-fuel'] = ('parking-fuel-practical-riding-da-lat', 'Riding in Da Lat: Parking, Fuel, Helmets and Rain Gear | Van Phu', 'Practical Riding in Da Lat: Parking, Fuel, Charging, Helmets and Rain Gear', 'Practical tips for riding a scooter in Da Lat: how paid parking (gửi xe) works, buying fuel at petrol stations, battery swaps for electric scooters, helmets and what rain gear to carry.', 'Riding around Da Lat is easy once you know a few everyday things: where to leave the bike, how to fill up, what to do about the battery on an electric scooter, and how to stay dry and warm. Here is the practical side of riding in the city, in one place.', 'honda-air-blade-vision-fleet-van-phu', '\n<h2>Parking: how gửi xe works</h2>\n<p>At markets, the lake, attractions and many cafés you will see a parking area with an attendant. This is called <b>gửi xe</b>. You ride in, the attendant gives you a small paper ticket (<b>vé xe</b>) or writes a number on the seat, and you pay a small fee in cash — either when you arrive or when you leave. Keep the ticket until you collect the bike.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Many cafés and restaurants have their own parking with staff who watch the bikes; it is often free for customers.</li>\n<li>Take valuables with you and don\'t leave your phone in the phone holder.</li>\n<li>Follow the attendant\'s directions. They often move bikes around to make space, so lock the steering only if they don\'t ask you to leave it unlocked.</li>\n<li>Avoid parking on footpaths or anywhere signs say no parking. When in doubt, use a gửi xe area.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Riding an electric scooter? Our guide to <a href="{ART:lt-ev-parking}">electric scooter parking in Da Lat</a> covers the details.</p>\n<h2>Fuel: filling up at a petrol station</h2>\n<p>Our gas scooters (Honda Vision and Honda Air Blade) come with 1 liter of fuel — enough to reach a petrol station near the centre. Filling up in Vietnam is simple:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Petrol stations are called <b>cây xăng</b>. Staff fill the tank for you — you stay on or next to the bike.</li>\n<li>Turn off the engine and open the fuel cap. We show you where it is at pick-up.</li>\n<li>Say <b>đổ đầy</b> (fill it up) or show an amount in VND, for example 50,000 VND.</li>\n<li>The usual choices are RON 95 or E5 RON 92 petrol; both work in our scooters.</li>\n<li>Cash is the easiest way to pay; many stations also accept bank transfer or cards.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Plan to fill up before longer rides out of the city — stations are common in Da Lat but fewer on quiet country roads.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-bui-thi-xuan-street-dalat}\n<h2>Charging: electric scooters</h2>\n<p>With an electric scooter you don\'t need to look for a charging point. Each scooter comes with 2 batteries, and when one runs low you swap it for a full one at our shop for 20,000 VND. Keep an eye on the display and plan your day so you pass the shop before the battery gets low. Our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">battery guide</a> explains how to plan it.</p>\n<h2>Helmets</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Helmets are required for both the rider and the passenger in Vietnam. Every rental includes a free helmet.</li>\n<li>Always fasten the strap — a helmet only protects you if it stays on.</li>\n<li>Sunglasses or a helmet with a visor help against dust, sun and wind.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rain gear and warm clothes</h2>\n<p>Da Lat\'s rainy season usually runs from about May to October or November, often with afternoon showers. A cheap rain poncho (<b>áo mưa</b>) from a small shop or convenience store fits under the seat and makes a big difference. On wet roads, ride slower, brake earlier — especially downhill — and take care on painted lines and steel plates.</p>\n<p>Mornings and evenings are cool all year. Bring a light jacket, and gloves if you ride before sunrise. More tips are in our article on <a href="{ART:lt-rainy}">renting a motorbike in the rainy season</a>.</p>\n<h2>A few everyday rules</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Use your lights in fog and after dark.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n<li>New to riding here? Read our <a href="{ART:riding}">tips for riding a motorbike in Vietnam</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Ride with Van Phu</h2>\n<p>All our bikes come with a free helmet and a phone holder, and gas scooters leave the shop with 1 liter of fuel. If you\'re unsure where to park or fill up on your route, just message us — we\'re happy to help. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-return'] = ('returning-rental-motorbike-da-lat', 'Returning a Rental Motorbike in Da Lat: Times, Extensions, Tips | Van Phu', 'Returning a Rental Motorbike in Da Lat: What to Know', 'Returning a rental motorbike in Da Lat: return by 21:00, how to extend by message, early returns before your bus, fuel and battery, and how the pick-up video makes the check quick.', "Returning a rental motorbike in Da Lat should be as easy as picking it up. Here's when to bring the bike back, how to extend if you want more time, and what happens at the shop so you can get your passport back and catch your bus without stress.", 'van-phu-shop-busy-morning-vision-air-blade', '\n<h2>When to return the bike</h2>\n<p>A rental day at Van Phu runs from pick-up until 21:00. Bring the bike back to our shop at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học by 21:00 on your last day. If you rented for several days, the same 21:00 time applies on the final day.</p>\n<p>Running late because of traffic or rain? Send us a message on WhatsApp, Zalo, Telegram or WeChat before 21:00 so we know you\'re on the way. A quick message is always better than a surprise.</p>\n<h2>Want more time? Extend by message</h2>\n<p>If you decide to stay in Da Lat longer, you don\'t need to come to the shop to extend. Just message us, tell us how many extra days you want, and we\'ll confirm. Every extra day is the same fixed price: 150,000 VND per day. Planning a longer stay from the start? See <a href="{ART:lt-multiday}">renting a motorbike for a week or longer</a>.</p>\n<h2>Returning early in the morning</h2>\n<p>Catching an early bus? Our shop opens at 4:30 in the morning, so you can return the bike on your way to the bus station. Tell us the evening before what time you\'ll come, and keep a few minutes for the check. Remember that the rental day ends at 21:00, so returning the next morning counts as another day — ask us and we\'ll help you choose the cheaper option.</p>\n{PHOTO:evening-pickup-van-phu-1-15-5-thong-thien-hoc}\n<h2>What happens at the shop</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Quick look at the bike.</b> At pick-up we recorded a short video of the bike\'s condition together with you. At return we simply compare — so nobody argues about old scratches.</li>\n<li><b>Helmet and phone holder.</b> Bring back the helmet you received; the phone holder stays on the bike.</li>\n<li><b>Passport or ID card back.</b> There is no cash deposit, so once the bike is checked we hand back your passport (foreign guests) or ID card (Vietnamese guests) and you\'re done.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The whole return usually takes just a few minutes.</p>\n<h2>Fuel and batteries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Gas scooters</b> leave the shop with 1 liter of fuel. If you\'re unsure how much fuel to leave in the tank at the end, ask us at pick-up so there are no questions later.</li>\n<li><b>Electric scooters</b> come with 2 batteries — please bring both back with the scooter.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Tips for an easy return</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Take a photo of where you parked the night before and save our shop as a pin in your maps.</li>\n<li>Check the under-seat storage for sunglasses, rain ponchos or chargers before you hand over the bike.</li>\n<li>If something went wrong during the rental — a flat tyre, a small fall, a warning light — tell us honestly; we\'d rather know and help.</li>\n<li>If we delivered the bike to your hotel, message us to arrange how to return it.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, even on the last evening, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The pick-up side is explained step by step in <a href="{ART:lt-procedure}">how to rent a motorbike in Da Lat</a>, and our <a href="{ART:lt-checklist}">checklist before renting</a> shows what we look at together.</p>\n<h2>Rent and return with Van Phu</h2>\n<p>Fixed price of 150,000 VND per day, no cash deposit, a free helmet and a simple return before 21:00. See all bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> and message us any time about returning a rental motorbike in Da Lat.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-morning'] = ('early-morning-electric-scooter-ride-da-lat', 'Early-Morning Electric Scooter Ride in Da Lat: Lake, Breakfast, Market | Van Phu', 'Early-Morning Electric Scooter Ride in Da Lat: A Quiet Start to the Day', 'An early-morning electric scooter ride in Da Lat: pick up from 4:30, misty Xuan Huong Lake, local breakfast, the market waking up, what to wear and how to ride safely with a quiet motor.', "Da Lat is at its calmest just after sunrise: mist on the lake, joggers in the park and the smell of fresh bread from the bakeries. A quiet electric scooter is a lovely way to see it. Here's a simple early-morning plan near the centre — and what to know before you go.", 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '\n<h2>Why ride in the early morning</h2>\n<p>Before about 8 am the streets of central Da Lat are quiet, the air is fresh and the light is soft. An electric scooter fits this time perfectly: it starts silently, it\'s easy to ride slowly, and you can stop wherever you like. Our shop opens at 4:30, so you can pick up a scooter while it\'s still dark or come at sunrise.</p>\n<h2>A simple early-morning route</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Xuan Huong Lake.</b> Ride the road around the lake while the mist lifts off the water. There are many places to stop for photos along the shore. Our guide to <a href="{ART:lt-ev-lake}">riding around Xuan Huong Lake by electric scooter</a> has more ideas.</li>\n<li><b>Lam Vien Square.</b> Wide and calm in the early morning, with views over the lake.</li>\n<li><b>Breakfast.</b> Da Lat wakes up with bánh mì, warm soy milk (<b>sữa đậu nành</b>), bánh căn and noodle soups from small local shops. A hot drink on a cool morning is part of the experience.</li>\n<li><b>Da Lat Market.</b> In the morning the market area is busy with fresh vegetables, flowers and fruit arriving from the farms. Park in a gửi xe area and walk around — see <a href="{ART:lt-ev-market}">going to Da Lat Market by electric scooter</a>.</li>\n<li><b>A café with a view.</b> Finish with coffee on the hillside streets around the centre before the city gets busy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All of this is close together in central Da Lat, so it\'s a relaxed ride with lots of stops rather than a long trip.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>What about sunrise at Cau Dat?</h2>\n<p>The tea hills of Cau Dat are a famous sunrise spot, but they are far from the city with long hill roads — not a good trip for an electric scooter. For Cau Dat or cloud hunting outside the city we recommend the Honda Air Blade 125cc; see <a href="{ART:lt-caudat-early}">renting a motorbike for a dawn ride to Cau Dat</a>. For an early start in town, the electric scooter is ideal.</p>\n<h2>Battery: plan around the shop</h2>\n<p>Each electric scooter comes with 2 batteries, and you can swap a low one for a full one at our shop for 20,000 VND. An early-morning loop near the centre is short, so you can easily pass the shop later in the day for a swap. Our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">battery guide</a> explains how to plan longer days.</p>\n<h2>Riding safely in the morning</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>The motor is quiet.</b> Joggers, walkers and people setting up stalls may not hear you coming. Ride slowly around the lake and the market, and use the horn gently when needed.</li>\n<li><b>Mist and low light.</b> Turn on your lights in fog and before sunrise.</li>\n<li><b>Cold.</b> Mornings in Da Lat are cool all year. Wear a jacket, and gloves if you start before dawn.</li>\n<li>Maximum 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg; both wear helmets.</li>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Please read our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a> before your ride, and see <a href="{ART:lt-early}">early-opening motorbike rental</a> for picking up before sunrise.</p>\n<h2>Book your morning scooter</h2>\n<p>Message us the evening before with your pick-up time and we\'ll have a scooter charged and ready. See our electric scooters on the <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> — 150,000 VND per day, no cash deposit, free helmet.</p>\n')
