C["articles"]['pagodas'] = ('temples-and-pagodas-of-da-lat', 'Temples and Pagodas of Da Lat Worth a Ride: Routes & Etiquette | Van Phu', 'Temples and Pagodas of Da Lat Worth a Ride by Motorbike', 'The temples and pagodas of Da Lat worth a ride: Linh Son, Van Hanh, Chua Tau, Truc Lam, Linh Phuoc and Linh An — easy routes by motorbike, best time to go and temple etiquette.', "Da Lat isn't only flowers and pine hills — the city and its outskirts are full of peaceful pagodas and Zen monasteries, each with its own style. Here are the temples worth a ride, how to group them into easy routes by motorbike, and how to visit respectfully.", 'honda-vision-girls-helmets-dalat', '\n<h2>Why visit Da Lat\'s pagodas</h2>\n<p>Most of Da Lat\'s pagodas are active Buddhist temples where local families come to pray, light incense and spend a quiet moment. For travellers they are some of the calmest places in the city: pine trees, gardens, statues and, on a clear morning, wide views over the hills. Most are free to enter, and many are only a few minutes from the centre — perfect for a slow half-day by motorbike.</p>\n<h2>Pagodas in and near the centre</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Linh Son Pagoda</b> — one of the oldest pagodas in Da Lat, set among pine trees on a small hill north of the centre. Quiet in the early morning.</li>\n<li><b>Linh Quang Pagoda</b> — a small pagoda with many statues and a dragon around a pond. Few tourists, very local.</li>\n<li><b>Van Hanh Zen Monastery</b> — known for its large golden Buddha statue and a view over the city; nice in the afternoon light.</li>\n<li><b>Chua Tau (Thien Vuong Co Sat)</b> — a Chinese-style pagoda with a tall white Buddha statue on a pine-covered hill, south of the centre.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Linh Son, Linh Quang and Van Hanh fit into one short northern loop of about an hour and a half with stops.</p>\n<h2>Worth the extra ride</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Truc Lam Zen Monastery</b> — on a hill above Tuyen Lam Lake, about 5–7 km south of the centre, with neat gardens and lake views. You can ride up or take the cable car from Robin Hill. Our <a href="{ART:tuyenlam}">Tuyen Lam Lake and Truc Lam guide</a> has the details.</li>\n<li><b>Linh Phuoc Pagoda (Chùa Ve Chai)</b> — in Trai Mat, about 8 km east, famous for its mosaics of broken pottery and glass, a long dragon and a tall bell tower. You can also get there on the old tourist train — see <a href="{ART:station}">Da Lat Railway Station and the train to Trai Mat</a>.</li>\n<li><b>Linh An Pagoda (Nam Ban)</b> — next to Elephant Falls, roughly 25–30 km from the city, with a tall Buddha statue you can climb inside. Combine it with the falls in our <a href="{ART:waterfalls}">waterfalls guide</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:van-phu-guest-air-blade-thumbs-up}\n<h2>Easy routes by motorbike</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>North loop (short):</b> Linh Son → Linh Quang → Van Hanh. City streets only — fine on any of our scooters, including electric.</li>\n<li><b>South:</b> Chua Tau, then Truc Lam above Tuyen Lam Lake. Go in the morning, when the monastery gardens are calm.</li>\n<li><b>East:</b> Linh Phuoc in Trai Mat — by bike or by train.</li>\n<li><b>Far west:</b> Linh An with Elephant Falls. This is a longer day with hill roads, so take the Honda Air Blade 125cc rather than an electric scooter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All locations, directions and opening times are on our <a href="../../pagoda-map/">Da Lat pagoda map</a>, which you can also download as a PDF. For more ideas, see our <a href="{ROUTES}">motorbike routes</a>.</p>\n<h2>Temple etiquette</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Cover shoulders and knees — no shorts or sleeveless tops in the main halls. A light scarf or long trousers in your bag solves it.</li>\n<li>Take off your shoes before entering a hall where you see shoes lined up.</li>\n<li>Speak softly, don\'t point your feet towards the altar, and ask before photographing monks or people praying.</li>\n<li>Don\'t climb on statues; leave a small donation in the box if you wish.</li>\n<li>Parking: use the parking area at the gate and keep your ticket.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Best time to go</h2>\n<p>Early morning is the most peaceful time at almost every pagoda: cool air, soft light and few visitors. The first and fifteenth days of the lunar month and big Buddhist holidays are busier with local worshippers — a beautiful atmosphere, but be extra respectful. Da Lat mornings are cool, so bring a jacket.</p>\n<h2>Ride to the pagodas with Van Phu</h2>\n<p>Our shop opens at 4:30, so you can start your temple morning early. Every bike is 150,000 VND per day with a free helmet and no cash deposit. Message us and we\'ll suggest the best loop for your day. No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-breakdown'] = ('flat-tyre-breakdown-rental-motorbike-da-lat', 'Flat Tyre or Breakdown on a Rental Motorbike in Da Lat: What to Do | Van Phu', 'Flat Tyre or Breakdown on a Rental Motorbike in Da Lat: What to Do', 'Got a flat tyre or breakdown on a rental motorbike in Da Lat? Stop safely, then call or message the shop. Simple steps, what to send us, and how to prevent problems.', "A flat tyre or a bike that won't start can happen to anyone, even on a well-maintained scooter. The good news: in Da Lat it is usually easy to sort out. Here's exactly what to do if you have a flat tyre or breakdown on a rental motorbike — calmly and safely.", 'van-phu-shop-early-pickup-air-blade', '\n<h2>Step 1: stop safely</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>If the bike feels wobbly, the steering pulls to one side or you hear a strange noise, slow down gently — no hard braking.</li>\n<li>Turn on your hazard lights or indicator and pull over to the right side of the road, away from bends and the middle of hill roads.</li>\n<li>Get off the bike and stand on the side, not in the traffic lane. On a slope, put the bike on its stand on flat ground if you can.</li>\n<li>Don\'t keep riding on a flat tyre — it can damage the tyre and the wheel, and it is unsafe on Da Lat\'s hills.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Step 2: call or message the shop</h2>\n<p>Contact us right away on WhatsApp, Zalo or Telegram, or call 0944 081 101. It helps a lot if you send:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Your location as a map pin (share it from Google Maps).</li>\n<li>A short photo or video of the problem — the tyre, a warning light, the noise.</li>\n<li>What happened just before: a pothole, rain, a long climb, the bike not starting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We\'ll tell you what to do next and help you sort it out. Please message us before getting any repair done, so we can agree together on the best solution.</p>\n<h2>Common problems and simple checks</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>The scooter won\'t start.</b> Check that the side stand is up and you are pressing a brake lever while pressing the start button — most automatic scooters need both. Also check the key position.</li>\n<li><b>Out of fuel.</b> Gas scooters leave the shop with 1 liter of fuel, which is enough to reach a petrol station (cây xăng) — fill up early. If the engine stops, push the bike to the side and message us.</li>\n<li><b>Flat tyre.</b> Don\'t ride on it; send us your pin. Small tyre shops are common along Vietnamese roads, but contact us first.</li>\n<li><b>Warning light or strange noise.</b> Stop, take a short video and send it to us.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:air-blade-handover-dalat-homestay}\n<h2>How to avoid problems in the first place</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>At pick-up we do a test ride and film the bike\'s condition with you — look at the tyres, brakes and lights together. Our <a href="{ART:lt-checklist}">checklist before renting</a> shows what we check.</li>\n<li>Avoid potholes and road edges, especially after rain.</li>\n<li>Ride the long passes on the Honda Air Blade 125cc and use engine braking downhill rather than holding the brakes all the way. See our <a href="{ART:riding}">riding tips for Vietnam</a>.</li>\n<li>Save our numbers in your phone before you leave, and keep your phone charged — the phone holder on every bike makes navigation easy.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Electric scooters</h2>\n<p>If an electric scooter loses power, first check the battery level on the display. Each scooter comes with 2 batteries, and you can swap a low one for a full one at our shop for 20,000 VND. If it still won\'t move, stop safely and message us.</p>\n<h2>At the end of the rental</h2>\n<p>If something happened during your trip, tell us honestly when you bring the bike back — we\'d rather know and help. Our guide to <a href="{ART:lt-return}">returning a rental motorbike</a> explains the return step by step.</p>\n<h2>Rent with a shop you can reach</h2>\n<p>We answer messages from 4:30 to 21:00 and help our guests when something goes wrong on the road. See all bikes, prices and contacts on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> — 150,000 VND per day, free helmet, no cash deposit.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-safety'] = ('electric-scooter-safety-tips-da-lat', 'Electric Scooter Safety Tips for Da Lat Traffic | Van Phu', 'Electric Scooter Safety Tips for Riding in Da Lat Traffic', 'Electric scooter safety tips for Da Lat: the quiet motor, signalling, right-hand traffic, roundabouts, hills, rain and night riding — practical advice before you rent.', "Electric scooters are easy to ride, but they behave a little differently from petrol scooters — the biggest difference is that people can't hear you coming. These electric scooter safety tips for Da Lat traffic will help you ride calmly and confidently from the first minute.", 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>1. The quiet motor: people don\'t hear you</h2>\n<p>An electric scooter is almost silent. Pedestrians, people stepping out of shops and other riders may not notice you, especially in busy streets around Da Lat Market, Xuan Huong Lake and the night market.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ride slowly near pedestrians and parked bikes, and be ready to stop.</li>\n<li>Use a short, friendly tap on the horn when someone might step out — it is normal and polite in Vietnam.</li>\n<li>Make eye contact at crossings; don\'t assume people have seen you.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>2. Smooth throttle, gentle start</h2>\n<p>Electric motors pull immediately. Turn the throttle gently when you start, especially uphill or with a passenger. Take a few slow laps at the shop during your test ride until starting and stopping feel natural. If you are new to scooters, read our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-beginners}">electric scooter for beginners</a> guide first.</p>\n<h2>3. Right-hand traffic and signalling</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Vietnam drives on the right. Keep to the right side of your lane; faster vehicles pass on your left.</li>\n<li>Signal early before every turn and switch the indicator off afterwards — many riders forget.</li>\n<li>Check your mirrors and look over your shoulder before changing lanes. Scooters often pass on both sides.</li>\n<li>At roundabouts and busy junctions, slow down, keep a steady line and avoid sudden moves; the traffic flows around you.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>4. Hills and braking</h2>\n<p>Da Lat is built on hills. Use both brakes gently and early on the way down, and keep a safe distance from the bike in front. Our guide to <a href="{ART:lt-ev-hills}">electric scooters on Da Lat\'s hilly streets</a> explains climbing and descending in detail. Long, steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road to Lang Biang are not for electric scooters — for those trips we recommend the Honda Air Blade 125cc.</p>\n<h2>5. Rain, fog and night</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Roads get slippery in the rain, especially painted lines and metal covers. Slow down and brake earlier.</li>\n<li>Turn on your lights in fog, rain and after dark, and wear something light-coloured if you can.</li>\n<li>Da Lat evenings are cool: a jacket and gloves keep your hands relaxed on the brakes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>6. Riders, helmets and battery</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Maximum 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg. Both always wear a helmet, fastened.</li>\n<li>Each scooter comes with 2 batteries; swap a low one for a full one at our shop for 20,000 VND. Plan your day so you don\'t run low far from the centre — see our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">battery guide</a>.</li>\n<li>Keep your phone in the phone holder for navigation and never use it while moving.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Before your first ride, please read our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent a safe, well-checked electric scooter</h2>\n<p>Every scooter gets a test ride and a condition video with you before you leave, plus a free helmet and no cash deposit. See our models on the <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> — 150,000 VND per day. Message us any time if you have questions about electric scooter safety in Da Lat.</p>\n')
