C["articles"]['danhia'] = ('dankia-lake-golden-stream-da-lat-motorbike', 'Dankia Lake & Golden Stream (Suoi Vang) by Motorbike from Da Lat | Van Phu', 'Dankia Lake and Golden Stream (Suoi Vang): a Half-Day Ride from Da Lat', 'How to ride to Dankia Lake and Golden Stream (Suoi Vang) from Da Lat: the route, which bike to take, best time of day, what to bring and how to combine it with Lang Biang.', "North-west of Da Lat, the city's water reservoirs lie hidden among pine hills: Dankia Lake and Golden Stream (Suoi Vang). Few tour buses go there, the road is quiet and the views are wide — a perfect half-day ride if you want to see the green countryside around Da Lat.", 'group-rental-air-blade-van-phu-morning', '\n<h2>What Dankia and Golden Stream are</h2>\n<p>Dankia Lake (hồ Đankia) and Golden Stream Lake (hồ Suối Vàng) are two connected reservoirs in Lac Duong district, about 15–20 km north-west of central Da Lat. They were created by dams and still supply water to the city, so the shores are mostly natural: grassy slopes, pine forests, small farms and the outline of Lang Biang mountain in the distance. People come here for the scenery, a picnic, photos and the feeling of open space — there is no theme park and no big tourist area.</p>\n<h2>The route by motorbike</h2>\n<p>From the centre, head north towards Lac Duong (the same direction as Lang Biang) and then follow Google Maps towards "Hồ Đankia" or "Hồ Suối Vàng". The ride takes about 35–50 minutes one way. The first part runs through villages, greenhouses and flower farms; later the road climbs gently through pine forest with views over the water. Some sections are narrow or patched, so ride slowly and watch for trucks and farm vehicles.</p>\n<p>A good loop for a half day: centre → Dankia and Golden Stream viewpoints → back via Lac Duong town, with an optional stop at the foot of Lang Biang. If you want to go up the mountain too, read our <a href="{ART:langbiang}">Lang Biang guide</a> first.</p>\n{PHOTO:couple-air-blade-dalat-center-street}\n<h2>Which bike to take</h2>\n<p>This is a longer countryside ride with climbs, so a gas scooter is the right choice. The Honda Air Blade 125cc is the most comfortable, especially with two people; the Honda Vision 110cc is fine for one rider. Electric scooters are better kept for the city — the round trip is long, there are hills and there is nowhere to swap a battery on the way. See our <a href="{ART:lt-airblade}">Air Blade rental guide</a> if you are choosing a bike.</p>\n<p>Gas scooters come with 1 liter of fuel, which is only enough to reach a petrol station — fill up in the city before you leave, because there are few stations near the lakes.</p>\n<h2>Best time to go</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Morning</b> (about 7–10 am) usually has the clearest views and the softest light on the water. Mist sometimes lies over the lakes early on.</li>\n<li><b>Late afternoon</b> is beautiful too, but come back before dark — the road has little lighting.</li>\n<li><b>Season:</b> the dry season (roughly December–April) is the easiest time to ride. In the rainy months, go in the morning, as showers usually come in the afternoon. In November and December the hills around Lac Duong can also have pink grass and wild sunflowers — see our <a href="{ART:pinkgrass}">pink grass guide</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Tips on the spot</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Park only where it is safe and does not block the road; many viewpoints are simply wide shoulders.</li>\n<li>Do not swim in the reservoirs and respect fenced areas — these are the city\'s water supply.</li>\n<li>Some shore paths cross private farmland. Stay on visible paths and ask before entering a field.</li>\n<li>Bring water, a snack, sunscreen and a light rain jacket. It is noticeably windier and cooler by the water.</li>\n<li>Take your rubbish back with you.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Combine it with other rides</h2>\n<p>Dankia fits well with Lang Biang and the Lac Duong area on the same day. For more ideas, see our <a href="{ROUTES}">Da Lat routes by motorbike</a> and our guide to <a href="{ART:passes}">scenic roads around Da Lat</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent your bike in Da Lat</h2>\n<p>Want to ride to Dankia Lake and Golden Stream yourself? At Văn Phú we rent the Honda Air Blade 125cc, Honda Vision 110cc and electric scooters for 150,000 VND per day, with a free helmet, a phone holder for navigation and no cash deposit. We open at 4:30 am, so an early start is easy. See the bikes on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-included'] = ('whats-included-motorbike-rental-da-lat', "What's Included in a Motorbike Rental in Da Lat? Helmet, Fuel & More | Van Phu", "What's Included in a Motorbike Rental in Da Lat", "What's included in a motorbike rental in Da Lat at Văn Phú: free helmet, phone holder, 1 liter of fuel, test ride, condition video and no cash deposit — and what you pay for yourself.", "When you compare rental shops, the daily price is only half the story — it matters just as much what's included in a motorbike rental in Da Lat. Here is exactly what you get with every bike at Văn Phú, and the few things you pay for yourself, so there are no surprises.", 'honda-air-blade-vision-fleet-van-phu', '\n<h2>The price: one fixed rate</h2>\n<p>Every bike — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter — costs 150,000 VND per day. The price is fixed and the same for everyone. A rental day runs from pick-up until 21:00. For the full price details, see our <a href="{ART:price}">rental price guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>What\'s included with every bike</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>A free helmet</b> — clean and adjusted to your head. If two people ride, ask for two helmets.</li>\n<li><b>A phone holder</b> — fitted on every bike, so you can follow Google Maps without holding your phone.</li>\n<li><b>1 liter of fuel</b> on gas scooters — enough to reach a petrol station and fill up.</li>\n<li><b>2 batteries</b> with every electric scooter.</li>\n<li><b>A test ride</b> before you leave, so you can get used to the bike in a quiet spot.</li>\n<li><b>A condition video</b> of the bike recorded together with you at pick-up — it protects both sides when you return it.</li>\n<li><b>No cash deposit</b> — you leave your passport (or ID card for Vietnamese guests) and get it back when you return the bike.</li>\n<li><b>Help by message</b> during your rental on WhatsApp, Zalo or Telegram if something goes wrong on the road.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-honda-vision-van-phu-shop}\n<h2>Hotel delivery</h2>\n<p>For rentals of 2 days or more, we deliver the bike to your hotel in central Da Lat for free. For a one-day rental, or if you stay outside the centre, message us and we will see what is possible. More details are in our <a href="{ART:lt-delivery}">hotel delivery guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>What you pay for yourself</h2>\n<p>A few everyday costs are not part of the rental — this is normal everywhere in Vietnam:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Fuel</b> beyond the first liter. Petrol stations (cây xăng) are easy to find in the city.</li>\n<li><b>Battery swaps</b> for electric scooters: swap a low battery for a full one at our shop for 20,000 VND.</li>\n<li><b>Parking</b> at markets, cafés and sights (gửi xe) — usually a small fee; keep the ticket.</li>\n<li><b>Entrance tickets</b> at attractions, which you pay at the gate.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-hotel-delivery-dalat}\n<h2>What you need to bring</h2>\n<p>Just your passport (or ID card for Vietnamese guests). Our guide on <a href="{ART:lt-documents}">what you need to rent a motorbike in Da Lat</a> explains it in detail. A light rain jacket and a warm layer are a good idea — Da Lat evenings are cool.</p>\n<h2>Check before you ride</h2>\n<p>At pick-up we go through the bike with you: brakes, lights, horn, tyres and mirrors. Our <a href="{ART:lt-checklist}">checklist before renting a motorbike</a> lists the ten things worth checking. Please ride without alcohol or beer and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</p>\n<h2>Book your bike</h2>\n<p>Now you know what\'s included in a motorbike rental in Da Lat at Văn Phú: one fixed price, helmet, phone holder, fuel to start, test ride, condition video and no cash deposit. See all bikes and contacts on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> and message us to book.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-flowers'] = ('flower-spots-da-lat-electric-scooter', 'Flower Spots in Da Lat by Electric Scooter: Gardens & Flower Villages | Van Phu', 'Flower Spots in Da Lat by Electric Scooter: Easy Rides from the Centre', 'Flower spots in Da Lat by electric scooter: the city flower garden, Van Thanh flower village, Domaine de Marie and Thai Phien — easy rides near the centre, parking and when to take an Air Blade instead.', 'Da Lat is called the city of flowers, and many of the best flower spots are close enough to reach on a quiet electric scooter. This guide shows the flower spots in Da Lat by electric scooter that are easy from the centre — and which farther farms are better on a gas scooter.', 'electric-scooters-van-phu-shop', '\n<h2>Why an electric scooter suits a flower ride</h2>\n<p>Flower spots near the centre are short hops apart, with lots of stopping, parking and walking. An electric scooter is light, quiet and easy to park, so it fits this kind of slow ride very well. Riding gently through the lanes of a flower village without engine noise is also simply nicer for you and for the farmers working there.</p>\n<h2>1. Da Lat Flower Garden (Vườn hoa thành phố)</h2>\n<p>The city flower garden sits on the northern shore of Xuan Huong Lake, only a few minutes from the centre. It is a landscaped park with flower beds, greenhouses and garden paths; there is an entrance ticket. Park in the motorbike parking at the entrance (gửi xe — keep your ticket). You can combine it with a slow ride around the lake — see our guide to <a href="{ART:lt-ev-lake}">Xuan Huong Lake by electric scooter</a>.</p>\n<h2>2. Van Thanh flower village</h2>\n<p>Van Thanh (làng hoa Vạn Thành) is about 2–3 km west of the centre and one of the oldest flower villages in Da Lat. Small lanes run between greenhouses of roses, chrysanthemums and gerberas. Ride slowly, keep to the lanes and ask with a smile before taking photos of people or entering a farm — these are working family farms, not a park.</p>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>3. Domaine de Marie and its gardens</h2>\n<p>Close to Van Thanh, the pink Domaine de Marie church has neat flower beds and garden paths around it, and it is an easy stop on the same ride. Our <a href="{ART:domainedemarie}">Domaine de Marie guide</a> has tips on visiting respectfully.</p>\n<h2>4. Thai Phien flower village</h2>\n<p>Thai Phien lies about 5–6 km north-east of the centre, on gentle slopes with flowers in greenhouses and open beds. The way there has some climbs. On an electric scooter, go there early in your day with a full battery, ride the slopes gently and come back the same way rather than continuing further out.</p>\n<h2>Farther farms: take a gas scooter</h2>\n<p>Hydrangea photo farms in the Trai Mat direction and beyond, the Cau Dat area and farms towards Lac Duong are longer rides with steeper roads. For those we recommend the Honda Air Blade 125cc. Long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road up Lang Biang are not for electric scooters. Our article on <a href="{ART:flowers}">flower villages and farms around Da Lat</a> covers the bigger loop.</p>\n<h2>Battery and practical tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Every electric scooter comes with 2 batteries, and you can swap a low one for a full one at our shop for 20,000 VND. Plan the ride around the centre — see our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">battery guide</a>.</li>\n<li>Morning is best: soft light, fewer people, and greenhouses are not yet hot.</li>\n<li>Maximum 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg; both wear a helmet.</li>\n<li>Lanes in flower villages can be narrow and wet — slow down and brake gently.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Before your first ride, please read our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter for your flower ride</h2>\n<p>Our electric scooters cost 150,000 VND per day with a free helmet, a phone holder for navigation and no cash deposit. See the models on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>, and message us to book your flower spots in Da Lat by electric scooter.</p>\n')
