C["articles"]['solo'] = ('da-lat-solo-travel-guide', 'Da Lat for Solo Travellers: Getting Around, Safety & Tips | Van Phu', 'Da Lat for Solo Travellers: A Practical Guide', 'Da Lat for solo travellers: where to stay, how to get around on your own, eating alone, meeting people, staying safe on a motorbike and a simple 2–3 day plan.', 'Da Lat is one of the easiest places in Vietnam to travel alone: it is compact, cool, relaxed and full of cafés where sitting by yourself feels completely normal. This guide to Da Lat for solo travellers covers the practical side — where to stay, getting around, food, safety and a simple plan.', 'white-honda-vision-sidewalk-dalat', '\n<h2>Why Da Lat works well on your own</h2>\n<p>The centre around Xuan Huong Lake and the market is walkable, the climate is mild, and the city has a calm, small-town rhythm. Many visitors are Vietnamese couples, families and groups of friends, but solo travellers — backpackers, digital nomads and people on a slow trip — are a common sight too, especially in the cafés and hostels in the centre.</p>\n<h2>Where to stay as a solo traveller</h2>\n<p>Staying in or near the centre makes everything easier: you can walk to the night market and the lake in the evening, find food late, and you are close to buses and rental shops. Hostels and small homestays with common rooms are the easiest places to meet other travellers. If you prefer quiet, a homestay on a hillside street is lovely, but check how far it is from the centre. Our guide on <a href="{ART:where-to-stay}">where to stay in Da Lat</a> compares the areas.</p>\n<h2>Getting around alone</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>On foot:</b> the lake, Da Lat Market, the cathedral and many cafés are within walking distance of each other — but the city is hilly, so wear good shoes.</li>\n<li><b>Grab and taxis:</b> useful at night, in heavy rain or for the trip from the bus station with luggage.</li>\n<li><b>Your own scooter:</b> the most freedom. Sights like Tuyen Lam Lake, the waterfalls, Cau Dat or the flower villages are spread out around the city, and a motorbike lets you go at your own pace, stop for photos and change plans whenever you like.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-cafe-garden-dalat}\n<h2>Eating alone</h2>\n<p>Eating alone is easy in Da Lat. Street-food stalls, noodle shops and the <a href="{ART:nightmarket}">night market</a> are perfect for one person: you point, sit on a small stool and eat. Try bánh căn, bánh tráng nướng, a bowl of noodles and hot soy milk in the evening. Our <a href="{ART:food}">Da Lat food guide</a> lists what to look for.</p>\n<h2>Meeting people</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Hostel common rooms and shared activities are the simplest way to find company for a day.</li>\n<li>Café culture is strong — many people work, read or chat there, and a shared table often starts a conversation.</li>\n<li>If you meet others who want to ride, you can rent bikes together and split up later; nobody needs to share one bike.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Staying safe on your own</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Share your plan and live location with a friend or with us before a longer ride.</li>\n<li>Ride the longer routes in daylight; Da Lat gets cold and misty after dark, and pass roads are not lit everywhere.</li>\n<li>Keep your phone charged — a power bank helps — and save your hotel and our shop as pins in Google Maps.</li>\n<li>Keep valuables in a zipped bag, use paid motorbike parking (gửi xe) and always take your key.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law. Read our <a href="{ART:riding}">riding tips</a> if you are new to Vietnamese traffic.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A simple 2–3 day solo plan</h2>\n<p>Day 1: walk the centre — lake, market, cathedral, a view café — and the night market in the evening. Day 2: ride a loop to Tuyen Lam Lake and the waterfalls, or follow our <a href="{ART:oneday}">one-day motorbike loop</a>. Day 3: get up early for sunrise and clouds — see <a href="{ART:cloudhunt}">cloud hunting around Da Lat</a> — then a slow afternoon in the flower villages. More ideas are on the <a href="{ROUTES}">routes page</a>.</p>\n<h2>Renting a scooter when you travel alone</h2>\n<p>At Văn Phú every bike costs 150,000 VND per day with a free helmet, a phone holder for navigation and no cash deposit — you leave your passport and get it back when you return the bike. We open at 4:30 am for sunrise rides, and if something goes wrong on the road you can message us on WhatsApp, Zalo or Telegram. A solo trip is a great way to see Da Lat at your own pace.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-navigation'] = ('navigating-da-lat-by-motorbike-google-maps', 'Navigating Da Lat by Motorbike with Google Maps: Tips | Van Phu', 'Navigating Da Lat by Motorbike with Google Maps', 'Navigating Da Lat by motorbike with Google Maps: using the phone holder, offline maps, saving pins, avoiding steep alleys, rain and phone battery, and what to do if you get lost.', 'Every rental bike at Văn Phú has a phone holder, so Google Maps becomes your guide around Da Lat. This short guide to navigating Da Lat by motorbike with Google Maps shows how to set it up before you ride, which routes to trust, and what to do if you get lost.', 'friends-air-blade-van-phu-gate', '\n<h2>Before you set off</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Mobile data:</b> a local mobile data package makes live navigation and messaging much easier. Hotel and café Wi-Fi is common, but not on the road.</li>\n<li><b>Offline map:</b> in Google Maps, download the Da Lat area as an offline map. Mountain roads outside the city can have weak signal, and an offline map keeps working.</li>\n<li><b>Save your pins:</b> save your hotel, our shop at 1/15/5 Thông Thiên Học and the places you plan to visit. We can also send you our location pin by WhatsApp, Zalo or Telegram.</li>\n<li><b>Phone battery:</b> navigation drains the battery fast, and cold mornings make it worse. Start fully charged and carry a power bank.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Using the phone holder safely</h2>\n<p>Put your phone in the holder before you start and check it is firm. Plan the route while you are parked, then just glance at the screen while riding. Do not hold or type on the phone while moving and do not wear earphones — if you need to change the route, pull over to the side of the road first. Strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law, and remember: no alcohol or beer when riding.</p>\n{PHOTO:honda-vision-bui-thi-xuan-street-dalat}\n<h2>Which route to trust</h2>\n<p>Google Maps usually picks the shortest way, and in Da Lat that sometimes means a very steep, narrow alley (hẻm) or a rough dirt road through farms. Some tips:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If your app shows a motorbike or two-wheeler option, use it; otherwise driving mode is fine.</li>\n<li>Prefer main roads, even if they are a little longer. If a lane suddenly gets very steep, narrow or muddy, turn back calmly.</li>\n<li>Road conditions change — a road can be closed for works or after heavy rain. If something looks closed, follow local signs, not the app.</li>\n<li>On an electric scooter, avoid long steep passes such as Prenn, Mimosa or the road up Lang Biang; for those, take the Honda Air Blade 125cc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our <a href="{ROUTES}">Da Lat routes page</a> shows tried and tested loops with approximate distances, so you can compare them with what the app suggests.</p>\n<h2>Rain and cold</h2>\n<p>Afternoon rain is common in the wet season. Keep a small plastic bag or waterproof pouch for your phone — most phones still work through thin plastic. In rain, stop under shelter to check the route instead of riding with wet hands on the screen.</p>\n<h2>Parking and finding your bike again</h2>\n<p>When you leave the bike at a paid parking spot (gửi xe), drop a pin or take a photo of the entrance and keep your parking ticket. Our article on <a href="{ART:parking-fuel}">parking and fuel in Da Lat</a> explains how gửi xe works.</p>\n<h2>If you get lost</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Stop somewhere safe, off the road, and open the map again.</li>\n<li>Locals are friendly: show the name of the place written in Vietnamese and point. Our page of <a href="{ART:phrases}">useful Vietnamese phrases</a> helps.</li>\n<li>Send us your live location on WhatsApp, Zalo or Telegram and we will help you find the way. If the bike has a problem, see <a href="{ART:lt-breakdown}">what to do in a breakdown</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Rent a bike with a phone holder</h2>\n<p>Every bike on our <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a> — Honda Vision 110cc, Honda Air Blade 125cc or electric scooter — costs 150,000 VND per day and comes with a free helmet and a phone holder, with no cash deposit. Message us before your trip and we will gladly send you our pin so navigating Da Lat by motorbike with Google Maps starts right at our door.</p>\n')
C["articles"]['lt-ev-luggage'] = ('carrying-bags-electric-scooter-da-lat', 'Carrying Bags on an Electric Scooter in Da Lat: What Fits | Van Phu', 'Carrying Bags on an Electric Scooter in Da Lat: What Fits and What Doesn’t', 'Carrying bags on an electric scooter in Da Lat: backpacks, shopping bags and market buys, where to put them, weight for two riders, rain and safety — and why suitcases go by taxi.', 'An electric scooter is perfect for short rides around the centre, but what about your bags? This guide to carrying bags on an electric scooter in Da Lat explains what you can comfortably take with you, where to put it, and when it is better to leave things at your hotel.', 'electric-scooters-helmets-van-phu', '\n<h2>What fits comfortably</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>A day backpack</b> with a jacket, rain gear, water and a camera — worn on your back or by the passenger.</li>\n<li><b>A small shoulder or waist bag</b> for phone, wallet and passport — keep it zipped and close to your body.</li>\n<li><b>Light shopping bags</b> from the market or a bakery, hung on the front hook or placed on the floorboard, as long as your feet still sit flat and the bag does not touch the handlebars.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Storage space differs by model, and the batteries need their place too. At pick-up we show you exactly where you can put things on your scooter.</p>\n<h2>What does not belong on an electric scooter</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Suitcases and large travel bags.</b> Take a Grab or taxi with luggage between the bus station and your hotel. For rentals of 2 days or more we deliver the scooter to your hotel in central Da Lat for free, so you never need to carry luggage on it.</li>\n<li><b>Anything long or wide</b> that sticks out to the sides or blocks your view.</li>\n<li><b>Heavy loads.</b> An electric scooter carries a maximum of 2 riders with a combined weight under 140 kg — heavy bags add weight and make the scooter harder to balance on Da Lat\'s hills.</li>\n</ul>\n{PHOTO:guest-white-electric-scooter-dalat}\n<h2>Riding with bags: safety tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep the load low and centred. A heavy bag on the front hook makes steering twitchy, especially when you turn slowly.</li>\n<li>Nothing should hang near the wheels, the brakes or your feet. Tie up loose straps.</li>\n<li>Brake earlier and gently on downhill streets — extra weight needs more distance to stop.</li>\n<li>The passenger should hold on with one hand, not carry big bags in both arms.</li>\n<li>No alcohol or beer when riding, and strictly follow Vietnamese traffic law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Shopping at the market</h2>\n<p>Da Lat Market is a favourite stop for strawberries, dried fruit, artichoke tea, coffee and flowers. Buy the heavy or fragile things last, just before you ride back, and ask the seller for a strong bag. Our guide to <a href="{ART:lt-ev-market}">Da Lat Market by electric scooter</a> explains where to park and the best time to go.</p>\n<h2>Rain and valuables</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Pack a rain cover or a plastic bag for your backpack — afternoon showers are common in the wet season.</li>\n<li>When you park at a paid parking spot (gửi xe), take your valuables with you and keep the ticket. See our tips on <a href="{ART:lt-ev-parking}">parking an electric scooter</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Battery</h2>\n<p>Every electric scooter comes with 2 batteries, and you can swap a low battery for a full one at our shop for 20,000 VND. Plan your shopping ride around the centre — our <a href="{ART:lt-ev-battery}">battery guide</a> explains how to plan your day. Please also read our <a href="{ART:evrules}">electric scooter rental rules</a> before your first ride.</p>\n<h2>Rent an electric scooter</h2>\n<p>Our electric scooters cost 150,000 VND per day with a free helmet, a phone holder and no cash deposit. See the models on our <a href="{EV}">electric scooter rental page</a> or all bikes on the <a href="{HOME}">Da Lat motorbike rental page</a>. Message us with any questions about carrying bags on an electric scooter in Da Lat — we are happy to help.</p>\n')
